07 :answer-field/correct "answer-ffef375f-eee1-5328-8ca8-8a877b28f600" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f38077d2-538a-52de-80fc-f81b787769d7" "answer-b564aa2d-1815-5b26-8895-65a367b53773" "answer-b32a8445-2ca4-5860-9a66-a3720ee75b12" "answer-44123af5-e2fd-5ea4-88e9-b70e9b3b267f" "answer-ffef375f-eee1-5328-8ca8-8a877b28f600"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f38077d2-538a-52de-80fc-f81b787769d7" :answer/id #uuid "f38077d2-538a-52de-80fc-f81b787769d7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{S}_{xy}=12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b564aa2d-1815-5b26-8895-65a367b53773" :answer/id #uuid "b564aa2d-1815-5b26-8895-65a367b53773" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{S}_{xy}=3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b32a8445-2ca4-5860-9a66-a3720ee75b12" :answer/id #uuid "b32a8445-2ca4-5860-9a66-a3720ee75b12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{S}_{xy}=41.9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44123af5-e2fd-5ea4-88e9-b70e9b3b267f" :answer/id #uuid "44123af5-e2fd-5ea4-88e9-b70e9b3b267f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{S}_{xy}=85.7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffef375f-eee1-5328-8ca8-8a877b28f600" :answer/id #uuid "ffef375f-eee1-5328-8ca8-8a877b28f600" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{S}_{xy}=9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-91b1cdc3-3a29-5654-81e4-e52f93ca5959" :question/id #uuid "91b1cdc3-3a29-5654-81e4-e52f93ca5959" :question/math-academy-id "q-251797" :question/problem "Consider two sets of $36$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{36},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{36}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\sum }_{i=1}^{36}{x}_{i}=202, \\\\  & {\\sum }_{i=1}^{36}{y}_{i}=225, \\\\  & {\\sum }_{i=1}^{36}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=1\\,329.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen,  ${S}_{xy}=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the statistic ${S}_{xy}$ be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}-\\frac{1}{n}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given statistics in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & =1\\,329-\\frac{1}{36}(202)(225) \\\\  & =1\\,329-1\\,262.5 \\\\  & =66.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3b264c3d-9a36-5510-bb44-273869121f99"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3b264c3d-9a36-5510-bb44-273869121f99" :answer-field/id #uuid "3b264c3d-9a36-5510-bb44-273869121f99" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a460ce37-c540-5545-a521-e22107bb905d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a460ce37-c540-5545-a521-e22107bb905d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a460ce37-c540-5545-a521-e22107bb905d" :answer/id #uuid "a460ce37-c540-5545-a521-e22107bb905d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "66.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ae961e11-0326-56d9-bf54-b78685d8bc1a" :question/id #uuid "ae961e11-0326-56d9-bf54-b78685d8bc1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-251795" :question/problem "Consider two sets of $16$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{16},$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{16}.$ You are given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\sum }_{i=1}^{16}{x}_{i}=168, \\\\  & {\\sum }_{i=1}^{16}{y}_{i}=158, \\\\  & {\\sum }_{i=1}^{16}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=1\\,742.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, ${S}_{xy}=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the statistic ${S}_{xy}$ be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}-\\frac{1}{n}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given statistics in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & =1\\,742-\\frac{1}{16}(168)(158) \\\\  & =1\\,742-1\\,659 \\\\  & =83.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-be6a6eec-8d66-5b27-9486-87b9b28cd48f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-be6a6eec-8d66-5b27-9486-87b9b28cd48f" :answer-field/id #uuid "be6a6eec-8d66-5b27-9486-87b9b28cd48f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-53c4e854-a95f-58d6-aaed-933846604762" :answer-field/choices ["answer-53c4e854-a95f-58d6-aaed-933846604762"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-53c4e854-a95f-58d6-aaed-933846604762" :answer/id #uuid "53c4e854-a95f-58d6-aaed-933846604762" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "83" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c92a6a30-c262-56d7-be68-e946a5d2130d" :question/id #uuid "c92a6a30-c262-56d7-be68-e946a5d2130d" :question/math-academy-id "q-251804" :question/problem "Consider the two sets of $8$ paired observations in the table below.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $8$ | $6$ | $10$ | $5$ | $1$ | $12$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $2$ | $4$ | $5$ | $7$ | $1$ | $6$ | $11$ | $6$ |\n\nGiven that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=260,$ calculate ${S}_{xy}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of products of deviations from the means can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}-\\frac{1}{n}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =8+6+10+5+1+12+4+3=49, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} & =2+4+5+7+1+6+11+6=42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=260.$\n\nFinally, by substituting in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n{S}_{xy}=260-\\frac{1}{8}(49)(42)=260-257.25=2.75.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4e4a57a7-76d3-509f-b185-ccce57c3cc55"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4e4a57a7-76d3-509f-b185-ccce57c3cc55" :answer-field/id #uuid "4e4a57a7-76d3-509f-b185-ccce57c3cc55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-68426059-edda-5ec7-b81c-b4828febf565" :answer-field/choices ["answer-826b8658-0dab-5fb2-a9bb-d4c31367b40c" "answer-68426059-edda-5ec7-b81c-b4828febf565" "answer-0368f1e8-5206-5cbe-b027-c81a9259238d" "answer-0359330d-c81f-5a2c-9bd7-99ff4a75ee26" "answer-b441c8c2-4523-544b-a9f9-830bb40032b7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-826b8658-0dab-5fb2-a9bb-d4c31367b40c" :answer/id #uuid "826b8658-0dab-5fb2-a9bb-d4c31367b40c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{S}_{xy}=1.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68426059-edda-5ec7-b81c-b4828febf565" :answer/id #uuid "68426059-edda-5ec7-b81c-b4828febf565" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{S}_{xy}=2.75" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0368f1e8-5206-5cbe-b027-c81a9259238d" :answer/id #uuid "0368f1e8-5206-5cbe-b027-c81a9259238d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{S}_{xy}=4.75" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0359330d-c81f-5a2c-9bd7-99ff4a75ee26" :answer/id #uuid "0359330d-c81f-5a2c-9bd7-99ff4a75ee26" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{S}_{xy}=5.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b441c8c2-4523-544b-a9f9-830bb40032b7" :answer/id #uuid "b441c8c2-4523-544b-a9f9-830bb40032b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{S}_{xy}=6.50" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8f3d0803-8d23-5ea9-98da-36f1c313a55f" :question/id #uuid "8f3d0803-8d23-5ea9-98da-36f1c313a55f" :question/math-academy-id "q-251803" :question/problem "Consider the two sets of $7$ paired observations in the table below.\n\n| **$\\,{x}_{i}\\,$** | $9$ | $11$ | $12$ | $4$ | $3$ | $2$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **$\\,{y}_{i}\\,$** | $3$ | $9$ | $27$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ | $1$ |\n\nGiven that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=494,$ calculate ${S}_{xy}.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a set of $n$ paired observations ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ and ${y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n},$ the sum of products of deviations from the means can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}-\\frac{1}{n}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the sum of the ${x}_{i}$ and ${y}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} & =9+11+12+4+3+2+8=49, \\\\ {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i} & =3+9+27+2+4+8+1=54\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given that ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{7}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}=494.$\n\nFinally, by substituting in the above formula, we get\n\n$$\n{S}_{xy}=494-\\frac{1}{7}(49)(54)=494-378=116.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0dfae525-f861-54d8-9ee3-bf782c835dce"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0dfae525-f861-54d8-9ee3-bf782c835dce" :answer-field/id #uuid "0dfae525-f861-54d8-9ee3-bf782c835dce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-441033b0-29be-53cb-a061-d8944e71cafe" :answer-field/choices ["answer-18636295-8de4-5069-bf45-99f931c91f77" "answer-55db0d56-f10f-55c9-b3b1-e5ac9217199a" "answer-441033b0-29be-53cb-a061-d8944e71cafe" "answer-213dd1f5-d9c2-5866-9c88-a69119799bed" "answer-34c23cc0-8a0e-5b53-8adb-9d28c46036b9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18636295-8de4-5069-bf45-99f931c91f77" :answer/id #uuid "18636295-8de4-5069-bf45-99f931c91f77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{S}_{xy}=104" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55db0d56-f10f-55c9-b3b1-e5ac9217199a" :answer/id #uuid "55db0d56-f10f-55c9-b3b1-e5ac9217199a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{S}_{xy}=112" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-441033b0-29be-53cb-a061-d8944e71cafe" :answer/id #uuid "441033b0-29be-53cb-a061-d8944e71cafe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{S}_{xy}=116" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-213dd1f5-d9c2-5866-9c88-a69119799bed" :answer/id #uuid "213dd1f5-d9c2-5866-9c88-a69119799bed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{S}_{xy}=81" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34c23cc0-8a0e-5b53-8adb-9d28c46036b9" :answer/id #uuid "34c23cc0-8a0e-5b53-8adb-9d28c46036b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{S}_{xy}=99" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205125 :tutorial/content "Given a data set $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n,$ we know that its variance, which we may denote as $\\sigma_x^2,$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\sigma_x^2 = \\dfrac1n \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline x)^2\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline x$ is the mean of $x.$ \n\nThe summation term\n\n$$\nS_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline x)^2\n$$\n\nis known as a **sum of squares** and is denoted $S_{xx}.$ As we'll see, this quantity often appears in statistical analysis, so giving it a separate notation is useful.\n\nSimilarly, if we have a data set $y_1, y_2, \\ldots, y_n,$ then we define\n\n$$\nS_{yy} =  \\sum_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\overline y)^2.\n$$\n\nNow, suppose we have the bivariate data set\n\n$$\n(x_1, y_1), \\qquad (x_2, y_2),\\qquad \\ldots, \\qquad (x_n, y_n).\n$$\n\nThen, the covariance is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) =  \\dfrac1n\\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline x)(y_i - \\overline y)\n$$\n\nand we may define $S_{xy}$ as\n\n$$\nS_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})(y_i - \\overline{y}).\n$$\n\nFinally, we can define the variance and covariance in terms of $S_{xx}, S_{yy},$ and $S_{xy}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{x}^{2} & =\\frac{{S}_{xx}}{n},\\,{\\sigma }_{y}^{2}=\\frac{{S}_{yy}}{n},\\,\\text{Cov}(x,y)=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205126 :tutorial/content "Calculating $S_{xx}, S_{yy},$ and $S_{xy}$ using their definitions can be cumbersome. Thankfully, we have the following shortcut formulas:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2}-\\frac{1}{n}{({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})}^{2} \\\\ {S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i}^{2}-\\frac{1}{n}{({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i})}^{2} \\\\ {S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}{y}_{i}-\\frac{1}{n}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i})({\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{y}_{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe'll prove these at the end of the lesson." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186205148 :tutorial/content "We wish to prove that\n\n![](images/6e/6e45f9e7a628f5275b3da6d56cbf02f7b36a058ed330f92176c50a3820a950d7.svg)\n\nWe start with the definition of ![](images/35/3596d7dd81777a4d3334085c1b707f68c8ade782b3a9b435ec815b5b9cd718c1.svg)\n\n![](images/47/4729fc0ed523938607245027c4e2354b97d5c70fc12faba653f826123ce7e116.svg)\n\nFirst, we expand the binomial:\n\n![](images/7f/7fbf3c03450c32af2eac42db8a67fa185cb7a88a48b9f55a22b21b8f15bbc8d0.svg)\n\nDistributing the summation, we have\n\n![](images/07/079466ea370445b1274143031c8ddf28d09569757c05a439ff967d6c5e835dc5.svg)\n\nNow, since ![](images/41/413a174ed8eab4231f8960e47693592ddfebbf73fbad3284ccd11c5233076316.svg) and ![](images/ed/ed33df1256b85619e6408ee6014ce30223f75c5df8ca599ec4f4c99fe0f293ac.svg) are constants, we can write this as\n\n![](images/8a/8a12508a4a5e1890cac8b9814966b826dd67c7c89977e2fcb223b859ee016464.svg)\n\nNext, we use the following results:\n\n![](images/de/de94aad0fc385eebb157d4a4fec1f763ef02190462eacf53f27de4769f3ae42d.svg)\n\nThis gives\n\n![](images/98/98de282215c98d829d9d7984442773a2086ca640b29542382a060826270489ae.svg)\nas required.\n\nThe proofs for ![](images/40/409afd81c4ea93a33782db88296b0a9f7e9e982a14a16274e2ec50739d363f14.svg) and ![](images/02/02f6f964f68603338fc2cbe3b6d3be2b6af947670bec73bc8810b14208e1090e.svg) are similar." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186204149 :activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186185634 :answer-field/choices 17592186185635] [:db/retract 17592186185634 :answer-field/choices 17592186185636] [:db/retract 17592186185634 :answer-field/choices 17592186185637] [:db/retract 17592186185634 :answer-field/choices 17592186185638] [:db/retract 17592186185634 :answer-field/choices 17592186185639] {:db/id 17592186188278 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c6ec9582-211a-54f7-b78a-6175feef090c" "question-989181af-177e-5503-a508-c854844640ac" "question-141fc269-b2bd-5ee7-86fd-0b448fecc7f6"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c6ec9582-211a-54f7-b78a-6175feef090c" :question/id #uuid "c6ec9582-211a-54f7-b78a-6175feef090c" :question/math-academy-id "q-110256" :question/problem "A seller delivers bricks on pallets. One pallet contains $500$ bricks. The weight of a brick is normally distributed with a mean of $3\\,\\text{lb}$ and a standard deviation of $0.25\\,\\text{lb},$ while the weight of a pallet is normally distributed with a mean of $40\\,\\text{lb}$ and a standard deviation of $4\\,\\text{lb}.$ If one pallet with bricks is randomly selected, what is the probability that it weighs less than $1\\,545\\,\\text{lb}?$ You may assume that the weights are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ | $\\,0.7357\\,$ | $\\,0.7389\\,$ | $\\,0.7422\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ | $\\,0.7673\\,$ | $\\,0.7704\\,$ | $\\,0.7734\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ | $\\,0.7967\\,$ | $\\,0.7995\\,$ | $\\,0.8023\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the weight of the $i$th brick, and let $Y$ denote the weight of a pallet. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(3,{0.25}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 500,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nY∼N(40,{4}^{2}).\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denote the weight of a randomly selected pallet with bricks. Then, since\n\n$$\nW={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{500}+Y,\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =500\\cdot 3+40 \\\\  & =1\\,540, \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =500\\cdot {0.25}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\  & =47.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW∼N(1\\,540,47.25).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(W<1\\,545),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W<1\\,545) & =P(Z<\\frac{1\\,545-1\\,540}{\\sqrt{47.25}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<0.73) \\\\  & =Φ(0.73)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.73)≈0.7673.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W<1\\,545) & ≈Φ(0.73) \\\\  & ≈0.7673.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-160c9e56-fbfc-5c34-9ac0-b74ec90271f9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-160c9e56-fbfc-5c34-9ac0-b74ec90271f9" :answer-field/id #uuid "160c9e56-fbfc-5c34-9ac0-b74ec90271f9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0ec85ad5-7ebb-57fe-b54b-9f5f21beff93" :answer-field/choices ["answer-10502504-65e5-552d-8583-e710377ff51e" "answer-6b9307e2-adf7-5f81-ab85-5654801c0e6b" "answer-0ec85ad5-7ebb-57fe-b54b-9f5f21beff93" "answer-f1b89101-b002-5379-99aa-15ff76a8e86e" "answer-be94cbff-4f1f-52e7-8aca-b344e58fdc42"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-10502504-65e5-552d-8583-e710377ff51e" :answer/id #uuid "10502504-65e5-552d-8583-e710377ff51e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2266" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6b9307e2-adf7-5f81-ab85-5654801c0e6b" :answer/id #uuid "6b9307e2-adf7-5f81-ab85-5654801c0e6b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2327" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ec85ad5-7ebb-57fe-b54b-9f5f21beff93" :answer/id #uuid "0ec85ad5-7ebb-57fe-b54b-9f5f21beff93" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7673" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f1b89101-b002-5379-99aa-15ff76a8e86e" :answer/id #uuid "f1b89101-b002-5379-99aa-15ff76a8e86e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7734" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be94cbff-4f1f-52e7-8aca-b344e58fdc42" :answer/id #uuid "be94cbff-4f1f-52e7-8aca-b344e58fdc42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7995" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188559 :question/problem "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{20}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(1,{2}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 20,\n$$\n\nwhat is the distribution of $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{20}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{20}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{20}$ are mutually independent, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{20} & ∼N(20\\cdot 1,\\,20\\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(20\\cdot 1,\\,20\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =N(20,80).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186185634 :answer-field/correct "answer-afdde64d-2ea7-5d46-8675-d296e7768222" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bf303566-e2e4-5512-bed7-eae7c312cb55" "answer-cd7cabdf-c24e-5682-a89a-fff839a0da01" "answer-2abc9490-9339-5c9e-986a-5e945e4cb8ca" "answer-6a8a87c2-8fa4-5c79-9bbb-69e4fccb6e51" "answer-afdde64d-2ea7-5d46-8675-d296e7768222"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf303566-e2e4-5512-bed7-eae7c312cb55" :answer/id #uuid "bf303566-e2e4-5512-bed7-eae7c312cb55" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(1,1600)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd7cabdf-c24e-5682-a89a-fff839a0da01" :answer/id #uuid "cd7cabdf-c24e-5682-a89a-fff839a0da01" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(1,80)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2abc9490-9339-5c9e-986a-5e945e4cb8ca" :answer/id #uuid "2abc9490-9339-5c9e-986a-5e945e4cb8ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(20,1600)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a8a87c2-8fa4-5c79-9bbb-69e4fccb6e51" :answer/id #uuid "6a8a87c2-8fa4-5c79-9bbb-69e4fccb6e51" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(20,400)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-afdde64d-2ea7-5d46-8675-d296e7768222" :answer/id #uuid "afdde64d-2ea7-5d46-8675-d296e7768222" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "Y∼N(20,80)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188560 :question/problem "Given that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{20}$ are I.I.D random variables, where\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(1,{2}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 20,\n$$\n\ncalculate $P(Y\\le 21),$ where  $Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{20}.$\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.1\\,$ | $\\,0.5398\\,$ | $\\,0.5438\\,$ | $\\,0.5478\\,$ | $\\,0.5517\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.2\\,$ | $\\,0.5793\\,$ | $\\,0.5832\\,$ | $\\,0.5871\\,$ | $\\,0.5910\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.3\\,$ | $\\,0.6179\\,$ | $\\,0.6217\\,$ | $\\,0.6255\\,$ | $\\,0.6293\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{20}$ are independent and identically distributed (I.I.D) normal random variables, where \n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le n,\n$$\n\nthen \n\n$$\nY={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}∼N(n\\mu ,\\,n{\\sigma }^{2}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{20}$ are independent and identically distributed, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Y={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{20} & ∼N(20\\cdot 1,\\,20\\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & ∼N(20\\cdot 1,\\,20\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =N(20,80).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe wish to compute the probability $P(Y\\le 21).$ We first convert $Y$ into a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\le 21) & =P(Z\\le \\frac{21-20}{\\sqrt{80}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{80}}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.11) \\\\  & =Φ(0.11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.11)=0.5438.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Y\\le 21)=Φ(0.11)=0.5438.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186185640 :answer-field/correct 17592186185645 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-989181af-177e-5503-a508-c854844640ac" :question/id #uuid "989181af-177e-5503-a508-c854844640ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-152019" :question/problem "In a particular shop, cables are sold in coils. The length of the cable in a coil is normally distributed with a mean of $50\\,\\text{m}$ and a standard deviation of $0.7\\,\\text{m}.$ If John bought $5$ coils, what is the probability that the total length of the cables he bought is greater than $250\\,\\text{m}$ and less than $253\\,m?$ You may assume that the lengths of the cable in the coils are mutually independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.9554\\,$ | $\\,0.9564\\,$ | $\\,0.9573\\,$ | $\\,0.9582\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.9641\\,$ | $\\,0.9649\\,$ | $\\,0.9656\\,$ | $\\,0.9664\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.9713\\,$ | $\\,0.9719\\,$ | $\\,0.9726\\,$ | $\\,0.9732\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the length of cable in the $i$th coil. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(50,{0.7}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 5.\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $L$ denote the total length of the cables in $5$ randomly selected coils. Then, since\n\n$$\nL={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{5},\n$$\n\nwe have that $L$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[L] & =5\\cdot 50 \\\\  & =250, \\\\ \\text{Var}[L] & =5\\cdot {0.7}^{2} \\\\  & =2.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nL∼N(250,2.45).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(250<L<253),$ we convert $L$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(250<L<253) & =P(\\frac{250-250}{\\sqrt{2.45}}<Z<\\frac{253-250}{\\sqrt{2.45}}) \\\\  & ≈P(0<Z<1.92) \\\\  & =Φ(1.92)-Φ(0) \\\\  & =Φ(1.92)-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.92)≈0.9726.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(250<L<253) & ≈Φ(0.95)-0.5 \\\\  & ≈0.4726.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-31e87839-a244-5651-ab20-8782a2fd6c04"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-31e87839-a244-5651-ab20-8782a2fd6c04" :answer-field/id #uuid "31e87839-a244-5651-ab20-8782a2fd6c04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-79702301-2460-524d-aca3-8152b4171769" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9756be1e-a09c-5697-a99e-d25af2662f83" "answer-25630579-f260-5b6b-b01f-0c206dde9b22" "answer-17949f3a-e1f1-5fa7-8148-b9750780d3b2" "answer-a48296a2-de5e-5ceb-9972-e4e36140f4b8" "answer-79702301-2460-524d-aca3-8152b4171769"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9756be1e-a09c-5697-a99e-d25af2662f83" :answer/id #uuid "9756be1e-a09c-5697-a99e-d25af2662f83" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4554" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-25630579-f260-5b6b-b01f-0c206dde9b22" :answer/id #uuid "25630579-f260-5b6b-b01f-0c206dde9b22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4564" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17949f3a-e1f1-5fa7-8148-b9750780d3b2" :answer/id #uuid "17949f3a-e1f1-5fa7-8148-b9750780d3b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4656" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a48296a2-de5e-5ceb-9972-e4e36140f4b8" :answer/id #uuid "a48296a2-de5e-5ceb-9972-e4e36140f4b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4719" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79702301-2460-524d-aca3-8152b4171769" :answer/id #uuid "79702301-2460-524d-aca3-8152b4171769" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4726" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-141fc269-b2bd-5ee7-86fd-0b448fecc7f6" :question/id #uuid "141fc269-b2bd-5ee7-86fd-0b448fecc7f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-152022" :question/problem "Fred picked $10$ watermelons from his vegetable garden. The weight of watermelons he grows is normally distributed with a mean of $11\\,\\text{kg}$ and a standard deviation of $3\\,\\text{kg}.$ What is the probability that the total weight of the watermelons Fred picked is greater than $100\\,\\text{kg}$ and less than $110\\,\\text{kg}?$ You may assume that the weights of the watermelons are mutually independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.8461\\,$ | $\\,0.8485\\,$ | $\\,0.8508\\,$ | $\\,0.8531\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8686\\,$ | $\\,0.8708\\,$ | $\\,0.8729\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8888\\,$ | $\\,0.8907\\,$ | $\\,0.8925\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let ${X}_{i}$ denote the weight of the $i$th watermelon. Then, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}∼N(11,{3}^{2}),\\,1\\le i\\le 10.\n$$\n\nLet the random variable $W$ denotes the total weight of the $10$ watermelons. Then, since\n\n$$\nW={X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{10},\n$$\n\nwe have that $W$ is normally distributed, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[W] & =10\\cdot 11 \\\\  & =110, \\\\ \\text{Var}[W] & =10\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\  & =90.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW∼N(110,90).\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(100<W<110),$ we convert $W$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(100<W<110) & =P(\\frac{100-110}{\\sqrt{90}}<Z<\\frac{110-110}{\\sqrt{90}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.05<Z<0) \\\\  & =Φ(0)-Φ(-1.05) \\\\  & =Φ(0)-(1-Φ(1.05)) \\\\  & =0.5-(1-Φ(1.05)) \\\\  & =Φ(1.05)-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\nΦ(1.05)≈0.8531.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(100<W<110) & ≈Φ(1.05)-0.5 \\\\  & ≈0.3531.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-65f1e10a-d8c8-58e5-b10d-6e1e77a5ab90"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-65f1e10a-d8c8-58e5-b10d-6e1e77a5ab90" :answer-field/id #uuid "65f1e10a-d8c8-58e5-b10d-6e1e77a5ab90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-39b93a6a-bc02-50a9-a873-0a2689de3fe8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7b822a13-ca96-5490-99e8-f8267cac2050" "answer-57d360fe-135c-5e2f-b5ac-5b495990fb44" "answer-39b93a6a-bc02-50a9-a873-0a2689de3fe8" "answer-e8ef5f62-ade0-52c3-b720-4570930c7d3d" "answer-013c0744-9228-52be-a7b5-6457edce0d29"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b822a13-ca96-5490-99e8-f8267cac2050" :answer/id #uuid "7b822a13-ca96-5490-99e8-f8267cac2050" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3485" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-57d360fe-135c-5e2f-b5ac-5b495990fb44" :answer/id #uuid "57d360fe-135c-5e2f-b5ac-5b495990fb44" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3508" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39b93a6a-bc02-50a9-a873-0a2689de3fe8" :answer/id #uuid "39b93a6a-bc02-50a9-a873-0a2689de3fe8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3531" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e8ef5f62-ade0-52c3-b720-4570930c7d3d" :answer/id #uuid "e8ef5f62-ade0-52c3-b720-4570930c7d3d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3708" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-013c0744-9228-52be-a7b5-6457edce0d29" :answer/id #uuid "013c0744-9228-52be-a7b5-6457edce0d29" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3925" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-27d0ee72-208c-5c09-ba82-7aa73065e9b0" :activity/id #uuid "27d0ee72-208c-5c09-ba82-7aa73065e9b0" :activity/title "I.I.D Normal Random Variables" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048382 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-502f1960-fa25-50be-8c53-84accf0f2fb6" "step-b77ef207-4e3c-52e8-8d17-85be88f14843" "step-04354192-e8c2-529d-a53b-555b1aaafd7d" "step-2b2730d2-89fd-53dd-b1f9-3ac5795fc863" "step-56dfdf28-b75e-51a6-9103-e7c4a4c58570"] :activity/first-step "step-502f1960-fa25-50be-8c53-84accf0f2fb6" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-502f1960-fa25-50be-8c53-84accf0f2fb6" :step/id #uuid "502f1960-fa25-50be-8c53-84accf0f2fb6" :step/content "question-c6ec9582-211a-54f7-b78a-6175feef090c" :step/next "step-b77ef207-4e3c-52e8-8d17-85be88f14843" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b77ef207-4e3c-52e8-8d17-85be88f14843" :step/id #uuid "b77ef207-4e3c-52e8-8d17-85be88f14843" :step/content 17592186188559 :step/next "step-04354192-e8c2-529d-a53b-555b1aaafd7d" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-04354192-e8c2-529d-a53b-555b1aaafd7d" :step/id #uuid "04354192-e8c2-529d-a53b-555b1aaafd7d" :step/content 17592186188560 :step/next "step-2b2730d2-89fd-53dd-b1f9-3ac5795fc863" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2b2730d2-89fd-53dd-b1f9-3ac5795fc863" :step/id #uuid "2b2730d2-89fd-53dd-b1f9-3ac5795fc863" :step/content "question-989181af-177e-5503-a508-c854844640ac" :step/next "step-56dfdf28-b75e-51a6-9103-e7c4a4c58570" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-56dfdf28-b75e-51a6-9103-e7c4a4c58570" :step/id #uuid "56dfdf28-b75e-51a6-9103-e7c4a4c58570" :step/content "question-141fc269-b2bd-5ee7-86fd-0b448fecc7f6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186229683 :question/answer-fields 17592186228289] [:db/retract 17592186229683 :question/answer-fields 17592186228294] [:db/retract 17592186229683 :question/answer-fields 17592186228279] [:db/retract 17592186229683 :question/answer-fields 17592186228284] {:db/id 17592186229132 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-dcde9f99-5a2a-52a5-a122-c3ef3631c507" "question-d29ce693-46aa-5cb5-a64c-988886b4e7a0" "question-8c12baec-9a09-54f7-8159-4ab5ea602070"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-dcde9f99-5a2a-52a5-a122-c3ef3631c507" :question/id #uuid "dcde9f99-5a2a-52a5-a122-c3ef3631c507" :question/math-academy-id "q-335070" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-y(t)+1,\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">$1-{e}^{-t}-t{e}^{-t}$ </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-t} & t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{-(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{-(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{-(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}(t-s){e}^{s-t} \\\\ {e}^{s-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}(t-s){e}^{s-t} \\\\ {e}^{s-t}\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}(-s+t+1){e}^{s-t} \\\\ {e}^{s-t}\\end{aligned}]}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-(t+1){e}^{-t} \\\\ 1-{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1-(t+1){e}^{-t} \\\\ 1-{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1-{e}^{-t}-t{e}^{-t} \\\\ 1-{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7d8ad94d-1b6b-50f4-917b-e60fcda59e4c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7d8ad94d-1b6b-50f4-917b-e60fcda59e4c" :answer-field/id #uuid "7d8ad94d-1b6b-50f4-917b-e60fcda59e4c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2ce80ed6-c2fb-573a-bcb7-17c24ee2cd02" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2ce80ed6-c2fb-573a-bcb7-17c24ee2cd02"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2ce80ed6-c2fb-573a-bcb7-17c24ee2cd02" :answer/id #uuid "2ce80ed6-c2fb-573a-bcb7-17c24ee2cd02" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-e^{-t}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d29ce693-46aa-5cb5-a64c-988886b4e7a0" :question/id #uuid "d29ce693-46aa-5cb5-a64c-988886b4e7a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-335072" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t)+y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} & t{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{2(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{2t}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{2t}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6115237a-ca95-5556-91b1-d22bf91c2c37"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6115237a-ca95-5556-91b1-d22bf91c2c37" :answer-field/id #uuid "6115237a-ca95-5556-91b1-d22bf91c2c37" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a13ebe21-3fd3-5659-96f0-dac3aebbcdb5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a13ebe21-3fd3-5659-96f0-dac3aebbcdb5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a13ebe21-3fd3-5659-96f0-dac3aebbcdb5" :answer/id #uuid "a13ebe21-3fd3-5659-96f0-dac3aebbcdb5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-e^{2t}-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8c12baec-9a09-54f7-8159-4ab5ea602070" :question/id #uuid "8c12baec-9a09-54f7-8159-4ab5ea602070" :question/math-academy-id "q-335084" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=4x(t)+y(t)+2 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=4y(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"120px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)={{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+{{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{⏟}}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}x(0) & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & t{e}^{4t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-s)}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4(t-s)} & (t-s){e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4(t-s)}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4(t-s)} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4t}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4t}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{4t}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-083c749e-9d27-588e-b8bf-03bf5d6d7291"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-083c749e-9d27-588e-b8bf-03bf5d6d7291" :answer-field/id #uuid "083c749e-9d27-588e-b8bf-03bf5d6d7291" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4b374613-ddcc-5c32-8993-19ba069fb8ec" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4b374613-ddcc-5c32-8993-19ba069fb8ec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b374613-ddcc-5c32-8993-19ba069fb8ec" :answer/id #uuid "4b374613-ddcc-5c32-8993-19ba069fb8ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e^{4t}-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-7fb4b445-0fd0-5c09-8ee7-7faef43b5ab2" :activity/id #uuid "7fb4b445-0fd0-5c09-8ee7-7faef43b5ab2" :activity/title "Solving Inhomogeneous Systems of ODEs Using Matrix Methods" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050707 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-cef7ab47-7ef6-5ce5-94ff-fd249257c533" "step-4a313e99-6bb3-5fac-9942-cc34685e8d8e" "step-a448eb6f-7419-52b7-bf05-0c980f27a9c2" "step-8fdaf50f-41f4-5f0b-b992-4f806b6b6092" "step-b160506a-1712-5199-a906-043763a0bcdb"] :activity/first-step "step-cef7ab47-7ef6-5ce5-94ff-fd249257c533" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-cef7ab47-7ef6-5ce5-94ff-fd249257c533" :step/id #uuid "cef7ab47-7ef6-5ce5-94ff-fd249257c533" :step/content "question-dcde9f99-5a2a-52a5-a122-c3ef3631c507" :step/next "step-4a313e99-6bb3-5fac-9942-cc34685e8d8e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-4a313e99-6bb3-5fac-9942-cc34685e8d8e" :step/id #uuid "4a313e99-6bb3-5fac-9942-cc34685e8d8e" :step/content "question-d29ce693-46aa-5cb5-a64c-988886b4e7a0" :step/next "step-a448eb6f-7419-52b7-bf05-0c980f27a9c2" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-a448eb6f-7419-52b7-bf05-0c980f27a9c2" :step/id #uuid "a448eb6f-7419-52b7-bf05-0c980f27a9c2" :step/content "question-8c12baec-9a09-54f7-8159-4ab5ea602070" :step/next "step-8fdaf50f-41f4-5f0b-b992-4f806b6b6092" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8fdaf50f-41f4-5f0b-b992-4f806b6b6092" :step/id #uuid "8fdaf50f-41f4-5f0b-b992-4f806b6b6092" :step/content 17592186229683 :step/next "step-b160506a-1712-5199-a906-043763a0bcdb" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b160506a-1712-5199-a906-043763a0bcdb" :step/id #uuid "b160506a-1712-5199-a906-043763a0bcdb" :step/content 17592186396755 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186362342 :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the rectangular parallelepiped's length, width, and height, respectively. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=4x+4y+4z,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\nxyz=125.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=125\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨4,4,4⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4=\\lambda yz \\\\ 4=\\lambda xz \\\\ 4=\\lambda xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x=\\lambda xyz \\\\ 4y=\\lambda xyz \\\\ 4z=\\lambda xyz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives $x=y=z.$\n\nNow, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xyz & =125 \\\\ x(x)(x) & =125 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =125 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(5,5,5),$ and the minimum value is \n\n$$\nf(5,5,5)=4(5)+4(5)+4(5)=60\\,\\text{in}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(25,5,1) & =4(25)+4(5)+4(1) \\\\  & =121 \\\\  & >f(5,5,5).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186364648 :question/worked-solution "Let $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ be the tank's length, width, and height, respectively. We wish to minimize the function\n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=xy+2xz+2yz,\n$$\n\nsubject to the constraint \n\n$$\nxyz=256.\n$$\n\nNote that we also have the constraints $x>0,y>0,z>0.$\n\nLet $g(x,y,z)=xyz.$ Therefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y,z)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z),\\,g(x,y,z)=256\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,z) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y,z),{f}_{y}(x,y,z),{f}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y,z),{g}_{y}(x,y,z),{g}_{z}(x,y,z)⟩ \\\\ ⟨y+2z,x+2z,2x+2y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y+2z=\\lambda yz \\\\ x+2z=\\lambda xz \\\\ 2x+2y=\\lambda xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first, second, and third equations by $x,y,$ and $z$ respectively, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x(y+2z)=\\lambda xyz \\\\ y(x+2z)=\\lambda xyz \\\\ z(2x+2y)=\\lambda xyz\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first and second equations above, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(y+2z) & =y(x+2z) \\\\ xy+2xz & =xy+2yz \\\\ 2xz & =2yz \\\\ 2z(x-y) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $z>0,$ we can conclude that $x=y.$\n\nSimilarly, from the second and third equations, we get that $x=y=2z.$ So, using the constraint equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}xyz & =256 \\\\ (2z)(2z)z & =256 \\\\ 4{z}^{3} & =256 \\\\ {z}^{3} & =64 \\\\ z & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is reached at the point $(8,8,4),$ and the minimum value is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(8,8,4)=8(8)+2(8)(4)+2(8)(4)=192\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** It's easy to show that the obtained point is indeed the minimum (and not the maximum). To do that, we could compare the value of the function at this point with the value of the function at any other (arbitrary) point belonging to the domain. For example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(16,16,1) & =(16)(16)+2(16)(1)+2(16)(1) \\\\  & =320 \\\\  & >f(8,8,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186362346 :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the critical values of the function in the region's interior. We compute the critical points by solving $∇f=0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =⟨0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the above equation has no solutions, we conclude that the function does not have critical values in the region's interior.\n\nNext, we need to find the minimum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y)\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,2⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we cannot have $\\lambda =0$ since this leads to a contradiction in the above equation. Therefore, assuming $\\lambda \\ne 0,$ we have the following\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-1=4\\lambda x \\\\ 2=2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\frac{1}{4\\lambda } \\\\ y=\\frac{1}{\\lambda }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,x=-\\frac{1}{4}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n  Substituting $x=-\\frac{1}{4}y$ into the condition $g(x,y)=4,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{8}+{y}^{2} & =4 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{32}{9} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{1}{4}y=∓\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}),\\,(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3}).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ | $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $5+3\\sqrt{2}$ | $5-3\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the function reaches a minimum value at $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3},-\\frac{4\\sqrt{2}}{3})\\,,$ and its minimum value is $5-3\\sqrt{2}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186362348 :question/worked-solution "We are given that the critical point of $f(x,y)$ in the region's interior is $(0,0).$\n\nNow, we need to find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=4\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y,) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨8x,20y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨2x,2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}8x & =2\\lambda x \\\\ 20y & =2\\lambda y\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(4-\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(10-\\lambda )=0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,y=\\pm 2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,\\pm 2).$\n\n- Assuming $y=0$ in the second equation of the system, the constraint condition $g(x,y)=4$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4\\,⇒\\,x=\\pm 2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(\\pm 2,0).$\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,\\pm 2),\\,(\\pm 2,0),\\,(0,0).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at each of these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,\\pm 2)$ | $(\\pm 2,0)$ | $(0,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,f(x,y)\\,$ | $40$ | $16$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at $(0,2)$ and $(0,-2),$ and its maximum value is $40.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-96f786d0-7a4e-5202-b49a-8bf736b34184" :activity/id #uuid "96f786d0-7a4e-5202-b49a-8bf736b34184" :activity/title "Optimizing Multivariable Functions Using Lagrange Multipliers" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/sc