re [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-9e1f3daa-4674-5726-9801-55cbf66f4728" :step/id #uuid "9e1f3daa-4674-5726-9801-55cbf66f4728" :step/content 17592186125047 :step/next "step-94c832ad-7f6f-569d-b0a4-050bc58efcae" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-94c832ad-7f6f-569d-b0a4-050bc58efcae" :step/id #uuid "94c832ad-7f6f-569d-b0a4-050bc58efcae" :step/content 17592186392121 :step/next "step-e9899580-b410-524f-9c40-c83ad8446596" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-e9899580-b410-524f-9c40-c83ad8446596" :step/id #uuid "e9899580-b410-524f-9c40-c83ad8446596" :step/content 17592186351262 :step/next "step-fa855198-452b-5674-819a-16ae32b90878" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-fa855198-452b-5674-819a-16ae32b90878" :step/id #uuid "fa855198-452b-5674-819a-16ae32b90878" :step/content 17592186354490 :step/next "step-dd8b30df-d376-5fc5-94a5-8a3802e54cb9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-dd8b30df-d376-5fc5-94a5-8a3802e54cb9" :step/id #uuid "dd8b30df-d376-5fc5-94a5-8a3802e54cb9" :step/content 17592186354491 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186371981 :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F=⟨z,\\,0,\\,x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z=9-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ and the plane $z=1.$ Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∬}_{D}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}}\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-fbb16356-5d9b-531b-bc04-17b88f44ef79" :activity/id #uuid "fbb16356-5d9b-531b-bc04-17b88f44ef79" :activity/title "Calculating Flux Through Closed Surfaces" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049184 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-406ef78e-6b81-5dfd-92d8-7cecdbdc2a9a" "step-3a200200-f9a3-5d67-b477-751312a3c4c2" "step-24a4d735-bf7c-573f-9ba5-80873e983f63" "step-d897879d-381d-5c1c-a360-cf41d1b97535" "step-2ec90c17-0169-5fa1-a78b-19f41aa17a65"] :activity/first-step "step-406ef78e-6b81-5dfd-92d8-7cecdbdc2a9a" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-406ef78e-6b81-5dfd-92d8-7cecdbdc2a9a" :step/id #uuid "406ef78e-6b81-5dfd-92d8-7cecdbdc2a9a" :step/content 17592186405069 :step/next "step-3a200200-f9a3-5d67-b477-751312a3c4c2" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3a200200-f9a3-5d67-b477-751312a3c4c2" :step/id #uuid "3a200200-f9a3-5d67-b477-751312a3c4c2" :step/content 17592186371981 :step/next "step-24a4d735-bf7c-573f-9ba5-80873e983f63" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-24a4d735-bf7c-573f-9ba5-80873e983f63" :step/id #uuid "24a4d735-bf7c-573f-9ba5-80873e983f63" :step/content 17592186405083 :step/next "step-d897879d-381d-5c1c-a360-cf41d1b97535" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d897879d-381d-5c1c-a360-cf41d1b97535" :step/id #uuid "d897879d-381d-5c1c-a360-cf41d1b97535" :step/content 17592186405090 :step/next "step-2ec90c17-0169-5fa1-a78b-19f41aa17a65" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2ec90c17-0169-5fa1-a78b-19f41aa17a65" :step/id #uuid "2ec90c17-0169-5fa1-a78b-19f41aa17a65" :step/content 17592186405097 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100962 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1ef4f411-6c67-5c13-837f-d11ff722cfe0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100862 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e2f10b5f-3a80-55aa-94de-01383831523e" "question-aeb0f117-1019-5572-b78f-39043f8c3ded"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100863 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f237f691-54ad-5d16-bd80-dc2031f08456" "question-a823921c-a56b-531a-8f8b-ef8538df5571" "question-de36a313-5e05-5888-a50c-ca20f5e00cf3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101169 :question/problem "![](images/a4/a4a238887e789979e276421850330c5b36a82c485312c06e831c0df96266b85b.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-1,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $6.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-1,5]$ given that $f(-1)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-1,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=-1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =1 \\\\ f(5) & =f(-1)+{\\int }_{-1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+6 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{1,7\\}=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=1,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186098349 :answer-field/correct 17592186098351 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-1ef4f411-6c67-5c13-837f-d11ff722cfe0" :question/id #uuid "1ef4f411-6c67-5c13-837f-d11ff722cfe0" :question/math-academy-id "q-342898" :question/problem "![](images/19/192a69596e851fa35b02bbfd6bda06f01814d0d1c05b5698a4179d00a4bb14d2.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[1,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the area of the region between the graph and the $x$-axis is $8.$ Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[1,5]$ given that $f(1)=-2.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[1,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (1,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)\\ne 0$ on this interval. Therefore, the candidates are $x=1,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =-2 \\\\ f(5) & =f(1)+{\\int }_{1}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2+(-8) \\\\  & =-10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-2,-10\\}=-10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-10,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-cd2cc657-19ea-5669-92aa-e552ae923d2f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cd2cc657-19ea-5669-92aa-e552ae923d2f" :answer-field/id #uuid "cd2cc657-19ea-5669-92aa-e552ae923d2f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-2f24ec07-37f7-5bf9-9196-8f7a42ebd048" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2f24ec07-37f7-5bf9-9196-8f7a42ebd048"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f24ec07-37f7-5bf9-9196-8f7a42ebd048" :answer/id #uuid "2f24ec07-37f7-5bf9-9196-8f7a42ebd048" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e2f10b5f-3a80-55aa-94de-01383831523e" :question/id #uuid "e2f10b5f-3a80-55aa-94de-01383831523e" :question/math-academy-id "q-342899" :question/problem "![](images/98/98290071482fa2a8082932304fbb0d0cc0db54c47324479da7979079e9542551.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-4,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $6$ and $9$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-4,3]$ given that $f(-4)=-1.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates' test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-4,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-4,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-2.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-4,-2,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-4) & =-1 \\\\ f(-2) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+6 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-4)+{\\int }_{-4}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+6-9 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{-1,5,-4\\}=-4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=-4,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8586adde-a0fb-5449-942e-6459137f3b33"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8586adde-a0fb-5449-942e-6459137f3b33" :answer-field/id #uuid "8586adde-a0fb-5449-942e-6459137f3b33" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-94d8ff16-032a-51c4-9910-7cfbc5ab703c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-94d8ff16-032a-51c4-9910-7cfbc5ab703c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94d8ff16-032a-51c4-9910-7cfbc5ab703c" :answer/id #uuid "94d8ff16-032a-51c4-9910-7cfbc5ab703c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-aeb0f117-1019-5572-b78f-39043f8c3ded" :question/id #uuid "aeb0f117-1019-5572-b78f-39043f8c3ded" :question/math-academy-id "q-104255" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c23b2d07d656695feda6ae6e92d192661e30f2738b6af8342813b44f2885667.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,3].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above, where the areas of the regions between the graph and the $x$-axis are $5$ and $6,$ respectively (from left to right). Find the minimum value of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,3]$ given that $f(-2)=1.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,3]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval.\n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,3),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,0,3.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the value of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =1 \\\\ f(0) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+5 \\\\  & =6 \\\\ f(3) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{3}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+5-6 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{1,6,0\\}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=0,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d6416470-1d7a-5a75-a4b7-d19f68263b06"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d6416470-1d7a-5a75-a4b7-d19f68263b06" :answer-field/id #uuid "d6416470-1d7a-5a75-a4b7-d19f68263b06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ee74ca24-de88-5417-bf75-85352a8d8dc1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d97dcea9-edb6-5328-b0e6-580c59c6fd22" "answer-567d486e-aad4-556e-b6f9-b033c500c0c0" "answer-ee74ca24-de88-5417-bf75-85352a8d8dc1" "answer-5495561b-b8e3-5cea-ba85-ba6d6ddebae1" "answer-9a15af2c-3fdb-5eb8-9d83-be8b4bab64ec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d97dcea9-edb6-5328-b0e6-580c59c6fd22" :answer/id #uuid "d97dcea9-edb6-5328-b0e6-580c59c6fd22" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-567d486e-aad4-556e-b6f9-b033c500c0c0" :answer/id #uuid "567d486e-aad4-556e-b6f9-b033c500c0c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee74ca24-de88-5417-bf75-85352a8d8dc1" :answer/id #uuid "ee74ca24-de88-5417-bf75-85352a8d8dc1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5495561b-b8e3-5cea-ba85-ba6d6ddebae1" :answer/id #uuid "5495561b-b8e3-5cea-ba85-ba6d6ddebae1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a15af2c-3fdb-5eb8-9d83-be8b4bab64ec" :answer/id #uuid "9a15af2c-3fdb-5eb8-9d83-be8b4bab64ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f237f691-54ad-5d16-bd80-dc2031f08456" :question/id #uuid "f237f691-54ad-5d16-bd80-dc2031f08456" :question/math-academy-id "q-342944" :question/problem "![](images/ab/ab8e0cbaf83156609db79ec08202c8f77acfebd03663dfe273e4d75f9db03afb.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is differentiable on $[0,5].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ if $f(0)=4.$\n\n${f}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[0,5]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (0,5),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=2,4.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=0,2,4,5.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of three triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/ea/ea837252a62313eae506ef3d101c0b70121f6380694a49c2f54e1a19702949a5.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =4 \\\\ f(2) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5 \\\\ f(4) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{4}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+1+(-3) \\\\  & =2 \\\\ f(5) & =f(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{5}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+1+(-3)+0.5 \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**:  Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{4,5,2,2.5\\}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=2,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=4.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f3887f5c-297a-596e-b84b-b76944fcf812"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f3887f5c-297a-596e-b84b-b76944fcf812" :answer-field/id #uuid "f3887f5c-297a-596e-b84b-b76944fcf812" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1092e238-1e6d-5e1d-95b0-0c6a67c145f3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1092e238-1e6d-5e1d-95b0-0c6a67c145f3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1092e238-1e6d-5e1d-95b0-0c6a67c145f3" :answer/id #uuid "1092e238-1e6d-5e1d-95b0-0c6a67c145f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a823921c-a56b-531a-8f8b-ef8538df5571" :question/id #uuid "a823921c-a56b-531a-8f8b-ef8538df5571" :question/math-academy-id "q-341256" :question/problem "![](images/91/91441cecdbe477e4cf0115c1f7c4cf413fa36786690ff7e025ac2e62c711f66e.png)\n\nThe function $h(x)$ is differentiable on $[-2,4].$ The graph of ${h}^{′}(x)$ is shown above. Determine the minimum value of $h(x)$ on $[-2,4]$ if $h(-2)=6.$\n\n${h}_{\\text{min}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $h(x)$ on $[-2,4]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${h}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in [-2,4],$ and ${h}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=0$ and $x=3.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,0,3,4.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $h$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of three triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/19/1996a5ab1052a63a9513ed57a6a74ea82ad5994b62288cb04e4df02dac7589b6.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h(-2) & =6 \\\\ h(0) & =h(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{0}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6+2 \\\\  & =8 \\\\ h(3) & =h(0)+{\\int }_{0}^{3}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8+3 \\\\  & =11 \\\\ h(4) & =h(3)+{\\int }_{3}^{4}{h}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =11+(-1) \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Select the smallest value of $h$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $h$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{h}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{6,8,11,10\\}=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $h$ is ${h}_{\\text{min}}=6,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $h(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-2.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7b9e9bfc-789a-50b5-b6bd-12951e72a53e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7b9e9bfc-789a-50b5-b6bd-12951e72a53e" :answer-field/id #uuid "7b9e9bfc-789a-50b5-b6bd-12951e72a53e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-9fbcee1d-43be-51a7-9bb4-2317e13ecb37" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9fbcee1d-43be-51a7-9bb4-2317e13ecb37"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9fbcee1d-43be-51a7-9bb4-2317e13ecb37" :answer/id #uuid "9fbcee1d-43be-51a7-9bb4-2317e13ecb37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-de36a313-5e05-5888-a50c-ca20f5e00cf3" :question/id #uuid "de36a313-5e05-5888-a50c-ca20f5e00cf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-19083" :question/problem "![](images/42/429cba100e1cebd4a2048226f3c9d252fdec171b33679ab8ff6ba21014386aa4.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined and differentiable on $[-2,2].$ The graph of ${f}^{′}(x),$ the derivative of $f(x),$ is shown above. Find an $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ on the interval $[-2,2]$ given that $f(-2)=4.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the candidates test, the minimum value of $f(x)$ on $[-2,2]$ must occur at a critical point or an endpoint of the interval. \n\n**Step 1**: Find the critical points:\n\nWe see that ${f}^{′}(x)$ is defined everywhere on $x\\in (-2,2),$ and ${f}^{′}(x)=0$ at $x=-1.$ Therefore, the candidates are $x=-2,-1,2.$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the values of $f$ for each candidate using the fundamental theorem of calculus:\n\nThe function is made up of two triangles. Let's add the areas bounded by these shapes to our diagram:\n\n![](images/d2/d20759e0c246f1fad5e0a36e495302c6b8de0e23a5f8ed25bc291147750a1342.png)\n\nUsing the given information and the fundamental theorem of calculus, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =4 \\\\ f(-1) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{-1}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+(-1) \\\\  & =3 \\\\ f(2) & =f(-2)+{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4+(-1)+4.5 \\\\  & =7.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**:  Select the smallest value of $f$ from our candidates.\n\nComparing the values of $f$ found in step $2,$ we have\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{min}}=min\\{4,3,7.5\\}=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of $f$ is ${f}_{\\text{min}}=3,$ and the value of $x$ that minimizes $f(x)$ is ${x}_{\\text{min}}=-1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d63c5698-714c-5871-9be3-b86baeca70c0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d63c5698-714c-5871-9be3-b86baeca70c0" :answer-field/id #uuid "d63c5698-714c-5871-9be3-b86baeca70c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e58b9ca2-7225-576e-b1c8-3e4ca5e53b39" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e58b9ca2-7225-576e-b1c8-3e4ca5e53b39" "answer-e32cfebd-6726-5a8f-8ae6-17f8a1288601" "answer-a0f7fac7-0bab-58a8-b469-4851316d7779" "answer-d1c7e36a-3c3b-5caf-a88f-dad913ccfea0" "answer-59866eb1-6270-5d69-87ee-cd8defdbb03c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e58b9ca2-7225-576e-b1c8-3e4ca5e53b39" :answer/id #uuid "e58b9ca2-7225-576e-b1c8-3e4ca5e53b39" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e32cfebd-6726-5a8f-8ae6-17f8a1288601" :answer/id #uuid "e32cfebd-6726-5a8f-8ae6-17f8a1288601" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0f7fac7-0bab-58a8-b469-4851316d7779" :answer/id #uuid "a0f7fac7-0bab-58a8-b469-4851316d7779" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1c7e36a-3c3b-5caf-a88f-dad913ccfea0" :answer/id #uuid "d1c7e36a-3c3b-5caf-a88f-dad913ccfea0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-59866eb1-6270-5d69-87ee-cd8defdbb03c" :answer/id #uuid "59866eb1-6270-5d69-87ee-cd8defdbb03c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-87baf9e8-15e7-5139-9463-a29efe77f1af" :activity/id #uuid "87baf9e8-15e7-5139-9463-a29efe77f1af" :activity/title "Line Integrals of Scalar Functions Over Polar Curves" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048694 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-32ef930d-9267-5cf7-9b38-81828905e1cc" "step-084a598c-db02-50f2-b2a3-c92d836b8651" "step-3a1fe02b-b141-526c-b50f-2169f538c454" "step-9dd0aacd-79c0-5ad3-9dba-68ec20f639da" "step-84c36a68-d168-50c2-9fa4-68085a6b5cc6"] :activity/first-step "step-32ef930d-9267-5cf7-9b38-81828905e1cc" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-32ef930d-9267-5cf7-9b38-81828905e1cc" :step/id #uuid "32ef930d-9267-5cf7-9b38-81828905e1cc" :step/content 17592186455452 :step/next "step-084a598c-db02-50f2-b2a3-c92d836b8651" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-084a598c-db02-50f2-b2a3-c92d836b8651" :step/id #uuid "084a598c-db02-50f2-b2a3-c92d836b8651" :step/content 17592186455436 :step/next "step-3a1fe02b-b141-526c-b50f-2169f538c454" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3a1fe02b-b141-526c-b50f-2169f538c454" :step/id #uuid "3a1fe02b-b141-526c-b50f-2169f538c454" :step/content 17592186455497 :step/next "step-9dd0aacd-79c0-5ad3-9dba-68ec20f639da" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9dd0aacd-79c0-5ad3-9dba-68ec20f639da" :step/id #uuid "9dd0aacd-79c0-5ad3-9dba-68ec20f639da" :step/content 17592186455490 :step/next "step-84c36a68-d168-50c2-9fa4-68085a6b5cc6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-84c36a68-d168-50c2-9fa4-68085a6b5cc6" :step/id #uuid "84c36a68-d168-50c2-9fa4-68085a6b5cc6" :step/content 17592186345254 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186159872 :answer-field/choices 17592186159873] [:db/retract 17592186159872 :answer-field/choices 17592186159874] [:db/retract 17592186159872 :answer-field/choices 17592186159875] [:db/retract 17592186159872 :answer-field/choices 17592186159876] [:db/retract 17592186159872 :answer-field/choices 17592186159877] [:db/retract 17592186159776 :answer-field/choices 17592186159780] {:db/id 17592186162135 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-0b02c01d-81c9-557d-8302-9b53cb371ef3" "question-b1903a33-4088-5de3-962d-d8462d93f10a" "question-7601220c-590a-55f7-9ebc-4ae99b2759bd" "question-ef29076a-1f6b-53fd-9d0b-390107f2fcd7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161701 :question/problem "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S=S_X\\times S_Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.45$ | $0.15$ |\n\nFind an expression for the covariance in sigma notation." :question/worked-solution "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] = \\sum_{(x,y)\\in S}(x − \\textrm E[X])(y − \\textrm E[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=5$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.45$ | $0.15$ | $0.6$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.6$ | $0.4$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.4+4\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0.4+2.4 \\\\  & =2.8 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.6+5\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =1.2+2 \\\\  & =3.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-2.8)(y-3.2)f(x,y).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162132 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-72eb6c64-1cf3-5dde-b025-69a7158cf18b" "question-2ce015c9-6f9e-5825-a2d1-d00ea38bdb08" "question-d4d126ea-cebf-548f-a0ce-ca7561957246" "question-a725de9b-9a65-5d96-80a3-b82e15275c01" "question-88c40e55-9afb-513d-8399-c6e943bf9ded"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161698 :question/problem "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0$ | $0.4$ |\n\nCalculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] = \\sum_{(x,y)\\in S}(x − \\textrm E[X])(y − \\textrm E[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.1$ | $0.5$ | $0.6$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0$ | $0.4$ | $0.4$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $0.1$ | $0.9$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.6+2\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =0.6+0.8 \\\\  & =1.4 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.1+1\\cdot 0.9 \\\\  & =0+0.9 \\\\  & =0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-1.4)(y-0.9)f(x,y) \\\\  & =(1-1.4)(0-0.9)f(1,0) \\\\  & +(1-1.4)(1-0.9)f(1,1) \\\\  & +(2-1.4)(0-0.9)f(2,0) \\\\  & +(2-1.4)(1-0.9)f(2,1) \\\\  & =(-0.4)(-0.9)(0.1) \\\\  & +(-0.4)(0.1)(0.5) \\\\  & +(0.6)(-0.9)(0) \\\\  & +(0.6)(0.1)(0.4) \\\\  & =0.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162136 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fefbc176-d8f3-53d4-bb24-386b85087084" "question-de144ac2-d89b-5e69-8e47-de2554ce330e" "question-9576e0da-b7f3-5620-ad1a-43d5569461f3" "question-b781bcca-e045-541a-9fb8-bd56d31a763e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161702 :question/problem "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ |\n\nIf $\\textrm{E} [X]=2.8$ and $\\textrm{E}[Y] = 2.4,$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]\n=\n\\textrm{E} [XY] - \\textrm{E}[X]\\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nWe use the rule of the lazy statistician to find $\\textrm{E}[XY]\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(1)(2)(0.2)+(1)(3)(0.2)+(4)(2)(0.4)+(4)(3)(0.2) \\\\  & =6.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =6.6-(2.8)(2.4) \\\\  & =-0.12.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162134 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-28314126-6eaa-5326-a420-9e962fb3a315" "question-57775fd5-4d0c-5406-bb3e-6af6db58e9c9" "question-cf31f09e-0f29-56f8-9f97-abccd7a0062d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161700 :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n![](images/be/bebcce16140d945e7f9059627d76fc4a28f5638294029c4ccf3ccd0adbfb71a2.svg)\n\nFind the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "The joint support of $X$ and $Y$ is given by the rectangle \n\n$$\nS = [0,1] \\times [2,3].\n$$\n\nMoreover, we can write \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = g(x) \\cdot h(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x) =\\dfrac{3}{5} \\sqrt{x}$ and $h(y) =y.$ \n\nTherefore, $X$ and $Y$ are independent, and consequently $\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y] = 0.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-0b02c01d-81c9-557d-8302-9b53cb371ef3" :question/id #uuid "0b02c01d-81c9-557d-8302-9b53cb371ef3" :question/math-academy-id "q-147919" :question/problem "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.35$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n\nWhich of the following gives the covariance of $X$ and $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.35$ | $0.4$ | $0.75$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ | $0.25$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.75+4\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =1.5+1 \\\\  & =2.5 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.4+2\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0.4+1.2 \\\\  & =1.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-2.5)(y-1.6)f(x,y).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2f6c5e9a-0900-5e11-9ea7-1b9d6a41d202"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2f6c5e9a-0900-5e11-9ea7-1b9d6a41d202" :answer-field/id #uuid "2f6c5e9a-0900-5e11-9ea7-1b9d6a41d202" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b4311664-2c93-5de9-becf-6e058c78a3f7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cc263153-ffd5-5e1e-b113-5188544340fd" "answer-c7bafe94-6fd3-5d6e-ab7a-2f596948d2e7" "answer-b4311664-2c93-5de9-becf-6e058c78a3f7" "answer-463c0e5c-2ba7-50f8-96e8-20483d4c6b9b" "answer-9e60445e-91ea-598a-b36b-f086786a0ad0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc263153-ffd5-5e1e-b113-5188544340fd" :answer/id #uuid "cc263153-ffd5-5e1e-b113-5188544340fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-1.6)(y-2.5)f(x,y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7bafe94-6fd3-5d6e-ab7a-2f596948d2e7" :answer/id #uuid "c7bafe94-6fd3-5d6e-ab7a-2f596948d2e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-2)(y-1)f(x,y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b4311664-2c93-5de9-becf-6e058c78a3f7" :answer/id #uuid "b4311664-2c93-5de9-becf-6e058c78a3f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-2.5)(y-1.6)f(x,y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-463c0e5c-2ba7-50f8-96e8-20483d4c6b9b" :answer/id #uuid "463c0e5c-2ba7-50f8-96e8-20483d4c6b9b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(xy-0.9)f(x,y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e60445e-91ea-598a-b36b-f086786a0ad0" :answer/id #uuid "9e60445e-91ea-598a-b36b-f086786a0ad0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}y(x-2.5)f(x,y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b1903a33-4088-5de3-962d-d8462d93f10a" :question/id #uuid "b1903a33-4088-5de3-962d-d8462d93f10a" :question/math-academy-id "q-147809" :question/problem "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ |\n\nWhich of the following gives the covariance of $X$ and $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.35$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.25$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.5+1\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0+0.5 \\\\  & =0.5 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.4+2\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0.4+1.2 \\\\  & =1.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-0.5)(y-1.6)f(x,y).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ce88684a-1856-52b5-9580-414ac84284b9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ce88684a-1856-52b5-9580-414ac84284b9" :answer-field/id #uuid "ce88684a-1856-52b5-9580-414ac84284b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-14139549-eab2-5905-aafa-60d90dabc834" :answer-field/choices ["answer-14139549-eab2-5905-aafa-60d90dabc834" "answer-1513f6db-97ab-5bf7-b299-4e700eb9fc0f" "answer-960ed080-6b3a-5a07-87cd-958404cfb2fb" "answer-ed9ba43f-0e76-58ed-bedf-25fb75353679" "answer-8f779a44-d3e2-5a9f-8dfe-1118b2784489"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14139549-eab2-5905-aafa-60d90dabc834" :answer/id #uuid "14139549-eab2-5905-aafa-60d90dabc834" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-0.5)(y-1.6)f(x,y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1513f6db-97ab-5bf7-b299-4e700eb9fc0f" :answer/id #uuid "1513f6db-97ab-5bf7-b299-4e700eb9fc0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-1)(y-2)f(x,y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-960ed080-6b3a-5a07-87cd-958404cfb2fb" :answer/id #uuid "960ed080-6b3a-5a07-87cd-958404cfb2fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-1.6)(y-0.5)f(x,y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed9ba43f-0e76-58ed-bedf-25fb75353679" :answer/id #uuid "ed9ba43f-0e76-58ed-bedf-25fb75353679" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(xy-0.5)f(x,y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f779a44-d3e2-5a9f-8dfe-1118b2784489" :answer/id #uuid "8f779a44-d3e2-5a9f-8dfe-1118b2784489" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}x(y-1.6)f(x,y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7601220c-590a-55f7-9ebc-4ae99b2759bd" :question/id #uuid "7601220c-590a-55f7-9ebc-4ae99b2759bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-147925" :question/problem "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.12$ | $0.28$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.18$ | $0.12$ |\n\nWhich of the following gives the covariance of $X$ and $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.1$ | $0.12$ | $0.28$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.18$ | $0.12$ | $0.5$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.3$ | $0.3$ | $0.4$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.5+4\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.3+3\\cdot 0.3+5\\cdot 0.4 \\\\  & =0.3+0.9+2 \\\\  & =3.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-3)(y-3.2)f(x,y).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1543b923-1e80-5eb5-8e96-fc9f0590c038"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1543b923-1e80-5eb5-8e96-fc9f0590c038" :answer-field/id #uuid "1543b923-1e80-5eb5-8e96-fc9f0590c038" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d83ebf83-a6a9-5845-be10-a3c5854de0b5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d83ebf83-a6a9-5845-be10-a3c5854de0b5" "answer-78d9bb9c-a88b-5fd8-b931-84bb41627aeb" "answer-baf3b981-1b24-5859-8dba-d16fd4d8d6d5" "answer-d33db9bc-7f32-5a64-8e28-bd38babbfb73" "answer-5b1d773b-d4bb-59c7-830e-2a801507e0f6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d83ebf83-a6a9-5845-be10-a3c5854de0b5" :answer/id #uuid "d83ebf83-a6a9-5845-be10-a3c5854de0b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-3)(y-3.2)f(x,y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78d9bb9c-a88b-5fd8-b931-84bb41627aeb" :answer/id #uuid "78d9bb9c-a88b-5fd8-b931-84bb41627aeb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-3.2)(y-3)f(x,y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-baf3b981-1b24-5859-8dba-d16fd4d8d6d5" :answer/id #uuid "baf3b981-1b24-5859-8dba-d16fd4d8d6d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(xy-3)f(x,y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d33db9bc-7f32-5a64-8e28-bd38babbfb73" :answer/id #uuid "d33db9bc-7f32-5a64-8e28-bd38babbfb73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(xy-6.2)f(x,y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5b1d773b-d4bb-59c7-830e-2a801507e0f6" :answer/id #uuid "5b1d773b-d4bb-59c7-830e-2a801507e0f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}y(x-3)f(x,y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ef29076a-1f6b-53fd-9d0b-390107f2fcd7" :question/id #uuid "ef29076a-1f6b-53fd-9d0b-390107f2fcd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-147922" :question/problem "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=6$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n\nWhich of the following gives the covariance of $X$ and $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.15$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.35$ | $0.55$ |\n| $X=6$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.25+4\\cdot 0.55+6\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0.5+2.2+1.2 \\\\  & =3.9 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.4+4\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0.8+2.4 \\\\  & =3.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-3.9)(y-3.2)f(x,y).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b38d90bb-799a-5c24-8952-a9192763487d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b38d90bb-799a-5c24-8952-a9192763487d" :answer-field/id #uuid "b38d90bb-799a-5c24-8952-a9192763487d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c4cede03-6baa-574c-a7c4-ebb828929447" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d17c957f-7117-5e98-a9d4-19630ec78772" "answer-c4cede03-6baa-574c-a7c4-ebb828929447" "answer-9a1a2a02-2456-5c73-acbf-e95158980052" "answer-ebfe97bf-65ea-59c0-a75e-fdeed2ff85a8" "answer-e120f5f5-62c9-5f28-b399-3c892b4d769c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d17c957f-7117-5e98-a9d4-19630ec78772" :answer/id #uuid "d17c957f-7117-5e98-a9d4-19630ec78772" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-3.2)(y-3.9)f(x,y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c4cede03-6baa-574c-a7c4-ebb828929447" :answer/id #uuid "c4cede03-6baa-574c-a7c4-ebb828929447" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-3.9)(y-3.2)f(x,y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9a1a2a02-2456-5c73-acbf-e95158980052" :answer/id #uuid "9a1a2a02-2456-5c73-acbf-e95158980052" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-4)(y-3)f(x,y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ebfe97bf-65ea-59c0-a75e-fdeed2ff85a8" :answer/id #uuid "ebfe97bf-65ea-59c0-a75e-fdeed2ff85a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(xy-3)f(x,y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e120f5f5-62c9-5f28-b399-3c892b4d769c" :answer/id #uuid "e120f5f5-62c9-5f28-b399-3c892b4d769c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}x(y-3)f(x,y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162445 :question/problem "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S={S}_{X}\\times {S}_{Y}$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.12$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.28$ | $0.2$ |\n\nWhich of the following gives the covariance of $X$ and $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=5$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.12$ | $0.4$ | $0.52$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.28$ | $0.2$ | $0.48$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.4$ | $0.6$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.52+3\\cdot 0.48 \\\\  & =0.52+1.44 \\\\  & =1.96 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.4+5\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0.8+3 \\\\  & =3.8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-1.96)(y-3.8)f(x,y).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186159872 :answer-field/correct "answer-dec1711d-2b9c-592b-9554-e3ed886ff6ab" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b2bfb33d-f2a7-5b86-b5e6-a2e6913324fc" "answer-dec1711d-2b9c-592b-9554-e3ed886ff6ab" "answer-f6069bc4-f2a1-5a73-abe2-ed74bda23d86" "answer-64951951-633f-5c92-b725-b9375ea62d0c" "answer-46aaddbb-bca6-5492-a607-bf65179003b4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b2bfb33d-f2a7-5b86-b5e6-a2e6913324fc" :answer/id #uuid "b2bfb33d-f2a7-5b86-b5e6-a2e6913324fc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-1)(y-2)f(x,y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dec1711d-2b9c-592b-9554-e3ed886ff6ab" :answer/id #uuid "dec1711d-2b9c-592b-9554-e3ed886ff6ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-1.96)(y-3.8)f(x,y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6069bc4-f2a1-5a73-abe2-ed74bda23d86" :answer/id #uuid "f6069bc4-f2a1-5a73-abe2-ed74bda23d86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-3.8)(y-1.96)f(x,y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-64951951-633f-5c92-b725-b9375ea62d0c" :answer/id #uuid "64951951-633f-5c92-b725-b9375ea62d0c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(xy-1.96)f(x,y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46aaddbb-bca6-5492-a607-bf65179003b4" :answer/id #uuid "46aaddbb-bca6-5492-a607-bf65179003b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}x(y-1)f(x,y)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-72eb6c64-1cf3-5dde-b025-69a7158cf18b" :question/id #uuid "72eb6c64-1cf3-5dde-b025-69a7158cf18b" :question/math-academy-id "q-147971" :question/problem "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.5$ | $0.1$ |\n\nCalculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.25$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.5$ | $0.1$ | $0.6$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.65$ | $0.35$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.4+2\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0+1.2 \\\\  & =1.2 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.65+3\\cdot 0.35 \\\\  & =0.65+1.05 \\\\  & =1.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-1.2)(y-1.7)f(x,y) \\\\  & =(0-1.2)(1-1.7)f(0,1) \\\\  & +(0-1.2)(3-1.7)f(0,3) \\\\  & +(2-1.2)(1-1.7)f(2,1) \\\\  & +(2-1.2)(3-1.7)f(2,3) \\\\  & =(-1.2)(-0.7)(0.15) \\\\  & +(-1.2)(1.3)(0.25) \\\\  & +(0.8)(-0.7)(0.5) \\\\  & +(0.8)(1.3)(0.1) \\\\  & =-0.44.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e318cf23-99c3-5de3-b262-09a09b316069"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e318cf23-99c3-5de3-b262-09a09b316069" :answer-field/id #uuid "e318cf23-99c3-5de3-b262-09a09b316069" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6290de49-ff0a-5e48-8570-362804720050" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b9321fe8-9a74-5033-a5e9-d9d7066c5ed1" "answer-5054ca35-1983-54f9-8a67-129a18df5cf4" "answer-94764131-66fd-56ec-b6a6-08e292c7818e" "answer-e45bf0f5-e0ae-5592-aa27-6e370b6c4f78" "answer-6290de49-ff0a-5e48-8570-362804720050"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9321fe8-9a74-5033-a5e9-d9d7066c5ed1" :answer/id #uuid "b9321fe8-9a74-5033-a5e9-d9d7066c5ed1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.18" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5054ca35-1983-54f9-8a67-129a18df5cf4" :answer/id #uuid "5054ca35-1983-54f9-8a67-129a18df5cf4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.26" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94764131-66fd-56ec-b6a6-08e292c7818e" :answer/id #uuid "94764131-66fd-56ec-b6a6-08e292c7818e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e45bf0f5-e0ae-5592-aa27-6e370b6c4f78" :answer/id #uuid "e45bf0f5-e0ae-5592-aa27-6e370b6c4f78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.38" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6290de49-ff0a-5e48-8570-362804720050" :answer/id #uuid "6290de49-ff0a-5e48-8570-362804720050" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.44" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2ce015c9-6f9e-5825-a2d1-d00ea38bdb08" :question/id #uuid "2ce015c9-6f9e-5825-a2d1-d00ea38bdb08" :question/math-academy-id "q-147968" :question/problem "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0$ | $0.65$ |\n\nCalculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.1$ | $0.25$ | $0.35$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0$ | $0.65$ | $0.65$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.1$ | $0.9$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 0.35+2\\cdot 0.65 \\\\  & =0+1.3 \\\\  & =1.3 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.1+4\\cdot 0.9 \\\\  & =0.1+3.6 \\\\  & =3.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-1.3)(y-3.7)f(x,y) \\\\  & =(0-1.3)(1-3.7)f(0,1) \\\\  & +(0-1.3)(4-3.7)f(0,4) \\\\  & +(2-1.3)(1-3.7)f(2,1) \\\\  & +(2-1.3)(4-3.7)f(2,4) \\\\  & =(-1.3)(-2.7)(0.1) \\\\  & +(-1.3)(0.3)(0.25) \\\\  & +(0.7)(-2.7)(0) \\\\  & +(0.7)(0.3)(0.65) \\\\  & =0.39.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1e0828f6-c287-56e0-916a-4821803b818a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1e0828f6-c287-56e0-916a-4821803b818a" :answer-field/id 