text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.3$ | $0.1$ | $0.4$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.4$ | $0.2$ | $0.6$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.7$ | $0.3$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.4+3\\cdot 0.6 \\\\  & =0.8+1.8 \\\\  & =2.6 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.7+2\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =0.7+0.6 \\\\  & =1.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-2.6)(y-1.3)f(x,y) \\\\  & =(2-2.6)(1-1.3)f(2,1) \\\\  & +(2-2.6)(2-1.3)f(2,2) \\\\  & +(3-2.6)(1-1.3)f(3,1) \\\\  & +(3-2.6)(2-1.3)f(3,2) \\\\  & =(-0.6)(-0.3)(0.3) \\\\  & +(-0.6)(0.7)(0.1) \\\\  & +(0.4)(-0.3)(0.4) \\\\  & +(0.4)(0.7)(0.2) \\\\  & =0.02.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-811156d6-65a9-5f35-a8ae-1aca43ee3f12"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-811156d6-65a9-5f35-a8ae-1aca43ee3f12" :answer-field/id #uuid "811156d6-65a9-5f35-a8ae-1aca43ee3f12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8924f8e7-ffa9-5f5c-a566-8747fbe8e0e0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8924f8e7-ffa9-5f5c-a566-8747fbe8e0e0" "answer-4b3ee59b-1cd4-5037-ae30-111664dd5bf5" "answer-371679c2-2b5e-5e2c-aee9-30b820dc41f2" "answer-6031512a-ed66-5a1d-baeb-1e186681d531" "answer-55a06749-2d40-522b-a303-cd5f186203c0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8924f8e7-ffa9-5f5c-a566-8747fbe8e0e0" :answer/id #uuid "8924f8e7-ffa9-5f5c-a566-8747fbe8e0e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.02" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b3ee59b-1cd4-5037-ae30-111664dd5bf5" :answer/id #uuid "4b3ee59b-1cd4-5037-ae30-111664dd5bf5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.08" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-371679c2-2b5e-5e2c-aee9-30b820dc41f2" :answer/id #uuid "371679c2-2b5e-5e2c-aee9-30b820dc41f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6031512a-ed66-5a1d-baeb-1e186681d531" :answer/id #uuid "6031512a-ed66-5a1d-baeb-1e186681d531" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-55a06749-2d40-522b-a303-cd5f186203c0" :answer/id #uuid "55a06749-2d40-522b-a303-cd5f186203c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.23" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a725de9b-9a65-5d96-80a3-b82e15275c01" :question/id #uuid "a725de9b-9a65-5d96-80a3-b82e15275c01" :question/math-academy-id "q-147967" :question/problem "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.5$ | $0$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ |\n\nCalculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find the marginal mass functions for $X$ and $Y.$ These are given by the row and column totals, respectively.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ |\n| $X=3$ | $0.2$ | $0.3$ | $0.5$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}$ | $0.7$ | $0.3$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now compute $\\text{E}[X]$ and $\\text{E}[Y],$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot {f}_{X}(x) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.5+3\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =0.5+1.5 \\\\  & =2 \\\\ \\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot {f}_{Y}(y) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 0.7+4\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =1.4+1.2 \\\\  & =2.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for the covariance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-2)(y-2.6)f(x,y) \\\\  & =(1-2)(2-2.6)f(1,2) \\\\  & +(1-2)(4-2.6)f(1,4) \\\\  & +(3-2)(2-2.6)f(3,2) \\\\  & +(3-2)(4-2.6)f(3,4) \\\\  & =(-1)(-0.6)(0.5) \\\\  & +(-1)(1.4)(0) \\\\  & +(1)(-0.6)(0.2) \\\\  & +(1)(1.4)(0.3) \\\\  & =0.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5afcdbc9-8272-5f52-8b74-ad215de0627c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5afcdbc9-8272-5f52-8b74-ad215de0627c" :answer-field/id #uuid "5afcdbc9-8272-5f52-8b74-ad215de0627c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dab5d621-8844-5673-8184-cb03e682f420" :answer-field/choices ["answer-375d7ff0-fa73-535c-b7b2-882d3c449c47" "answer-c2a22f44-f278-57d8-bf67-af4a421c8db7" "answer-f8889368-331a-5a5c-a70e-2170551b3d70" "answer-dab5d621-8844-5673-8184-cb03e682f420" "answer-2a605469-14f1-547a-a954-a026a62f884f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-375d7ff0-fa73-535c-b7b2-882d3c449c47" :answer/id #uuid "375d7ff0-fa73-535c-b7b2-882d3c449c47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2a22f44-f278-57d8-bf67-af4a421c8db7" :answer/id #uuid "c2a22f44-f278-57d8-bf67-af4a421c8db7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f8889368-331a-5a5c-a70e-2170551b3d70" :answer/id #uuid "f8889368-331a-5a5c-a70e-2170551b3d70" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dab5d621-8844-5673-8184-cb03e682f420" :answer/id #uuid "dab5d621-8844-5673-8184-cb03e682f420" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a605469-14f1-547a-a954-a026a62f884f" :answer/id #uuid "2a605469-14f1-547a-a954-a026a62f884f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-88c40e55-9afb-513d-8399-c6e943bf9ded" :question/id #uuid "88c40e55-9afb-513d-8399-c6e943bf9ded" :question/math-academy-id "q-147964" :question/problem "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.05$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=1$ | $0.15$ | $0.6$ |\n\nCalculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$\n\n*Hint: It can be shown that $P(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)$ for all possible values of $x$ and $y.$*" :question/worked-solution "The covariance of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with joint support $S$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}(x-\\text{E}[X])(y-\\text{E}[Y])f(x,y).\n$$\n\nWe're told that\n\n$$\nP(X=x,Y=y)=P(X=x)\\cdot P(Y=y)\n$$\n\nfor all possible values of $x$ and $y.$ Therefore, $X$ and $Y$ are independent. \n\nSince $X$ are $Y$ are independent, we have $\\text{Cov}(X,Y)=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6210317b-2bf1-5300-979a-28521cef14d8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6210317b-2bf1-5300-979a-28521cef14d8" :answer-field/id #uuid "6210317b-2bf1-5300-979a-28521cef14d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-83ff5871-6f27-5a5e-9a5a-cfcf1a804151" :answer-field/choices ["answer-83ff5871-6f27-5a5e-9a5a-cfcf1a804151" "answer-b0d883f9-65cd-5210-a239-f5446174972c" "answer-92f5bc33-1122-5445-b18c-128cf3c6fad5" "answer-205120df-f97d-504e-8f59-c17c4b4fa683" "answer-142f00ef-98a1-5d96-923f-8c06a4dafd48"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83ff5871-6f27-5a5e-9a5a-cfcf1a804151" :answer/id #uuid "83ff5871-6f27-5a5e-9a5a-cfcf1a804151" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0d883f9-65cd-5210-a239-f5446174972c" :answer/id #uuid "b0d883f9-65cd-5210-a239-f5446174972c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.02" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-92f5bc33-1122-5445-b18c-128cf3c6fad5" :answer/id #uuid "92f5bc33-1122-5445-b18c-128cf3c6fad5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-205120df-f97d-504e-8f59-c17c4b4fa683" :answer/id #uuid "205120df-f97d-504e-8f59-c17c4b4fa683" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-142f00ef-98a1-5d96-923f-8c06a4dafd48" :answer/id #uuid "142f00ef-98a1-5d96-923f-8c06a4dafd48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fefbc176-d8f3-53d4-bb24-386b85087084" :question/id #uuid "fefbc176-d8f3-53d4-bb24-386b85087084" :question/math-academy-id "q-148014" :question/problem "If $\\text{E}[X]=5.2,$ $\\text{E}[Y]=4.2,$ and $\\text{E}[XY]=22$ for two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =22-(5.2)\\cdot (4.2) \\\\  & =0.16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of $X$ and $Y$ is $0.16.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6c475b4b-06c7-52f7-8d6e-71a8a2f19ded"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6c475b4b-06c7-52f7-8d6e-71a8a2f19ded" :answer-field/id #uuid "6c475b4b-06c7-52f7-8d6e-71a8a2f19ded" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-54d258a4-8ebf-51a7-a775-2aba72b3518a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bcce625e-1d39-516d-a209-5a72ac27a373" "answer-54d258a4-8ebf-51a7-a775-2aba72b3518a" "answer-c3490969-2466-5c09-b71b-7b9702da6997" "answer-75bec082-0e2e-5a44-bf42-16ad67606664" "answer-bc4512a9-45d3-53d4-9436-13626851cb5e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bcce625e-1d39-516d-a209-5a72ac27a373" :answer/id #uuid "bcce625e-1d39-516d-a209-5a72ac27a373" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54d258a4-8ebf-51a7-a775-2aba72b3518a" :answer/id #uuid "54d258a4-8ebf-51a7-a775-2aba72b3518a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3490969-2466-5c09-b71b-7b9702da6997" :answer/id #uuid "c3490969-2466-5c09-b71b-7b9702da6997" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75bec082-0e2e-5a44-bf42-16ad67606664" :answer/id #uuid "75bec082-0e2e-5a44-bf42-16ad67606664" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.36" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc4512a9-45d3-53d4-9436-13626851cb5e" :answer/id #uuid "bc4512a9-45d3-53d4-9436-13626851cb5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.42" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-de144ac2-d89b-5e69-8e47-de2554ce330e" :question/id #uuid "de144ac2-d89b-5e69-8e47-de2554ce330e" :question/math-academy-id "q-148028" :question/problem "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.25$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.35$ | $0.25$ |\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=3.2$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=3.8,$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nWe use the rule of the lazy statistician to find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(2)(3)(0.25)+(2)(5)(0.15)+(4)(3)(0.35)+(4)(5)(0.25) \\\\  & =12.2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =12.2-(3.2)(3.8) \\\\  & =0.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ce8c62ae-2570-57ee-ab84-4a3dc6da3175"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ce8c62ae-2570-57ee-ab84-4a3dc6da3175" :answer-field/id #uuid "ce8c62ae-2570-57ee-ab84-4a3dc6da3175" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-873920de-2222-59b9-88f0-66b71c16c424" :answer-field/choices ["answer-873920de-2222-59b9-88f0-66b71c16c424" "answer-2e6403bf-0c0f-5722-a905-d4b7937789ae" "answer-5c85b8f8-8638-554c-8039-45a2e66a8c10" "answer-07ef0ca5-25f0-5fa7-8ba9-189382bdbf81" "answer-3e134d87-91b5-59ee-9fa9-8526a1584916"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-873920de-2222-59b9-88f0-66b71c16c424" :answer/id #uuid "873920de-2222-59b9-88f0-66b71c16c424" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.04" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e6403bf-0c0f-5722-a905-d4b7937789ae" :answer/id #uuid "2e6403bf-0c0f-5722-a905-d4b7937789ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.08" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c85b8f8-8638-554c-8039-45a2e66a8c10" :answer/id #uuid "5c85b8f8-8638-554c-8039-45a2e66a8c10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07ef0ca5-25f0-5fa7-8ba9-189382bdbf81" :answer/id #uuid "07ef0ca5-25f0-5fa7-8ba9-189382bdbf81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e134d87-91b5-59ee-9fa9-8526a1584916" :answer/id #uuid "3e134d87-91b5-59ee-9fa9-8526a1584916" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9576e0da-b7f3-5620-ad1a-43d5569461f3" :question/id #uuid "9576e0da-b7f3-5620-ad1a-43d5569461f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-148027" :question/problem "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=0$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.4$ | $0.25$ |\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=1.3$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=1.9,$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nWe use the rule of the lazy statistician to find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(0)(1)(0.15)+(0)(3)(0.2)+(2)(1)(0.4)+(2)(3)(0.25) \\\\  & =2.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =2.3-(1.3)(1.9) \\\\  & =-0.17.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5e9bc202-ef39-51ee-b9b1-9f179e2aa12e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5e9bc202-ef39-51ee-b9b1-9f179e2aa12e" :answer-field/id #uuid "5e9bc202-ef39-51ee-b9b1-9f179e2aa12e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-783f33c0-deac-509b-8860-a758465241bc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2a68ae83-4e32-50f6-a16e-5467eb325b33" "answer-ebb3475e-596a-53ca-a088-db2e7f5abce7" "answer-783f33c0-deac-509b-8860-a758465241bc" "answer-5ea5a9f6-1000-5073-8452-d449754e937c" "answer-edf7be1a-0847-520d-a68d-210de044d15a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a68ae83-4e32-50f6-a16e-5467eb325b33" :answer/id #uuid "2a68ae83-4e32-50f6-a16e-5467eb325b33" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.06" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ebb3475e-596a-53ca-a088-db2e7f5abce7" :answer/id #uuid "ebb3475e-596a-53ca-a088-db2e7f5abce7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-783f33c0-deac-509b-8860-a758465241bc" :answer/id #uuid "783f33c0-deac-509b-8860-a758465241bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ea5a9f6-1000-5073-8452-d449754e937c" :answer/id #uuid "5ea5a9f6-1000-5073-8452-d449754e937c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.02" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-edf7be1a-0847-520d-a68d-210de044d15a" :answer/id #uuid "edf7be1a-0847-520d-a68d-210de044d15a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162422 :question/problem "If $\\text{E}[X]=20,$ $\\text{E}[Y]=2,$ and $\\text{E}[XY]=44.8$ for two random variables $X$ and $Y,$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =44.8-20\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the covariance of $X$ and $Y$ is $4.8.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186159734 :answer-field/correct 17592186159739 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-b781bcca-e045-541a-9fb8-bd56d31a763e" :question/id #uuid "b781bcca-e045-541a-9fb8-bd56d31a763e" :question/math-academy-id "q-148030" :question/problem "The joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is given below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=2$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n| $X=4$ | $0.2$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=6$ | $0.2$ | $0.1$ |\n\nIf $\\text{E}[X]=4$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=1.4,$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nWe use the rule of the lazy statistician to find $\\text{E}[XY]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\sum }_{(x,y)\\in S}xy\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & =(2)(1)(0.2)+(2)(2)(0.1)+(4)(1)(0.2) \\\\  & +(4)(2)(0.2)+(6)(1)(0.2)+(6)(2)(0.1) \\\\  & =5.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the covariance formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =5.6-(4)(1.4) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-042ea3ff-f58a-51d0-9dff-1a3e79511b55"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-042ea3ff-f58a-51d0-9dff-1a3e79511b55" :answer-field/id #uuid "042ea3ff-f58a-51d0-9dff-1a3e79511b55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-dfeb9861-b71c-5185-9816-b52111412339" :answer-field/choices ["answer-57eb2365-78bc-5b7f-80bb-17bad2196177" "answer-bd05d92c-95ee-535e-ba58-0ce2922a4501" "answer-dfeb9861-b71c-5185-9816-b52111412339" "answer-3ee2ecab-b07c-54f2-bc92-ac62bef82687" "answer-3309982c-a8e1-5289-81aa-260983631647"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-57eb2365-78bc-5b7f-80bb-17bad2196177" :answer/id #uuid "57eb2365-78bc-5b7f-80bb-17bad2196177" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bd05d92c-95ee-535e-ba58-0ce2922a4501" :answer/id #uuid "bd05d92c-95ee-535e-ba58-0ce2922a4501" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dfeb9861-b71c-5185-9816-b52111412339" :answer/id #uuid "dfeb9861-b71c-5185-9816-b52111412339" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3ee2ecab-b07c-54f2-bc92-ac62bef82687" :answer/id #uuid "3ee2ecab-b07c-54f2-bc92-ac62bef82687" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3309982c-a8e1-5289-81aa-260983631647" :answer/id #uuid "3309982c-a8e1-5289-81aa-260983631647" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162429 :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-x-y},\\, & x>0,\\,y>0, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "The joint support of $X$ and $Y$ is given by the rectangle \n\n$$\nS=(0,\\infty )\\times (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nMoreover, we can write \n\n$$\nf(x,y)=g(x)\\cdot h(y),\n$$\n\nwhere $g(x)={e}^{-x}$ and $h(y)={e}^{-y}.$ \n\nTherefore, $X$ and $Y$ are independent, and consequently $\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186159776 :answer-field/correct 17592186159777 :answer-field/choices ["answer-6f5cd552-4fe8-5f5e-92a8-327795378a43"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6f5cd552-4fe8-5f5e-92a8-327795378a43" :answer/id #uuid "6f5cd552-4fe8-5f5e-92a8-327795378a43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-28314126-6eaa-5326-a420-9e962fb3a315" :question/id #uuid "28314126-6eaa-5326-a420-9e962fb3a315" :question/math-academy-id "q-147950" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5}({x}^{3}+y),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{14}{25}$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{19}{15},$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nTo find $\\text{E}[XY],$ we use the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xyf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot \\frac{2}{5}({x}^{3}+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2}{5}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{4}+xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2}{5}y{[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{2}{5}y[\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{1}{2}y]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{2}{25}y+\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{25}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{15}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{75}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the covariance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{52}{75}-(\\frac{14}{25})(\\frac{19}{15}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{125}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0c5eaee8-55ac-56b1-9f8a-3b273fc778c6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0c5eaee8-55ac-56b1-9f8a-3b273fc778c6" :answer-field/id #uuid "0c5eaee8-55ac-56b1-9f8a-3b273fc778c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b96bcde9-98d4-501d-9658-acfed2d6a463" :answer-field/choices ["answer-00c23a39-a692-59f0-82d6-1e42aa201914" "answer-b96bcde9-98d4-501d-9658-acfed2d6a463" "answer-587f7451-fccf-5637-a502-7b906ce3c5c4" "answer-5c47d0b3-930f-5e0f-872b-04d2b1a8ab8a" "answer-a7f54150-f261-5643-bee1-64de82d8bd4c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00c23a39-a692-59f0-82d6-1e42aa201914" :answer/id #uuid "00c23a39-a692-59f0-82d6-1e42aa201914" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{214}{125}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b96bcde9-98d4-501d-9658-acfed2d6a463" :answer/id #uuid "b96bcde9-98d4-501d-9658-acfed2d6a463" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{125}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-587f7451-fccf-5637-a502-7b906ce3c5c4" :answer/id #uuid "587f7451-fccf-5637-a502-7b906ce3c5c4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c47d0b3-930f-5e0f-872b-04d2b1a8ab8a" :answer/id #uuid "5c47d0b3-930f-5e0f-872b-04d2b1a8ab8a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7f54150-f261-5643-bee1-64de82d8bd4c" :answer/id #uuid "a7f54150-f261-5643-bee1-64de82d8bd4c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{52}{75}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-57775fd5-4d0c-5406-bb3e-6af6db58e9c9" :question/id #uuid "57775fd5-4d0c-5406-bb3e-6af6db58e9c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-147945" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{16}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3}),\\, & 0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{13}{10}$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{13}{10},$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nTo find $\\text{E}[XY],$ we use the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xyf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}xy\\cdot \\frac{1}{16}({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{16}y{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{4}+x{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{16}y{[\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{16}y[\\frac{32}{5}+2{y}^{3}]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{2}{5}y+\\frac{1}{8}{y}^{4})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{5}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{40}{y}^{5}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the covariance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}-(\\frac{13}{10})(\\frac{13}{10}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{100}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-df1e1d9e-0dc1-5725-b77d-a7edfadad3f3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-df1e1d9e-0dc1-5725-b77d-a7edfadad3f3" :answer-field/id #uuid "df1e1d9e-0dc1-5725-b77d-a7edfadad3f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d4c9bf09-d22c-5175-8478-b7129d7b3be1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a49377ee-551e-5cb2-a025-28a2082dd9a6" "answer-d4c9bf09-d22c-5175-8478-b7129d7b3be1" "answer-d5d6b444-7189-5948-8da8-571f44e98958" "answer-ee2b176c-6cdb-59e7-ad44-08efb439d9ed" "answer-8a36bbdb-d7de-52bd-851d-e6bd6d33739c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a49377ee-551e-5cb2-a025-28a2082dd9a6" :answer/id #uuid "a49377ee-551e-5cb2-a025-28a2082dd9a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{161}{100}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4c9bf09-d22c-5175-8478-b7129d7b3be1" :answer/id #uuid "d4c9bf09-d22c-5175-8478-b7129d7b3be1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{100}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d5d6b444-7189-5948-8da8-571f44e98958" :answer/id #uuid "d5d6b444-7189-5948-8da8-571f44e98958" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee2b176c-6cdb-59e7-ad44-08efb439d9ed" :answer/id #uuid "ee2b176c-6cdb-59e7-ad44-08efb439d9ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8a36bbdb-d7de-52bd-851d-e6bd6d33739c" :answer/id #uuid "8a36bbdb-d7de-52bd-851d-e6bd6d33739c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cf31f09e-0f29-56f8-9f97-abccd7a0062d" :question/id #uuid "cf31f09e-0f29-56f8-9f97-abccd7a0062d" :question/math-academy-id "q-147844" :question/problem "The random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y, & 0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{7}{12}$ and $\\text{E}[Y]=\\frac{7}{12},$ calculate the covariance of $X$ and $Y.$" :question/worked-solution "The covariance of $X$ and $Y$ can be calculated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nTo find $\\text{E}[XY],$ we use the rule of the lazy statistician:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xyf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}y[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{2}y]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{1}{3}y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{6}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the covariance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-(\\frac{7}{12})(\\frac{7}{12}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{144}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d2d1682f-7eb6-5691-9d8f-0cd7e762540d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d2d1682f-7eb6-5691-9d8f-0cd7e762540d" :answer-field/id #uuid "d2d1682f-7eb6-5691-9d8f-0cd7e762540d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9d4df0cb-ec84-5087-bbab-1841e915272d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fb927e68-a451-56b0-abb4-b479e7119a3a" "answer-9d4df0cb-ec84-5087-bbab-1841e915272d" "answer-8efe1823-faf5-5f48-bcfa-08d5910e2be9" "answer-caf882ed-b1ef-5c87-ab85-e0fb21914627" "answer-6edd5f67-1149-5905-9dfd-2531aac7d778"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb927e68-a451-56b0-abb4-b479e7119a3a" :answer/id #uuid "fb927e68-a451-56b0-abb4-b479e7119a3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9d4df0cb-ec84-5087-bbab-1841e915272d" :answer/id #uuid "9d4df0cb-ec84-5087-bbab-1841e915272d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{144}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8efe1823-faf5-5f48-bcfa-08d5910e2be9" :answer/id #uuid "8efe1823-faf5-5f48-bcfa-08d5910e2be9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-caf882ed-b1ef-5c87-ab85-e0fb21914627" :answer/id #uuid "caf882ed-b1ef-5c87-ab85-e0fb21914627" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{96}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6edd5f67-1149-5905-9dfd-2531aac7d778" :answer/id #uuid "6edd5f67-1149-5905-9dfd-2531aac7d778" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{7}{144}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163290 :tutorial/content "We often want to measure the strength of the (linear) dependence between two random variables. One measure that we use for this purpose is covariance. \n\nThe **covariance** between two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] = \\textrm{E}\\Big[\\left(X - \\textrm{E}[X]\\right)\\left(Y - \\textrm{E}[Y]\\right) \\Big].\n$$\n\nLet's break this formula down a bit:\n\n- $\\left(X - \\textrm{E}[X]\\right)$ is the deviation of $X$ from its mean\n\n- $\\left(Y - \\textrm{E}[Y]\\right)$ is the deviation of $Y$ from its mean\n\nTherefore, the covariance measures the expected value of the product of these deviations.\n\nThe covariance can also be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\sum_{(x,y)\\in S}(x − \\textrm E[X])(y − \\textrm E[Y])f(x,y)\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)$ is the joint PMF of $X$ and $Y,$ and $S$ is the joint support.\n\nLet's sketch a few diagrams to get a feel for what the covariance tells us.\n\n- A *positive* covariance means that if $X$ increases, then $Y$ is likely to increase. We say that $X$ and $Y$ are **positively correlated**.\n\n![](images/bb/bbfae3f0fb38bc8652cd26716e4b0b128cf1f2039350267034d97161d7f5dac4.png)\n\n- A *negative* covariance means that if $X$ increases, then $Y$ is likely to decrease. We say that $X$ and $Y$ are **negatively correlated**.\n\n![](images/fd/fd45053fdc39f070e3438c3b8c61fe01613201595cfe00bd4cc6c6e481bf73ab.png)\n\nNote the following:\n\n- If $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] = 0.\n$$\n\nThe reverse implication does not always hold! For example, two random variables can have zero covariance yet be dependent. This is because covariance measures *linear* dependence only.\n\n- The covariance of a random variable with itself is its variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,X] & =\\text{E}[(X-\\text{E}[X])(X-\\text{E}[X])] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{(X-\\text{E}[X])}^{2}] \\\\  & =\\text{Var}[X]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Aside from indicating whether two random variables are positively or negatively correlated, the covariance *by itself* does not tell us very much. For example, if \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X_1, Y_1] > \\textrm{Cov}[X_2, Y_2] > 0\n$$\n\nthis does not necessarily mean that the dependence between $X_1$ and $Y_1$ is somehow \"stronger\" than the dependence between $X_2$ and $Y_2.$ This is because the covariance does not take the variance of the individual random variables into account. \n\nIn a future lesson, we'll use the covariance to define the so-called  **correlation coefficient,** which *does* consider the variance of the individual random variables and so *can* be used to compare the relative strength of the linear dependence between two pairs of random variables." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163343 :tutorial/content "You may have noticed that computing the covariance using the definition is quite a lot of work. Is there a formula that makes it easier?  \n\nIndeed, there is. It can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\textrm E [XY] − \\textrm E [X]\\cdot \\textrm E [Y].\n$$\n\nWe'll prove this formula at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's get some practice at applying it." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163293 :tutorial/content "If $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, the covariance between $X$ and $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\iint_\\limits{\\mathbb R^2}(x − \\textrm E[X])(y − \\textrm E[Y])f(x,y) \\, \\textrm d x\\textrm d y.\n$$\n\nAs is the case with discrete random variables, it's often easier to compute the covariance using the following formula: \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X,Y]\n=\n\\textrm{E} [XY] - \\textrm{E}[X]\\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y]\n$$\n\nSuppose we're given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function\n\n![](images/c5/c589b62f579cb5efe7c4a2b86aa9bebbbbec81517c8bd7872c3c6a0b019e08cc.svg)\n\nTo compute the covariance of this distribution, we first use the rule of the lazy statistician to find $\\textrm{E}[XY]\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[XY] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xyf(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}xy\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(x+\\frac{y}{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}xy)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}y{[\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+\\frac{1}{4}{x}^{2}y]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{2}y[\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{4}y]\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{1}{6}y+\\frac{1}{8}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{12}{y}^{2}+\\frac{1}{24}{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\cdot {2}^{2}+\\frac{1}{24}\\cdot {2}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing methods discussed in previous lessons, we can compute $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and $\\textrm{E}[Y]{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\textrm{E}[X] = \\dfrac{7}{12}, \\qquad \\textrm{E}[Y] = \\dfrac76\n$$\n\nFinally, using the covariance formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}-(\\frac{7}{12})(\\frac{7}{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{72}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that if two random variables are independent then their covariance equals zero. Let's see an example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163292 :tutorial/content "Let's now prove the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\textrm E [XY] − \\textrm E[X]\\cdot \\textrm E[Y]\n$$\n\nWe start with the definition of covariance, given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}[X, Y] =\\textrm  E\\Big[\\left(X− \\textrm E[X]\\right)\\left(Y−\\textrm E[Y]\\right)\\Big].\n$$\n\nExpanding the product in the brackets, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y]=\\text{E}[XY-X\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot Y+\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall that if $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[a]=a, \\qquad \\textrm{E}[aX + bY] = a\\cdot \\textrm{E}[X] + b \\cdot \\textrm{E}[Y].\n$$\n\nSince $\\textrm{E}[X]$ and are $\\textrm{E}[Y]$ are both constants, the covariance formula simplifies as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}[X,Y] & =\\text{E}[XY-X\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot Y+\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]]-\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X]\\cdot Y]+\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]+\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[XY]-\\text{E}[X]\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162187 :activity/expected-seconds 1200.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221402 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ebc091be-da12-5892-8a06-6dc1bcb20797"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ebc091be-da12-5892-8a06-6dc1bcb20797" :question/id #uuid "ebc091be-da12-5892-8a06-6dc1bcb20797" :question/math-academy-id "q-334642" :question/problem "A numerical method is used to approximate the solution to an initial value problem.\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.10,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.10)=0.24.$\n\n- With step size $Δx=0.05,$ the observed local truncation error is $τ(0.05)=0.02.$\n\nEstimate the order of the numerical method." :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the LTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p+1}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.10$ to $0.05.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.10}{0.05}=2.\n$$\n\nAs a result, for a numerical method of order $p,$ the LTE should decrease by a factor of approximately\n\n$$\n{k}^{p+1}={2}^{p+1}.\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, the LTE actually decreases from $0.24$ to $0.02.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\n\\frac{0.24}{0.02}=12.\n$$\n\nComparing the two scale factors for the LTE, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{p+1} & ≈12 \\\\ p+1 & ≈{log}_{2}⁡(12) \\\\ p+1 & ≈3.585… \\\\ p & ≈2.585….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounding to the nearest integer, the estimated order of the method is $3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0f815379-499f-5491-abe7-bd5c1ed810d9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0f815379-499f-5491-abe7-bd5c1ed810d9" :answer-field/id #uuid "0f815379-499f-5491-abe7-bd5c1ed810d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-36011859-1afd-5d56-97d3-4bd6c1c53226" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7557f1ef-873e-57b4-b6d6-7690a0ee0f59" "answer-713dc69b-eedb-57c7-b566-963d0364f484" "answer-36011859-1afd-5d56-97d3-4bd6c1c53226" "answer-a804836a-c5a1-5048-8cb1-94c9973a4fc0" "answer-e6ae3264-3b35-5e19-bcf1-18378d20d72c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7557f1ef-873e-57b4-b6d6-7690a0ee0f59" :answer/id #uuid "7557f1ef-873e-57b4-b6d6-7690a0ee0f59" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-713dc69b-eedb-57c7-b566-963d0364f484" :answer/id #uuid "713dc69b-eedb-57c7-b566-963d0364f484" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36011859-1afd-5d56-97d3-4bd6c1c53226" :answer/id #uuid "36011859-1afd-5d56-97d3-4bd6c1c53226" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a804836a-c5a1-5048-8cb1-94c9973a4fc0" :answer/id #uuid "a804836a-c5a1-5048-8cb1-94c9973a4fc0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6ae3264-3b35-5e19-bcf1-18378d20d72c" :answer/id #uuid "e6ae3264-3b35-5e19-bcf1-18378d20d72c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221403 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-288ecca2-4a65-5e47-af70-06519e1d4ea1" "question-80296baf-83a9-58bd-9c2b-7e79ee3813aa"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-288ecca2-4a65-5e47-af70-06519e1d4ea1" :question/id #uuid "288ecca2-4a65-5e47-af70-06519e1d4ea1" :question/math-academy-id "q-334747" :question/problem "Which of the following global truncation error expressions represent a third-order numerical method?\n\nI. $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{4}+5(Δx)^{6}$\n\nII. $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{2}+3(Δx)^{3}$\n\nIII. $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+8(Δx)^{5}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $e(Δx)=2(Δx)^{4}+5(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $2(Δx)^{4}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=4(Δx)^{2}+3(Δx)^{3},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $4(Δx)^{2}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $2$ method.\n\n- For $e(Δx)=6(Δx)^{3}+8(Δx)^{5},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $6(Δx)^{3}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-01277570-5a55-57cd-b355-f92266d0d444"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-01277570-5a55-57cd-b355-f92266d0d444" :answer-field/id #uuid "01277570-5a55-57cd-b355-f92266d0d444" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c274000b-29cf-5c45-9c94-1b409cdb85c9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-82ff2bc6-4ad2-5485-9688-6c45d0274b48" "answer-585966c4-0f5a-5998-b590-6b01a0ed43b3" "answer-03959187-9418-5be5-ac79-7e4967d9b00d" "answer-c735b7b9-b2be-5342-be94-4a2ee43a5709" "answer-c274000b-29cf-5c45-9c94-1b409cdb85c9"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82ff2bc6-4ad2-5485-9688-6c45d0274b48" :answer/id #uuid "82ff2bc6-4ad2-5485-9688-6c45d0274b48" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-585966c4-0f5a-5998-b590-6b01a0ed43b3" :answer/id #uuid "585966c4-0f5a-5998-b590-6b01a0ed43b3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03959187-9418-5be5-ac79-7e4967d9b00d" :answer/id #uuid "03959187-9418-5be5-ac79-7e4967d9b00d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c735b7b9-b2be-5342-be94-4a2ee43a5709" :answer/id #uuid "c735b7b9-b2be-5342-be94-4a2ee43a5709" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c274000b-29cf-5c45-9c94-1b409cdb85c9" :answer/id #uuid "c274000b-29cf-5c45-9c94-1b409cdb85c9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-80296baf-83a9-58bd-9c2b-7e79ee3813aa" :question/id #uuid "80296baf-83a9-58bd-9c2b-7e79ee3813aa" :question/math-academy-id "q-334749" :question/problem "Which of the following local truncation error expressions represent a third-order numerical method?\n\nI. $τ(Δx)=3(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{6}$\n\nII. $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+8(Δx)^{7}$\n\nIII. $τ(Δx)=7(Δx)^{5}+5(Δx)^{6}$" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\nτ(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=3(Δx)^{4}+2(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $3(Δx)^{4}=3(Δx)^{3+1}.$ So, statement I corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=0.5(Δx)^{4}+8(Δx)^{7},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $0.5(Δx)^{4}=0.5(Δx)^{3+1}.$ So, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For $τ(Δx)=7(Δx)^{5}+5(Δx)^{6},$ the dominant term as $Δx\\to 0$ is $7(Δx)^{5}=7(Δx)^{4+1}.$ So, statement III corresponds to an order $4$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-27aac6e5-28a9-5d6f-bdc3-723dde44ae4d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-27aac6e5-28a9-5d6f-bdc3-723dde44ae4d" :answer-field/id #uuid "27aac6e5-28a9-5d6f-bdc3-723dde44ae4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5981bd8d-11b6-5c7a-91f1-9aca33191d67" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5981bd8d-11b6-5c7a-91f1-9aca33191d67" "answer-6d96eeb8-a870-5867-9c64-0e82e01c23ca" "answer-f6852f41-c0ac-53c8-b2e9-1ad22c6166af" "answer-b5c4497b-4049-5d0b-93c0-4a718cf248a3" "answer-424a9e92-70d2-56e9-9aed-a2fa1212a8eb"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5981bd8d-11b6-5c7a-91f1-9aca33191d67" :answer/id #uuid "5981bd8d-11b6-5c7a-91f1-9aca33191d67" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6d96eeb8-a870-5867-9c64-0e82e01c23ca" :answer/id #uuid "6d96eeb8-a870-5867-9c64-0e82e01c23ca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f6852f41-c0ac-53c8-b2e9-1ad22c6166af" :answer/id #uuid "f6852f41-c0ac-53c8-b2e9-1ad22c6166af" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5c4497b-4049-5d0b-93c0-4a718cf248a3" :answer/id #uuid "b5c4497b-4049-5d0b-93c0-4a718cf248a3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-424a9e92-70d2-56e9-9aed-a2fa1212a8eb" :answer/id #uuid "424a9e92-70d2-56e9-9aed-a2fa1212a8eb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221404 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-87976b3b-af1b-5fcb-8c01-533b581f6a90"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-87976b3b-af1b-5fcb-8c01-533b581f6a90" :question/id #uuid "87976b3b-af1b-5fcb-8c01-533b581f6a90" :question/math-academy-id "q-334826" :question/problem "A numerical method for approximating a solution to an initial value problem is known to be order $4.$  Suppose that using step size $Δx=0.25$ produces a global truncation error (GTE) of $e(0.25)≈0.3125.$  If we instead use a step size $Δx=0.10,$ what GTE should we expect?\n\n$e(0.10)≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A numerical method has order $p$ if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(Δx)=O\\,((Δx)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,Δx\\to 0.\n$$\n\nThe order describes how fast errors shrink as $Δx$ decreases.\n\nIn other words, a numerical method has order $p$ if, for sufficiently small step sizes, reducing the step size by a factor of $k$ reduces the GTE by a factor of approximately ${k}^{p}.$\n\nIn our case, the step size decreases from $0.25$ to $0.10.$ So, it reduces by a factor of\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{0.25}{0.10}=2.5.\n$$\n\nNow, since the numerical method is of order $4,$ the error is expected to decrease by a factor of\n\n$$\n{k}^{4}=(2.5)^{4}=39.0625.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using a step size of $Δx=0.10,$ the expected GTE is\n\n$$\ne(0.10)≈\\frac{0.3125}{39.0625}=0.008.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-290c0fd4-796b-5b98-9efb-32925a5e1121"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-290c0fd4-796b-5b98-9efb-32925a5e1121" :answer-field/id #uuid "290c0fd4-796b-5b98-9efb-32925a5e1121" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-27539fe2-4c37-500d-b2f1-bf9c12b924fa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-27539fe2-4c37-500d-b2f1-bf9c12b924fa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27539fe2-4c37-500d-b2f1-bf9c12b924fa" :answer/id #uuid "27539fe2-4c37-500d-b2f1-bf9c12b924fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.008" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-9cbd980a-784d-5a1b-93c3-51df50e05a4c" :activity/id #uuid "9cbd980a-784d-5a1b-93c3-51df50e05a4c" :activity/title "Order of Numerical Methods" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186051034 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-98e32d8b-896e-5b81-93c2-6516863ca64c" "step-10f1833a-b6bb-5d67-9812-3e3fe1c988ec" "step-b87614ba-eda8-5587-9a21-7a8fbab941af" "step-630e5c31-48d0-509b-b72b-9cf2dd9d0d36" "step-0a696d2d-bcf2-5d59-8cbe-c8b94fa6e1c7"] :activity/first-step "step-98e32d8b-896e-5b81-93c2-6516863ca64c" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-98e32d8b-896e-5b81-93c2-6516863ca64c" :step/id #uuid "98e32d8b-896e-5b81-93c2-6516863ca64c" :step/content "question-ebc091be-da12-5892-8a06-6dc1bcb20797" :step/next "step-10f1833a-b6bb-5d67-9812-3e3fe1c988ec" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-10f1833a-b6bb-5d67-9812-3e3fe1c988ec" :step/id #uuid "10f1833a-b6bb-5d67-9812-3e3fe1c988ec" :step/content "question-288ecca2-4a65-5e47-af70-06519e1d4ea1" :step/next "step-b87614ba-eda8-5587-9a21-7a8fbab941af" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b87614ba-eda8-5587-9a21-7a8fbab941af" :step/id #uuid "b87614ba-eda8-5587-9a21-7a8fbab941af" :step/content "question-80296baf-83a9-58bd-9c2b-7e79ee3813aa" :step/next "step-630e5c31-48d0-509b-b72b-9cf2dd9d0d36" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-630e5c31-48d0-509b-b72b-9cf2dd9d0d36" :step/id #uuid "630e5c31-48d0-509b-b72b-9cf2dd9d0d36" :step/content 17592186308683 :step/next "step-0a696d2d-bcf2-5d59-8cbe-c8b94fa6e1c7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-0a696d2d-bcf2-5d59-8cbe-c8b94fa6e1c7" :step/id #uuid "0a696d2d-bcf2-5d59-8cbe-c8b94fa6e1c7" :step/content "question-87976b3b-af1b-5fcb-8c01-533b581f6a90" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221456 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7658d4f4-3b1c-50cc-bb09-9642192fb8ae"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7658d4f4-3b1c-50cc-bb09-9642192fb8ae" :question/id #uuid "7658d4f4-3b1c-50cc-bb09-9642192fb8ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-342372" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that all the sine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)-(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))=1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n-\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)=1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=1 \\\\ {a}_{n}(-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}-1)=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-1 \\\\ {a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+1)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-58617cc3-c088-55aa-805f-3f4f74b88173" "answer-field-b53af9ba-07ea-5f68-ae7e-da94231dbd26"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-58617cc3-c088-55aa-805f-3f4f74b88173" :answer-field/id #uuid "58617cc3-c088-55aa-805f-3f4f74b88173" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-9009e0f7-5d50-5aa5-a503-ea03f3e99675" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9009e0f7-5d50-5aa5-a503-ea03f3e99675" "answer-62559ed5-80cf-5704-bf90-5935af3f40e7" "answer-914f8df8-af61-5f9e-a17f-75583f136c10" "answer-0da3558a-014b-530b-8b42-aea977dc4e14"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9009e0f7-5d50-5aa5-a503-ea03f3e99675" :answer/id #uuid "9009e0f7-5d50-5aa5-a503-ea03f3e99675" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-62559ed5-80cf-5704-bf90-5935af3f40e7" :answer/id #uuid "62559ed5-80cf-5704-bf90-5935af3f40e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-914f8df8-af61-5f9e-a17f-75583f136c10" :answer/id #uuid "914f8df8-af61-5f9e-a17f-75583f136c10" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0da3558a-014b-530b-8b42-aea977dc4e14" :answer/id #uuid "0da3558a-014b-530b-8b42-aea977dc4e14" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b53af9ba-07ea-5f68-ae7e-da94231dbd26" :answer-field/id #uuid "b53af9ba-07ea-5f68-ae7e-da94231dbd26" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-e9dd1e71-1b42-5658-b30c-6f8df61b1ce8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-20e270f2-1405-5a3a-bdf2-62a39dd31496" "answer-e84259ba-5de4-5201-9e89-fe85cb2ea4c0" "answer-283b9537-1996-5ae6-a737-f53e608ac594" "answer-e9dd1e71-1b42-5658-b30c-6f8df61b1ce8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20e270f2-1405-5a3a-bdf2-62a39dd31496" :answer/id #uuid "20e270f2-1405-5a3a-bdf2-62a39dd31496" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{\\pi }^{2}}{\\pi +{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e84259ba-5de4-5201-9e89-fe85cb2ea4c0" :answer/id #uuid "e84259ba-5de4-5201-9e89-fe85cb2ea4c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi (1+{n}^{2}\\pi )}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-283b9537-1996-5ae6-a737-f53e608ac594" :answer/id #uuid "283b9537-1996-5ae6-a737-f53e608ac594" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}(1-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9dd1e71-1b42-5658-b30c-6f8df61b1ce8" :answer/id #uuid "e9dd1e71-1b42-5658-b30c-6f8df61b1ce8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+1)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186221455 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b98de4ab-a079-5d39-b165-ba7998caa236"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-b98de4ab-a079-5d39-b165-ba7998caa236" :question/id #uuid "b98de4ab-a079-5d39-b165-ba7998caa236" :question/math-academy-id "q-343834" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}+5y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{{field-2}}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,+5(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n})cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{b}_{n})sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the cosine and sine coefficients, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2}=0 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n}=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{b}_{n}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=0.$ \n\nNext, from the third equation, \n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15})\\frac{n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n}=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${b}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{n\\pi }{15}\\cdot \\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4e6a7300-844d-5db4-8005-d85823b210da" "answer-field-a5322b59-ac05-57c5-bebd-3ff95532c124"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4e6a7300-844d-5db4-8005-d85823b210da" :answer-field/id #uuid "4e6a7300-844d-5db4-8005-d85823b210da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-b6d76ec8-e500-538c-8442-85b23edc88a4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4cb46238-0858-5e29-93f5-3b1abf6e8453" "answer-b6d76ec8-e500-538c-8442-85b23edc88a4" "answer-5bc4c62d-f986-5b1e-8753-4ec4676a3b8f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4cb46238-0858-5e29-93f5-3b1abf6e8453" :answer/id #uuid "4cb46238-0858-5e29-93f5-3b1abf6e8453" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6d76ec8-e500-538c-8442-85b23edc88a4" :answer/id #uuid "b6d76ec8-e500-538c-8442-85b23edc88a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5bc4c62d-f986-5b1e-8753-4ec4676a3b8f" :answer/id #uuid "5bc4c62d-f986-5b1e-8753-4ec4676a3b8f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{15(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a5322b59-ac05-57c5-bebd-3ff95532c124" :answer-field/id #uuid "a5322b59-ac05-57c5-bebd-3ff95532c124" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-9e473653-b9e6-5e7c-a302-54a5b439449d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dadc4114-78f9-5557-a713-9afe7cf481ca" "answer-9336c13e-c8cb-5577-898b-73fc979337d5" "answer-9e473653-b9e6-5e7c-a302-54a5b439449d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dadc4114-78f9-5557-a713-9afe7cf481ca" :answer/id #uuid "dadc4114-78f9-5557-a713-9afe7cf481ca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9336c13e-c8cb-5577-898b-73fc979337d5" :answer/id #uuid "9336c13e-c8cb-5577-898b-73fc979337d5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{45(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e473653-b9e6-5e7c-a302-54a5b439449d" :answer/id #uuid "9e473653-b9e6-5e7c-a302-54a5b439449d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186368337 :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})-6(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))=6+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n-6\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}-6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})=6+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and sine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=6 \\\\ {b}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}-6)=\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-1 \\\\ {b}_{n}=\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+54)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+54)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186368351 :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $6$-periodic function $(L=3)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}))+x(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}):$\n\n$$\n[-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A=0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\pi }{3}B=1\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=0 \\\\ B=\\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\frac{3x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186317451 :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}))+x(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}):$\n\n$$\n[-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\pi A=0 \\\\ \\pi B=6\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=0 \\\\ B=\\frac{6}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\frac{6x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "activity-5d2d2b52-5ff3-5595-845a-94f1c9e2f4a2" :activity/id #uuid "5d2d2b52-5ff3-5595-845a-94f1c9e2f4a2" :activity/title "Solving ODEs Using Fourier Series" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049288 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-38154cfd-c70d-53d6-a6c1-72899f8c40bb" "step-d60f6806-c6cb-503b-a2c8-fff99cda0786" "step-9099693d-f185-5899-981c-a69e22a33e27" "step-8730a614-36f9-5c60-80e1-eac2fd3a8305" "step-7402cf38-a638-59a3-b9f9-c0865bae0160"] :activity/first-step "step-38154cfd-c70d-53d6-a6c1-72899f8c40bb" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-38154cfd-c70d-53d6-a6c1-72899f8c40bb" :step/id #uuid "38154cfd-c70d-53d6-a6c1-72899f8c40bb" :step/content "question-7658d4f4-3b1c-50cc-bb09-9642192fb8ae" :step/next "step-d60f6806-c6cb-503b-a2c8-fff99cda0786" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d60f6806-c6cb-503b-a2c8-fff99cda0786" :step/id #uuid "d60f6806-c6cb-503b-a2c8-fff99cda0786" :step/content "question-b98de4ab-a079-5d39-b165-ba7998caa236" :step/next "step-9099693d-f185-5899-981c-a69e22a33e27" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9099693d-f185-5899-981c-a69e22a33e27" :step/id #uuid "9099693d-f185-5899-981c-a69e22a33e27" :step/content 17592186368337 :step/next "step-8730a614-36f9-5c60-80e1-eac2fd3a8305" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8730a614-36f9-5c60-80e1-eac2fd3a8305" :step/id #uuid "8730a614-36f9-5c60-80e1-eac2fd3a8305" :step/content 17592186368351 :step/next "step-7402cf38-a638-59a3-b9f9-c0865bae0160" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-7402cf38-a638-59a3-b9f9-c0865bae0160" :step/id #uuid "7402cf38-a638-59a3-b9f9-c0865bae0160" :step/content 17592186317451 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186159344 :answer-field/choices 17592186159345] [:db/retract 17592186159344 :answer-field/choices 17592186159346] [:db/retract 17592186159344 :answer-field/choices 17592186159347] [:db/retract 17592186159344 :answer-field/choices 17592186159348] [:db/retract 17592186159344 :answer-field/choices 17592186159349] [:db/retract 17592186159320 :answer-field/choices 17592186159321] [:db/retract 17592186159320 :answer-field/choices 17592186159322] [:db/retract 17592186159320 :answer-field/choices 17592186159323] [:db/retract 17592186159320 :answer-field/choices 17592186159324] [:db/retract 17592186159320 :answer-field/choices 17592186159325] [:db/retract 17592186159350 :answer-field/choices 17592186159351] [:db/retract 17592186159350 :answer-field/choices 17592186159352] [:db/retract 17592186159350 :answer-field/choices 17592186159353] [:db/retract 17592186159350 :answer-field/choices 17592186159354] [:db/retract 17592186159350 :answer-field/choices 17592186159355] [:db/retract 17592186159326 :answer-field/choices 17592186159328] [:db/retract 17592186159326 :answer-field/choices 17592186159329] [:db/retract 17592186159326 :answer-field/choices 17592186159330] [:db/retract 17592186159326 :answer-field/choices 17592186159331] [:db/retract 17592186159326 :answer-field/choices 17592186159327] [:db/retract 17592186159332 :answer-field/choices 17592186159333] [:db/retract 17592186159332 :answer-field/choices 17592186159334] [:db/retract 17592186159332 :answer-field/choices 17592186159335] [:db/retract 17592186159332 :answer-field/choices 17592186159336] [:db/retract 17592186159332 :answer-field/choices 17592186159337] {:db/id 17592186162094 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-02ad0f7a-4664-5f54-8474-68fa73e23e7a" "question-f38b0380-d78d-5094-b9f0-211071b581d6" "question-b27cf292-094d-512e-855d-8e418541cd09" "question-c1c74464-e8b7-5891-8564-303d0ba39aac"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161907 :question/problem "![](images/d1/d1fd70958028ae2c12be1df5808616421da9bb5758999237031a143e48d8e167.svg)\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample mean vector of $X.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the sample mean vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{m} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{x}_i,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{x}_n$ are the individual observations (columns of $X$).\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n![](images/f9/f97a4bc5d9323be573cc78cca322502d86d4c890d46b7116ffa1fac87e291672.svg)\n\nTherefore, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{4}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162096 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fd960ab2-8677-5176-9a5c-025cfe279786" "question-52c2be5d-881c-537a-9b60-cd461ebff8ca" "question-c1c1c9b0-94ce-5a76-9444-4ee707ea7768"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161909 :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9617e549830347cdd014ce812011ed679a95c576ea2920a93fdc4f09a824b55.svg)\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the mean-deviation form of $X.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the mean-deviation form of the observation matrix\n\n![](images/11/11679f410212ead297737ff3a6aaf5a8b2d8daba21abcb1199224857a14d3484.svg)\n\nis given by \n\n![](images/b7/b79eadc9e62263e563c3b385a3fe6dcb3eff75e9667cf9dbb7ccdb47c76ff12d.svg)\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{b}_i = \\mathbf{x}_i - \\mathbf{m}$ for $i = 1,2, \\ldots, n,$ and $\\mathbf{m}$ is sample mean vector.\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n![](images/3b/3bdd31917808dd86f0b247fba185f0f8f916412b9e86b1135ceb3a11f2c0ac1a.svg)\n\nAs a result, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n![](images/a7/a7d9c53005a25972fe250eafc83343434d213cb24ce883c1a6083966bcc39126.svg)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162095 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a2f76723-41c1-52ba-a0e5-e2eb590f9ddf" "question-67636a56-1e5b-5200-b7f3-14f05ab714df" "question-11c41377-dcf0-57a2-b4f3-049053a2f2ed"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161908 :question/problem "![](images/44/44fa46a2d63c5c59dcc95df4132bdfa56400989234b44d2a382eaa2e51d5713b.svg)\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample covariance matrix of the data." :question/worked-solution "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} B B^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nSo, we first need to compute the mean-deviation form of our observation matrix.\n\nThe sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n![](images/91/91d73b98e8b008382c1ef311fa262b1c2f0287d6ec64e3e86bd4734304ada251.svg)\n\nFinally, the sample covariance matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{3-1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 0 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & -5 \\\\ 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 50\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162357 :question/problem "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 8 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 10 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample mean vector of $X.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the sample mean vector is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ are the individual observations (columns of $X$).\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{4}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{4}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186159344 :answer-field/correct "answer-c7c38621-1b2b-5a28-b363-f6665e35d0c2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5e7cba67-e096-5b95-a14a-487e24567f2e" "answer-a8166e60-bcc2-5189-bbf7-7402566f0e78" "answer-c7c38621-1b2b-5a28-b363-f6665e35d0c2" "answer-9235d87a-9ca8-57a7-b50d-33d634911d1d" "answer-a28ae8b8-c35f-5e4a-a510-e53932d48486"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e7cba67-e096-5b95-a14a-487e24567f2e" :answer/id #uuid "5e7cba67-e096-5b95-a14a-487e24567f2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a8166e60-bcc2-5189-bbf7-7402566f0e78" :answer/id #uuid "a8166e60-bcc2-5189-bbf7-7402566f0e78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c7c38621-1b2b-5a28-b363-f6665e35d0c2" :answer/id #uuid "c7c38621-1b2b-5a28-b363-f6665e35d0c2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9235d87a-9ca8-57a7-b50d-33d634911d1d" :answer/id #uuid "9235d87a-9ca8-57a7-b50d-33d634911d1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a28ae8b8-c35f-5e4a-a510-e53932d48486" :a