here the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample mean vector of $X.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the sample mean vector is given by\n\n$$\nm=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ are the individual observations (columns of $X$).\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{4}=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{4}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -14\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a0e94315-388e-53ae-b553-50eed95f095c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a0e94315-388e-53ae-b553-50eed95f095c" :answer-field/id #uuid "a0e94315-388e-53ae-b553-50eed95f095c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-44f55327-e5e0-5b53-b975-ff63991e5282" :answer-field/choices ["answer-44f55327-e5e0-5b53-b975-ff63991e5282" "answer-d81ee82a-b0d6-5a90-b4c7-d66a7ac4a6e7" "answer-dd34b4f4-4513-5aa2-817b-3b15838c2384" "answer-99a0c948-2647-5d5d-89cd-05affd33ccd6" "answer-8efda6cd-141f-5350-a1f5-d71b313fd0e0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44f55327-e5e0-5b53-b975-ff63991e5282" :answer/id #uuid "44f55327-e5e0-5b53-b975-ff63991e5282" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d81ee82a-b0d6-5a90-b4c7-d66a7ac4a6e7" :answer/id #uuid "d81ee82a-b0d6-5a90-b4c7-d66a7ac4a6e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd34b4f4-4513-5aa2-817b-3b15838c2384" :answer/id #uuid "dd34b4f4-4513-5aa2-817b-3b15838c2384" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99a0c948-2647-5d5d-89cd-05affd33ccd6" :answer/id #uuid "99a0c948-2647-5d5d-89cd-05affd33ccd6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8efda6cd-141f-5350-a1f5-d71b313fd0e0" :answer/id #uuid "8efda6cd-141f-5350-a1f5-d71b313fd0e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 11\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162353 :question/problem "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the mean-deviation form of $X.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the mean-deviation form of the observation matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ {x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & ⋯ & {x}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis given by \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & ⋯ & {b}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere ${b}_{i}={x}_{i}-m$ for $i=1,2,…,n,$ and $m$ is sample mean vector.\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186159320 :answer-field/correct "answer-1992f01f-9787-5991-8f07-c542a44b2013" :answer-field/choices ["answer-29bb63a6-1e69-50a0-8eec-34dab87c0ea0" "answer-1992f01f-9787-5991-8f07-c542a44b2013" "answer-b52309b5-e519-5c28-a0f7-1a897f42f5e9" "answer-5dc5a73a-1e0e-58fc-855d-8bc21714321a" "answer-1c6d92ea-4f1e-56e7-878a-54a61789a2b4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-29bb63a6-1e69-50a0-8eec-34dab87c0ea0" :answer/id #uuid "29bb63a6-1e69-50a0-8eec-34dab87c0ea0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1992f01f-9787-5991-8f07-c542a44b2013" :answer/id #uuid "1992f01f-9787-5991-8f07-c542a44b2013" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b52309b5-e519-5c28-a0f7-1a897f42f5e9" :answer/id #uuid "b52309b5-e519-5c28-a0f7-1a897f42f5e9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5dc5a73a-1e0e-58fc-855d-8bc21714321a" :answer/id #uuid "5dc5a73a-1e0e-58fc-855d-8bc21714321a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c6d92ea-4f1e-56e7-878a-54a61789a2b4" :answer/id #uuid "1c6d92ea-4f1e-56e7-878a-54a61789a2b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fd960ab2-8677-5176-9a5c-025cfe279786" :question/id #uuid "fd960ab2-8677-5176-9a5c-025cfe279786" :question/math-academy-id "q-138613" :question/problem "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}15 & 23 & 16 \\\\ 14 & 20 & 20\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the mean-deviation form of $X.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the mean-deviation form of the observation matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ {x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & ⋯ & {x}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis given by \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & ⋯ & {b}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere ${b}_{i}={x}_{i}-m$ for $i=1,2,…,n,$ and $m$ is sample mean vector.\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}23 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}23 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}54 \\\\ 54\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 14\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}23 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 20\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}18 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9c612bcd-820c-5cdc-80d7-637ac5af81c6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9c612bcd-820c-5cdc-80d7-637ac5af81c6" :answer-field/id #uuid "9c612bcd-820c-5cdc-80d7-637ac5af81c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c81e532d-af9a-57e2-b63d-acd0722b13be" :answer-field/choices ["answer-86f71e12-ebbe-5491-b113-0c7da13d21c3" "answer-c81e532d-af9a-57e2-b63d-acd0722b13be" "answer-801331b6-d1a4-5072-8af1-2b93b2ebefc1" "answer-ed76a43b-1de2-586d-a13a-82bab00dc68c" "answer-fc97fbb2-6b7d-542c-ad48-76d5092ef42c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-86f71e12-ebbe-5491-b113-0c7da13d21c3" :answer/id #uuid "86f71e12-ebbe-5491-b113-0c7da13d21c3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c81e532d-af9a-57e2-b63d-acd0722b13be" :answer/id #uuid "c81e532d-af9a-57e2-b63d-acd0722b13be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-801331b6-d1a4-5072-8af1-2b93b2ebefc1" :answer/id #uuid "801331b6-d1a4-5072-8af1-2b93b2ebefc1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ed76a43b-1de2-586d-a13a-82bab00dc68c" :answer/id #uuid "ed76a43b-1de2-586d-a13a-82bab00dc68c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -1 & -2 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc97fbb2-6b7d-542c-ad48-76d5092ef42c" :answer/id #uuid "fc97fbb2-6b7d-542c-ad48-76d5092ef42c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -4 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162358 :question/problem "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 6 & 8 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the mean-deviation form of $X.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the mean-deviation form of the observation matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ {x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & ⋯ & {x}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis given by \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & ⋯ & {b}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere ${b}_{i}={x}_{i}-m$ for $i=1,2,…,n,$ and $m$ is sample mean vector.\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}21 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186159350 :answer-field/correct "answer-2b23785e-59c3-5c8b-a7cc-6a257ed32da9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4e971735-2555-5c2e-bdb4-f467bf99cf12" "answer-2b23785e-59c3-5c8b-a7cc-6a257ed32da9" "answer-631442a5-3529-5b28-b80d-ea82910b12bf" "answer-baee0313-3aa9-58bc-a651-9d7d64343bda" "answer-e2da0a70-ad5b-56e8-a291-1aa3b5630162"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e971735-2555-5c2e-bdb4-f467bf99cf12" :answer/id #uuid "4e971735-2555-5c2e-bdb4-f467bf99cf12" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b23785e-59c3-5c8b-a7cc-6a257ed32da9" :answer/id #uuid "2b23785e-59c3-5c8b-a7cc-6a257ed32da9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-631442a5-3529-5b28-b80d-ea82910b12bf" :answer/id #uuid "631442a5-3529-5b28-b80d-ea82910b12bf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-baee0313-3aa9-58bc-a651-9d7d64343bda" :answer/id #uuid "baee0313-3aa9-58bc-a651-9d7d64343bda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2da0a70-ad5b-56e8-a291-1aa3b5630162" :answer/id #uuid "e2da0a70-ad5b-56e8-a291-1aa3b5630162" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-52c2be5d-881c-537a-9b60-cd461ebff8ca" :question/id #uuid "52c2be5d-881c-537a-9b60-cd461ebff8ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-138609" :question/problem "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}11 & 13 & 15 \\\\ 10 & 7 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the mean-deviation form of $X.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the mean-deviation form of the observation matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ {x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & ⋯ & {x}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis given by \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & ⋯ & {b}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere ${b}_{i}={x}_{i}-m$ for $i=1,2,…,n,$ and $m$ is sample mean vector.\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}39 \\\\ 21\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}11 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}15 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2cd35d7c-3dff-56b6-ae44-55a4d6ea096b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2cd35d7c-3dff-56b6-ae44-55a4d6ea096b" :answer-field/id #uuid "2cd35d7c-3dff-56b6-ae44-55a4d6ea096b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c48aab5b-9b99-510a-a4db-cb8c07a9b5a4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c48aab5b-9b99-510a-a4db-cb8c07a9b5a4" "answer-89d6aeda-7054-5e32-a74a-0e2ca343b3d8" "answer-3d61031c-4423-50f9-9efb-1452f6b1952b" "answer-a389c464-59e1-5313-a31f-2255742146be" "answer-81c17eac-92ca-59f1-b6be-d25f5b5973a4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c48aab5b-9b99-510a-a4db-cb8c07a9b5a4" :answer/id #uuid "c48aab5b-9b99-510a-a4db-cb8c07a9b5a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89d6aeda-7054-5e32-a74a-0e2ca343b3d8" :answer/id #uuid "89d6aeda-7054-5e32-a74a-0e2ca343b3d8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3d61031c-4423-50f9-9efb-1452f6b1952b" :answer/id #uuid "3d61031c-4423-50f9-9efb-1452f6b1952b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a389c464-59e1-5313-a31f-2255742146be" :answer/id #uuid "a389c464-59e1-5313-a31f-2255742146be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81c17eac-92ca-59f1-b6be-d25f5b5973a4" :answer/id #uuid "81c17eac-92ca-59f1-b6be-d25f5b5973a4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c1c1c9b0-94ce-5a76-9444-4ee707ea7768" :question/id #uuid "c1c1c9b0-94ce-5a76-9444-4ee707ea7768" :question/math-academy-id "q-138607" :question/problem "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 5 & 6 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the mean-deviation form of $X.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the mean-deviation form of the observation matrix\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ {x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & ⋯ & {x}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis given by \n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}| & | &  & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & ⋯ & {b}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nwhere ${b}_{i}={x}_{i}-m$ for $i=1,2,…,n,$ and $m$ is sample mean vector.\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nAs a result, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4e40b0a9-1361-5dd4-aab2-b04b18adc043"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4e40b0a9-1361-5dd4-aab2-b04b18adc043" :answer-field/id #uuid "4e40b0a9-1361-5dd4-aab2-b04b18adc043" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-748097f9-a18e-56b6-8d3d-fbd3e7580604" :answer-field/choices ["answer-44e9ff8e-9005-5662-92b6-2423d657e0b2" "answer-4a84cf80-9ac6-550c-9cf0-410296323c5b" "answer-748097f9-a18e-56b6-8d3d-fbd3e7580604" "answer-fbe7ebd9-1482-50a1-accc-9e0e26fccee0" "answer-fbb61e4f-bff1-5ded-88cc-c64443477ea1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-44e9ff8e-9005-5662-92b6-2423d657e0b2" :answer/id #uuid "44e9ff8e-9005-5662-92b6-2423d657e0b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a84cf80-9ac6-550c-9cf0-410296323c5b" :answer/id #uuid "4a84cf80-9ac6-550c-9cf0-410296323c5b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-748097f9-a18e-56b6-8d3d-fbd3e7580604" :answer/id #uuid "748097f9-a18e-56b6-8d3d-fbd3e7580604" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbe7ebd9-1482-50a1-accc-9e0e26fccee0" :answer/id #uuid "fbe7ebd9-1482-50a1-accc-9e0e26fccee0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fbb61e4f-bff1-5ded-88cc-c64443477ea1" :answer/id #uuid "fbb61e4f-bff1-5ded-88cc-c64443477ea1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162354 :question/problem "$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $B$ in the mean-deviation form above, where the individual observations are given in columns. \nFind the sample covariance matrix of the data." :question/worked-solution "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{4-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 4 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 \\\\ -3 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}30 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 14\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186159326 :answer-field/correct "answer-ad38523d-e931-56dc-9284-35ef8fafb131" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b933cfc5-7683-5ac9-83e0-5bc009cb9e7b" "answer-3bcec93e-d7e4-55fc-819d-e39a0c56c36a" "answer-66dc2bfe-a8d0-52a2-bc36-bab980d55b1e" "answer-ad38523d-e931-56dc-9284-35ef8fafb131" "answer-b62c6af3-3c8a-56d5-b2ac-1774ba25ab19"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b933cfc5-7683-5ac9-83e0-5bc009cb9e7b" :answer/id #uuid "b933cfc5-7683-5ac9-83e0-5bc009cb9e7b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3bcec93e-d7e4-55fc-819d-e39a0c56c36a" :answer/id #uuid "3bcec93e-d7e4-55fc-819d-e39a0c56c36a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}10 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-66dc2bfe-a8d0-52a2-bc36-bab980d55b1e" :answer/id #uuid "66dc2bfe-a8d0-52a2-bc36-bab980d55b1e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}30 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 14\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad38523d-e931-56dc-9284-35ef8fafb131" :answer/id #uuid "ad38523d-e931-56dc-9284-35ef8fafb131" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}30 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 14\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b62c6af3-3c8a-56d5-b2ac-1774ba25ab19" :answer/id #uuid "b62c6af3-3c8a-56d5-b2ac-1774ba25ab19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}30 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a2f76723-41c1-52ba-a0e5-e2eb590f9ddf" :question/id #uuid "a2f76723-41c1-52ba-a0e5-e2eb590f9ddf" :question/math-academy-id "q-138641" :question/problem "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 13 & 10 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample covariance matrix of the data." :question/worked-solution "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nSo, we first need to compute the mean-deviation form of our observation matrix.\n\nThe sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}30 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample covariance matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{3-1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}18 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b4dedb56-1543-5c95-9a8b-146642e9b77e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b4dedb56-1543-5c95-9a8b-146642e9b77e" :answer-field/id #uuid "b4dedb56-1543-5c95-9a8b-146642e9b77e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4536e172-f637-5139-9267-23d685944188" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4536e172-f637-5139-9267-23d685944188" "answer-7dacd88f-1a54-51a7-88e9-6306954f67ed" "answer-58969516-8d79-5408-bf8d-22119bd156fd" "answer-faa4bf33-0b8b-57e1-99cc-e6c1e2c14038" "answer-3b7af902-b842-5b29-a228-d25f383a43ab"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4536e172-f637-5139-9267-23d685944188" :answer/id #uuid "4536e172-f637-5139-9267-23d685944188" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}18 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7dacd88f-1a54-51a7-88e9-6306954f67ed" :answer/id #uuid "7dacd88f-1a54-51a7-88e9-6306954f67ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}18 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-58969516-8d79-5408-bf8d-22119bd156fd" :answer/id #uuid "58969516-8d79-5408-bf8d-22119bd156fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}18 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-faa4bf33-0b8b-57e1-99cc-e6c1e2c14038" :answer/id #uuid "faa4bf33-0b8b-57e1-99cc-e6c1e2c14038" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}9 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3b7af902-b842-5b29-a228-d25f383a43ab" :answer/id #uuid "3b7af902-b842-5b29-a228-d25f383a43ab" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-67636a56-1e5b-5200-b7f3-14f05ab714df" :question/id #uuid "67636a56-1e5b-5200-b7f3-14f05ab714df" :question/math-academy-id "q-138640" :question/problem "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 8 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample covariance matrix of the data." :question/worked-solution "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nSo, we first need to compute the mean-deviation form of our observation matrix.\n\nThe sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample covariance matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{3-1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}8 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 18\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2ee2384a-f8ed-53d5-a42e-da2c4beee47c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2ee2384a-f8ed-53d5-a42e-da2c4beee47c" :answer-field/id #uuid "2ee2384a-f8ed-53d5-a42e-da2c4beee47c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8e900ee1-3dca-5910-b252-b7b9299af900" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4a37a48b-8c5c-5ce1-8b9b-361377f41e19" "answer-99853a00-52f1-50b5-8ddd-c3bbcc1f0f0f" "answer-d7ba38e6-e652-5b50-b3d7-ba917e7a89bb" "answer-8e900ee1-3dca-5910-b252-b7b9299af900" "answer-221e06ea-f68c-51ef-b279-1b5f659de9d1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a37a48b-8c5c-5ce1-8b9b-361377f41e19" :answer/id #uuid "4a37a48b-8c5c-5ce1-8b9b-361377f41e19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-99853a00-52f1-50b5-8ddd-c3bbcc1f0f0f" :answer/id #uuid "99853a00-52f1-50b5-8ddd-c3bbcc1f0f0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d7ba38e6-e652-5b50-b3d7-ba917e7a89bb" :answer/id #uuid "d7ba38e6-e652-5b50-b3d7-ba917e7a89bb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e900ee1-3dca-5910-b252-b7b9299af900" :answer/id #uuid "8e900ee1-3dca-5910-b252-b7b9299af900" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}8 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 18\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-221e06ea-f68c-51ef-b279-1b5f659de9d1" :answer/id #uuid "221e06ea-f68c-51ef-b279-1b5f659de9d1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 18\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-11c41377-dcf0-57a2-b4f3-049053a2f2ed" :question/id #uuid "11c41377-dcf0-57a2-b4f3-049053a2f2ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-138628" :question/problem "$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $B$ in the mean-deviation form above, where the individual observations are given in columns. \nFind the sample covariance matrix of the data." :question/worked-solution "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{4-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}30 & 15 \\\\ 15 & 26\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a45e3436-de97-54f9-a787-6f9a2b4c2e98"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a45e3436-de97-54f9-a787-6f9a2b4c2e98" :answer-field/id #uuid "a45e3436-de97-54f9-a787-6f9a2b4c2e98" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0fd9255b-f615-5d83-92d9-2bc78b91998e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f26c8ed4-8447-5837-bfea-0aa85c183599" "answer-0fd9255b-f615-5d83-92d9-2bc78b91998e" "answer-79759a38-a235-5771-92d6-228d11800351" "answer-90c22079-4b59-5228-be34-e7bd06a853b9" "answer-bf7bf3b9-12f7-5121-8c02-4ec81b6cd692"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f26c8ed4-8447-5837-bfea-0aa85c183599" :answer/id #uuid "f26c8ed4-8447-5837-bfea-0aa85c183599" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}11 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 34\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0fd9255b-f615-5d83-92d9-2bc78b91998e" :answer/id #uuid "0fd9255b-f615-5d83-92d9-2bc78b91998e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}30 & 15 \\\\ 15 & 26\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-79759a38-a235-5771-92d6-228d11800351" :answer/id #uuid "79759a38-a235-5771-92d6-228d11800351" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}37 & -87 \\\\ -87 & 14\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90c22079-4b59-5228-be34-e7bd06a853b9" :answer/id #uuid "90c22079-4b59-5228-be34-e7bd06a853b9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bf7bf3b9-12f7-5121-8c02-4ec81b6cd692" :answer/id #uuid "bf7bf3b9-12f7-5121-8c02-4ec81b6cd692" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}9 & 27 \\\\ 27 & 34\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162355 :question/problem "$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -10 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample covariance matrix of the data." :question/worked-solution "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nSo, we first need to compute the mean-deviation form of our observation matrix.\n\nThe sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample covariance matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{3-1}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -7 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ -7 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}74 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186159332 :answer-field/correct "answer-23427465-7faa-5ff0-b334-b66b99b1e3d6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-585eb8e8-05fa-5f15-975e-62db16d4ca73" "answer-5e602860-7b01-5bf5-9eef-058a5b7a3b43" "answer-a58cfba0-1623-53e1-b489-d1c2c499e821" "answer-f5bf6451-c2f0-513e-b209-8d9a57401167" "answer-23427465-7faa-5ff0-b334-b66b99b1e3d6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-585eb8e8-05fa-5f15-975e-62db16d4ca73" :answer/id #uuid "585eb8e8-05fa-5f15-975e-62db16d4ca73" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}34 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e602860-7b01-5bf5-9eef-058a5b7a3b43" :answer/id #uuid "5e602860-7b01-5bf5-9eef-058a5b7a3b43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}34 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 25\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a58cfba0-1623-53e1-b489-d1c2c499e821" :answer/id #uuid "a58cfba0-1623-53e1-b489-d1c2c499e821" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}74 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5bf6451-c2f0-513e-b209-8d9a57401167" :answer/id #uuid "f5bf6451-c2f0-513e-b209-8d9a57401167" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}74 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 12\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23427465-7faa-5ff0-b334-b66b99b1e3d6" :answer/id #uuid "23427465-7faa-5ff0-b334-b66b99b1e3d6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}74 & -9 \\\\ -9 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163310 :tutorial/content "The table below describes the number of classes taken per semester and the average final grade in a sample of four college students.\n\n| **Number of Courses** | $4$ | $3$ | $5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Average Grade** | $80$ | $85$ | $75$ | $72$ |\n\nWe can collect this data in a matrix so that each column reports the number of courses and the average grade of a single student:\n\n![](images/8a/8a489e90c10dbb65c2f5e005c32677696073cc63203116ed07d445a91d7f2d32.svg)\n\nThe matrix $X$ is called the **observation matrix.**\n\nSuppose we want to determine the mean number of courses and the mean average grade of this sample. The **sample mean vector,** denoted $\\mathbf m,$ is a vector containing these values. The sample mean vector is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{m} = \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\mathbf{x}_i,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_1, \\mathbf{x}_2, \\ldots, \\mathbf{x}_n$ are the individual observations (columns of $X$).\n\nFrom our matrix above, we have that  \n\n![](images/45/4549af29f9356865dc9f3edb67960da00a4007a71281778d4a273a1a6eb0dbf9.svg)\n\nTherefore, the sample mean vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{4}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}+{x}_{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 80\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 85\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 75\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 72\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ 312\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 78\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the average number of courses is $4,$ and the average final grade is $78.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163321 :tutorial/content "Earlier, we studied data that reported the number of courses selected and the average grades of a sample of four college students. The observation matrix for this data was\n\n![](images/9b/9bdfe848443d0014461cc51e2decfbed8dc151a72edb34825dd33ac964ed6b2f.svg)\n\nThe individual observations are\n\n![](images/3f/3f935ba5f9adea7f75a63642837a3b993286f9535d5bf1842ce3a2267413c6bc.svg)\n\nWe also found the sample mean vector \n\n![](images/26/261e1d746aafa95ac2f7a6d1ff36a741ff5ae37ba738f75b451a542baed1c5fc.svg)\n\nOne thing we can do with this data is to check how much each observation differs from the mean values. To do that, we compute the difference between each observation and the sample mean vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 80\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 78\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 85\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 78\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 75\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 78\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {b}_{4} & ={x}_{4}-m=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 72\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 78\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe collect these differences in a new matrix, as follows:\n\n![](images/83/831e698a632d40ab447c75a3b169e29c6ae8ab10c6600d838bd699a38e837931.svg)\n\nThe matrix $B$ is the **mean-deviation form** of $X.$ It's often easier to work with the mean-deviation form, especially if the original data contains large numbers.\n\nNotice that the mean of a mean-deviation form equals the zero vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{B} & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 7\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}([\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186163311 :tutorial/content "Let's consider again the mean-deviation matrix for the students' data we found previously:\n\n![](images/1f/1f5440b7ae08ffd19bdae816854c236afe3ca254d5bd23e26f15e34cd9c9feb2.svg)\n\nCalculating the product $B B^T,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 7 & -3 & -6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 7 \\\\ 1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -10 \\\\ -10 & 98\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's analyze what we've obtained:\n\n- The entry in the first row and first column (${\\color{red}2}$) is the sum of the squares of the mean deviations of the number of courses.\n\n- The entry in the second row and second column (${\\color{blue}98}$) is the sum of the squares of the mean deviations of the final grades.\n\n- The off-diagonal entries (${\\color{purple}-10}$) are the sum of the products of the mean deviations.\n\nSince there were $4$ observations in total, if we divide each entry by $4-1=3,$  we will get a matrix $C$ with the *sample variances* in the diagonal and the *sample covariance* in the off-diagonal positions:\n\n![](images/7d/7d0216b462e1bf7dc34d638a7a0de30fcf063ce39d1865b45f85b526ba7bd0a8.svg)\n\nThis matrix is called the **covariance matrix** of the data.\n\nIn general, the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} B B^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162232 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186186354 :answer-field/choices 17592186186355] [:db/retract 17592186186354 :answer-field/choices 17592186186356] [:db/retract 17592186186354 :answer-field/choices 17592186186357] [:db/retract 17592186186354 :answer-field/choices 17592186186358] [:db/retract 17592186186354 :answer-field/choices 17592186186359] {:db/id 17592186188260 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b7b25e27-3f05-5502-a400-9d84bc6b903e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188679 :question/problem "A nationwide study found that $38%$ of families own a pet. If a town randomly samples $200$ families, find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $80$ of them are pet owners. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=200,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.38.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=200(0.38)=76>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=200(1-0.38)=124>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=200$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.38,\\frac{0.38(1-0.38)}{200}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.38,0.0012).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If more than $80$ families in the sample own a pet, then \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}>\\frac{80}{200}=0.4.\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.4-0.38}{\\sqrt{0.0012}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>0.58) \\\\  & =1-P(Z<0.58) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.58).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186186354 :answer-field/correct "answer-b6c0529c-fbdc-5cfe-88eb-01281ed4b7cc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-873cc424-c085-5723-b881-20ef7fecbd77" "answer-e538117d-c9dc-58a0-8c48-4a9c7a9e89b3" "answer-b6c0529c-fbdc-5cfe-88eb-01281ed4b7cc" "answer-5d1f49e2-0e08-5966-bf0b-33d79bc46bdb" "answer-ee346f5b-62c5-52d8-9668-4cfe0bea6f78"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-873cc424-c085-5723-b881-20ef7fecbd77" :answer/id #uuid "873cc424-c085-5723-b881-20ef7fecbd77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-Φ(0.52)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e538117d-c9dc-58a0-8c48-4a9c7a9e89b3" :answer/id #uuid "e538117d-c9dc-58a0-8c48-4a9c7a9e89b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-Φ(0.53)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6c0529c-fbdc-5cfe-88eb-01281ed4b7cc" :answer/id #uuid "b6c0529c-fbdc-5cfe-88eb-01281ed4b7cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-Φ(0.58)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d1f49e2-0e08-5966-bf0b-33d79bc46bdb" :answer/id #uuid "5d1f49e2-0e08-5966-bf0b-33d79bc46bdb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-Φ(0.66)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ee346f5b-62c5-52d8-9668-4cfe0bea6f78" :answer/id #uuid "ee346f5b-62c5-52d8-9668-4cfe0bea6f78" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-Φ(0.69)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ba83e244-060a-5dec-904e-55d3bc3fab0a" "question-102d618f-6dbe-5b03-8427-52afbec9d15d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ba83e244-060a-5dec-904e-55d3bc3fab0a" :question/id #uuid "ba83e244-060a-5dec-904e-55d3bc3fab0a" :question/math-academy-id "q-146974" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=88$ from a population where $44%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $52%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=88,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.44.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=88(0.44)=38.72>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=88(1-0.44)=49.28>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=88$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.44,\\frac{0.44(1-0.44)}{88}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.44,0.0028).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.52).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.52) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.52-0.44}{\\sqrt{0.0028}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.51) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.51) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.51)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-99799375-0cad-5fd7-aafe-9d47d5e34722"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-99799375-0cad-5fd7-aafe-9d47d5e34722" :answer-field/id #uuid "99799375-0cad-5fd7-aafe-9d47d5e34722" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-05de7114-c6d2-5aa0-af39-01c4eb3c61be" :answer-field/choices ["answer-71142195-0b10-524d-8d89-cc3312aea868" "answer-e08790cd-6ba8-55b1-8612-1e73f81cd371" "answer-05de7114-c6d2-5aa0-af39-01c4eb3c61be" "answer-8cfe4040-0a28-5e2a-beae-ec384a40584e" "answer-c1d038d4-8c45-51ea-a946-61383d3a330e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-71142195-0b10-524d-8d89-cc3312aea868" :answer/id #uuid "71142195-0b10-524d-8d89-cc3312aea868" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-Φ(1.29)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e08790cd-6ba8-55b1-8612-1e73f81cd371" :answer/id #uuid "e08790cd-6ba8-55b1-8612-1e73f81cd371" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-Φ(1.34)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-05de7114-c6d2-5aa0-af39-01c4eb3c61be" :answer/id #uuid "05de7114-c6d2-5aa0-af39-01c4eb3c61be" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-Φ(1.51)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8cfe4040-0a28-5e2a-beae-ec384a40584e" :answer/id #uuid "8cfe4040-0a28-5e2a-beae-ec384a40584e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-Φ(1.78)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1d038d4-8c45-51ea-a946-61383d3a330e" :answer/id #uuid "c1d038d4-8c45-51ea-a946-61383d3a330e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1-Φ(1.93)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-102d618f-6dbe-5b03-8427-52afbec9d15d" :question/id #uuid "102d618f-6dbe-5b03-8427-52afbec9d15d" :question/math-academy-id "q-147211" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=96$ from a population where $72%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $70%$ but less than $80%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9591\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9599\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9608\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.8\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9671\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9678\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9686\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,1.9\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9738\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9744\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.9750\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.06\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.4\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3300\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3264\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3228\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.3\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3669\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3632\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3594\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4052\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4013\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.3974\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=96,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.72.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=96(0.72)=69.12>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=96(1-0.72)=26.88>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=96$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.72,\\frac{0.72(1-0.72)}{96}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.72,0.0021).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.7<\\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.8).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.7<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.8) & =P(\\frac{0.7-0.72}{\\sqrt{0.0021}}<Z\\le \\frac{0.8-0.72}{\\sqrt{0.0021}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-0.44<Z\\le 1.75) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.75)-P(Z\\le -0.44) \\\\  & =Φ(1.75)-Φ(-0.44)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.75) & =0.9599 \\\\ Φ(-0.44) & =0.3300.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.7<\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.8) & =Φ(1.75)-Φ(-0.44) \\\\  & =0.9599-0.3300 \\\\  & =0.6299.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-42b08898-7743-5ffd-b5a7-2f858adb5713"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-42b08898-7743-5ffd-b5a7-2f858adb5713" :answer-field/id #uuid "42b08898-7743-5ffd-b5a7-2f858adb5713" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-473980d8-f328-531a-b6ba-ec572af6ec3d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-473980d8-f328-531a-b6ba-ec572af6ec3d" "answer-1073d13f-794e-5f37-a58c-9a508bde8a49" "answer-04ae5602-2088-5fc5-8cd0-18b6e2828415" "answer-dcc9c121-5f5f-5fac-9860-8c845e4d48bd" "answer-27f8649f-e3ed-5db7-be91-6fb5aec1a2d4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-473980d8-f328-531a-b6ba-ec572af6ec3d" :answer/id #uuid "473980d8-f328-531a-b6ba-ec572af6ec3d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.6299" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1073d13f-794e-5f37-a58c-9a508bde8a49" :answer/id #uuid "1073d13f-794e-5f37-a58c-9a508bde8a49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.7582" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04ae5602-2088-5fc5-8cd0-18b6e2828415" :answer/id #uuid "04ae5602-2088-5fc5-8cd0-18b6e2828415" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.8117" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dcc9c121-5f5f-5fac-9860-8c845e4d48bd" :answer/id #uuid "dcc9c121-5f5f-5fac-9860-8c845e4d48bd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.9352" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27f8649f-e3ed-5db7-be91-6fb5aec1a2d4" :answer/id #uuid "27f8649f-e3ed-5db7-be91-6fb5aec1a2d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.9517" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b7b25e27-3f05-5502-a400-9d84bc6b903e" :question/id #uuid "b7b25e27-3f05-5502-a400-9d84bc6b903e" :question/math-academy-id "q-147386" :question/problem "A fitness center has $1600$ members, $480$ of which play tennis. If the center randomly samples $60$ members, find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $24$ members in the sample play tennis. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ | $\\,0.08\\,$ | $\\,0.09\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.9418\\,$ | $\\,0.9429\\,$ | $\\,0.9441\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.9525\\,$ | $\\,0.9535\\,$ | $\\,0.9545\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.9616\\,$ | $\\,0.9625\\,$ | $\\,0.9633\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=60,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=\\frac{480}{1600}=0.3.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=60(0.3)=18>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=60(1-0.3)=42>5,\n$$\n\nand the $n=60$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,\\frac{0.3(1-0.3)}{60}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.3,0.0035).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the probability we wish to find. If more than $24$ members in the sample play tennis, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & >\\frac{24}{60}=0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}>0.4) & =P(Z>\\frac{0.4-0.3}{\\sqrt{0.0035}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z>1.69) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.69) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.69)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(1.69)=0.9545.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.4\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}) & =1-Φ(1.69) \\\\  & =0.0455.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6f23f906-2914-521c-94e2-15d11518c774"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6f23f906-2914-521c-94e2-15d11518c774" :answer-field/id #uuid "6f23f906-2914-521c-94e2-15d11518c774" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-46ae5c47-ac36-5863-b19b-b928b3e368a9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7ff81e55-fb61-53d9-a0ed-ee5226f18b68" "answer-46ae5c47-ac36-5863-b19b-b928b3e368a9" "answer-4b8c7c87-239c-57d6-a57d-5db3fb45492c" "answer-b3c2ca1f-4a64-547f-8f50-f67ee8173783" "answer-5f83bd20-bc13-5e22-81e1-73b889147394"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ff81e55-fb61-53d9-a0ed-ee5226f18b68" :answer/id #uuid "7ff81e55-fb61-53d9-a0ed-ee5226f18b68" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.0234" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-46ae5c47-ac36-5863-b19b-b928b3e368a9" :answer/id #uuid "46ae5c47-ac36-5863-b19b-b928b3e368a9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.0455" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b8c7c87-239c-57d6-a57d-5db3fb45492c" :answer/id #uuid "4b8c7c87-239c-57d6-a57d-5db3fb45492c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.0645" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3c2ca1f-4a64-547f-8f50-f67ee8173783" :answer/id #uuid "b3c2ca1f-4a64-547f-8f50-f67ee8173783" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.0735" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5f83bd20-bc13-5e22-81e1-73b889147394" :answer/id #uuid "5f83bd20-bc13-5e22-81e1-73b889147394" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.1235" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-b137b7a5-4e68-530a-a72a-5c5c10f189b2" :activity/id #uuid "b137b7a5-4e68-530a-a72a-5c5c10f189b2" :activity/title "Point Estimates of Population Proportions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049862 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-93871095-a91d-5ed4-99a6-3d9809d62a85" "step-f271daa5-a674-53a1-aec8-1e8b65924285" "step-d725c15f-0c7f-5967-adb3-d56954c5934b" "step-30aaec14-16cc-59bc-9876-aeee76db4409"] :activity/first-step "step-93871095-a91d-5ed4-99a6-3d9809d62a85" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-93871095-a91d-5ed4-99a6-3d9809d62a85" :step/id #uuid "93871095-a91d-5ed4-99a6-3d9809d62a85" :step/content 17592186188679 :step/next "step-f271daa5-a674-53a1-aec8-1e8b65924285" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f271daa5-a674-53a1-aec8-1e8b65924285" :step/id #uuid "f271daa5-a674-53a1-aec8-1e8b65924285" :step/content "question-ba83e244-060a-5dec-904e-55d3bc3fab0a" :step/next "step-d725c15f-0c7f-5967-adb3-d56954c5934b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d725c15f-0c7f-5967-adb3-d56954c5934b" :step/id #uuid "d725c15f-0c7f-5967-adb3-d56954c5934b" :step/content "question-102d618f-6dbe-5b03-8427-52afbec9d15d" :step/next "step-30aaec14-16cc-59bc-9876-aeee76db4409" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-30aaec14-16cc-59bc-9876-aeee76db4409" :step/id #uuid "30aaec14-16cc-59bc-9876-aeee76db4409" :step/content "question-b7b25e27-3f05-5502-a400-9d84bc6b903e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133764 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-04de5946-03c2-52be-a91c-cc43fa9abf34"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186133763 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-890c5dfd-af10-56f3-b11c-f667a9950308" "question-6a15f1db-7af4-5426-9f16-816905676768"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-890c5dfd-af10-56f3-b11c-f667a9950308" :question/id #uuid "890c5dfd-af10-56f3-b11c-f667a9950308" :question/math-academy-id "q-43813" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$, equipped with the inner product\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(0)y(0)+x(-1)y(-1)+x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\nFind $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$, if\n\n$$\nx(t)=3+t-2{t}^{2},\\,y(t)=t-3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Computing the inner product according to the definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =x(0)y(0)+x(-1)y(-1)+x(2)y(2) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-3)+0\\cdot (-4)+(-3)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-9+0+3 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5ca6ff97-40bd-5746-ace1-2856a95eb89e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5ca6ff97-40bd-5746-ace1-2856a95eb89e" :answer-field/id #uuid "5ca6ff97-40bd-5746-ace1-2856a95eb89e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5261906e-cddb-5dc3-a97d-890d155fa5c7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-db85a92e-4361-5ea7-b58b-801f6714295d" "answer-22514fb0-f6fc-5f81-86f8-4d2e1207589f" "answer-36b65fb5-0fee-5676-afc4-645c1b803bc6" "answer-5261906e-cddb-5dc3-a97d-890d155fa5c7" "answer-e66a7f06-33be-5e84-baf2-c460003b0fda"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-db85a92e-4361-5ea7-b58b-801f6714295d" :answer/id #uuid "db85a92e-4361-5ea7-b58b-801f6714295d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-22514fb0-f6fc-5f81-86f8-4d2e1207589f" :answer/id #uuid "22514fb0-f6fc-5f81-86f8-4d2e1207589f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-36b65fb5-0fee-5676-afc4-645c1b803bc6" :answer/id #uuid "36b65fb5-0fee-5676-afc4-645c1b803bc6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5261906e-cddb-5dc3-a97d-890d155fa5c7" :answer/id #uuid "5261906e-cddb-5dc3-a97d-890d155fa5c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e66a7f06-33be-5e84-baf2-c460003b0fda" :answer/id #uuid "e66a7f06-33be-5e84-baf2-c460003b0fda" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6a15f1db-7af4-5426-9f16-816905676768" :question/id #uuid "6a15f1db-7af4-5426-9f16-816905676768" :question/math-academy-id "q-76442" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$, equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(0)y(0)+x(1)y(1)+x(2)y(2).\n$$\n\n Find $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$, if\n\n$$\nx(t)=t-1,\\,y(t)=t+2.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Computing the inner product according to the definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =x(0)y(0)+x(1)y(1)+x(2)y(2) \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 3+1\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-2+0+4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5ffd9e21-a21c-5ae7-a7c7-e2f36c42374e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5ffd9e21-a21c-5ae7-a7c7-e2f36c42374e" :answer-field/id #uuid "5ffd9e21-a21c-5ae7-a7c7-e2f36c42374e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-34ee9bea-a7ee-5f69-b60d-dab66dd19aa6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ffe9ee0f-144d-585c-9fe5-c21ac8f7efcd" "answer-34ee9bea-a7ee-5f69-b60d-dab66dd19aa6" "answer-b6ec2195-abc8-59fa-8eec-d947e7a76b5a" "answer-adf80400-9bf0-5756-9a8b-0e7694b4521f" "answer-33d9ec8b-a60d-5b77-ac5a-1965ca2efb92"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ffe9ee0f-144d-585c-9fe5-c21ac8f7efcd" :answer/id #uuid "ffe9ee0f-144d-585c-9fe5-c21ac8f7efcd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34ee9bea-a7ee-5f69-b60d-dab66dd19aa6" :answer/id #uuid "34ee9bea-a7ee-5f69-b60d-dab66dd19aa6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b6ec2195-abc8-59fa-8eec-d947e7a76b5a" :answer/id #uuid "b6ec2195-abc8-59fa-8eec-d947e7a76b5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-adf80400-9bf0-5756-9a8b-0e7694b4521f" :answer/id #uuid "adf80400-9bf0-5756-9a8b-0e7694b4521f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33d9ec8b-a60d-5b77-ac5a-1965ca2efb92" :answer/id #uuid "33d9ec8b-a60d-5b77-ac5a-1965ca2efb92" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-04de5946-03c2-52be-a91c-cc43fa9abf34" :question/id #uuid "04de5946-03c2-52be-a91c-cc43fa9abf34" :question/math-academy-id "q-76450" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,2\\pi ],$ the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,2\\pi ].$ Find $⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)⟩$ if the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the identity $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )).$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the identity $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-4t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6t)}{6}){|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\pi )}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(12\\pi )}{6}]-\\frac{1}{2}[-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{2}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0}{6}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a756d2bc-643b-5bbc-b0e1-1d9618cee002"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a756d2bc-643b-5bbc-b0e1-1d9618cee002" :answer-field/id #uuid "a756d2bc-643b-5bbc-b0e1-1d9618cee002" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-338b9ad0-884f-5a4e-8674-e7a8e1019500" :answer-field/choices ["answer-21b9f9b0-0c28-514b-949a-8339e5b0f60e" "answer-d1b7320f-6d58-56c5-83c3-fa0d4c1b8eca" "answer-338b9ad0-884f-5a4e-8674-e7a8e1019500" "answer-7c4a6c4d-c28d-5c73-8281-c978daf12c53" "answer-c61412bc-118a-5e93-b6c0-91732aceaf1d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21b9f9b0-0c28-514b-949a-8339e5b0f60e" :answer/id #uuid "21b9f9b0-0c28-514b-949a-8339e5b0f60e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1b7320f-6d58-56c5-83c3-fa0d4c1b8eca" :answer/id #uuid "d1b7320f-6d58-56c5-83c3-fa0d4c1b8eca" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-338b9ad0-884f-5a4e-8674-e7a8e1019500" :answer/id #uuid "338b9ad0-884f-5a4e-8674-e7a8e1019500" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7c4a6c4d-c28d-5c73-8281-c978daf12c53" :answer/id #uuid "7c4a6c4d-c28d-5c73-8281-c978daf12c53" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c61412bc-118a-5e93-b6c0-91732aceaf1d" :answer/id #uuid "c61412bc-118a-5e93-b6c0-91732aceaf1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-e9da63ab-03d4-5b28-8d6e-c384781de626" :activity/id #uuid "e9da63ab-03d4-5b28-8d6e-c384781de626" :activity/title "Inner Product Spaces" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048169 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-6ecbe002-c0f8-5d49-b600-f72c319b64d5" "step-512ebfd7-20c8-529c-8047-68f33115cdf7" "step-93e68841-8807-59e4-aeea-be3ba4a3970a" "step-63e80d05-6040-5f25-99b4-9ebfe7b7e44a" "step-3dbec995-d7b1-5d9d-9fb5-ba6abfa94b3e"] :activity/first-step "step-6ecbe002-c0f8-5d49-b600-f72c319b64d5" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-6ecbe002-c0f8-5d49-b600-f72c319b64d5" :step/id #uuid "6ecbe002-c0f8-5d49-b600-f72c319b64d5" :step/content 17592186329639 :step/next "step-512ebfd7-20c8-529c-8047-68f33115cdf7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-512ebfd7-20c8-529c-8047-68f33115cdf7" :step/id #uuid "512ebfd7-20c8-529c-8047-68f33115cdf7" :step/content 17592186134385 :step/next "step-93e68841-8807-59e4-aeea-be3ba4a3970a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-93e68841-8807-59e4-aeea-be3ba4a3970a" :step/id #uuid "93e68841-8807-59e4-aeea-be3ba4a3970a" :step/content "question-890c5dfd-af10-56f3-b11c-f667a9950308" :step/next "step-63e80d05-6040-5f25-99b4-9ebfe7b7e44a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-63e80d05-6040-5f25-99b4-9ebfe7b7e44a" :step/id #uuid "63e80d05-6040-5f25-99b4-9ebfe7b7e44a" :step/content "question-6a15f1db-7af4-5426-9f16-816905676768" :step/next "step-3dbec995-d7b1-5d9d-9fb5-ba6abfa94b3e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3dbec995-d7b1-5d9d-9fb5-ba6abfa94b3e" :step/id #uuid "3dbec995-d7b1-5d9d-9fb5-ba6abfa94b3e" :step/content "question-04de5946-03c2-52be-a91c-cc43fa9abf34" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157426 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a34954f0-edee-5e78-b8c6-4b95b31dcf9d" "question-b0787255-f508-561f-a5f7-88e7552739fb" "question-70144f61-d6d7-503d-8fc3-74d38b71f718" "question-12761a27-c492-58e4-90c1-fe69f195f607"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157044 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=x^2 + e^{-2x}$ in the interval $[0,1].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point $x_0=0.5.$ Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ and $f''(x_n)$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n,$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2}+{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =2x-2{e}^{-2x}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(2x-2{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =2+4{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{2{x}_{n}-2{e}^{-2{x}_{n}}}{2+4{e}^{-2{x}_{n}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{2{x}_{0}-2{e}^{-2{x}_{0}}}{2+4{e}^{-2{x}_{0}}} \\\\  & =0.5-\\frac{2(0.5)-2{e}^{-2(0.5)}}{2+4{e}^{-2(0.5)}} \\\\  & ≈0.424,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157427 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-69e00eda-cc65-5659-9307-298376017360" "question-0cd7384c-d991-5262-b3c3-37190574fa1c" "question-ce107c95-0185-5fbd-a5eb-aca5a16f428e" "question-f6e563d1-d5b4-5654-a9e3-927ced1c53e0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157045 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=x^3-6x^2+9x$ in the interval $[0,2].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point $x_0=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ and $f''(x_n)$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n,$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+9x)^{′}=3{x}^{2}-12x+9, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(3{x}^{2}-12x+9)^{′}=6x-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{3{x}_{n}^{2}-12{x}_{n}+9}{6{x}_{n}-12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{3{x}_{0}^{2}-12{x}_{0}+9}{6{x}_{0}-12} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{3(0)^{2}-12(0)+9}{6(0)-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{12} \\\\  & =0.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{3{x}_{1}^{2}-12{x}_{1}+9}{6{x}_{1}-12} \\\\  & =0.75-\\frac{3(0.75)^{2}-12(0.75)+9}{6(0.75)-12} \\\\  & =0.975\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x_1 = 0.75$ and $x_2 = 0.975.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157428 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-003098de-0500-522c-8022-2eac5e6b41e0" "question-afb3acd5-a2e1-5607-b3f9-fd70e360b05d" "question-c2a1716c-abcd-5c70-b5af-eb300c75e232" "question-34cbee90-4c9c-598a-b567-3653b89bdcd4" "question-de551d86-8bf0-5d70-b4cf-bdf9b46a682a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157046 :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)=x^4-3x+1$ has a critical point in the interval $[0,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from $x_0=1$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ and $f''(x_n)$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n,$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{4}-3x+1)^{′}=4{x}^{3}-3, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(4{x}^{3}-3)^{′}=12{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{4{x}_{n}^{3}-3}{12{x}_{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{x}_{n}^{3}-(4{x}_{n}^{3}-3)}{12{x}_{n}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8{x}_{n}^{3}+3}{12{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0 = 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{8{x}_{0}^{3}+3}{12{x}_{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8(1)^{3}+3}{12(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.916\\,666….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{8{x}_{1}^{3}+3}{12{x}_{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8(0.916\\,666…)^{3}+3}{12(0.916\\,666…)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.908\\,631…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{8{x}_{2}^{3}+3}{12{x}_{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8(0.908\\,631…)^{3}+3}{12(0.908\\,631…)^{2}} \\\\  & =0.908\\,560….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations $x_2 \\approx0.91$ and $x_3 \\approx 0.91$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x \\approx 0.91,$ rounded to two decimal places." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157716 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+{x}^{3}$  in the interval $[-2,0].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=-1.$ Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+{x}^{3})^{′} \\\\  & =2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})+3{x}^{2}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})+3{x}^{2})^{′} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})-4{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})+6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{2{x}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{n}^{2})+3{x}_{n}^{2}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{n}^{2})-4{x}_{n}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{n}^{2})+6{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=-1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{2{x}_{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{0}^{2})+3{x}_{0}^{2}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}_{0}^{2})-4{x}_{0}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}_{0}^{2})+6{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =-1-\\frac{2(-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2})+3(-1)^{2}}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡((-1)^{2})-4(-1)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡((-1)^{2})+6(-1)} \\\\  & =-1+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-3}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(1)-6} \\\\  & ≈-0.768,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186154807 :answer-field/correct "answer-a54572aa-b330-5a02-9337-defdf9128b26" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a54572aa-b330-5a02-9337-defdf9128b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a54572aa-b330-5a02-9337-defdf9128b26" :answer/id #uuid "a54572aa-b330-5a02-9337-defdf9128b26" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-0.768" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a34954f0-edee-5e78-b8c6-4b95b31dcf9d" :question/id #uuid "a34954f0-edee-5e78-b8c6-4b95b31dcf9d" :question/math-academy-id "q-256783" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)={x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}+x-5$ in the interval $[0,2].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=1.8.$ Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{4}-2{x}^{2}+x-5)^{′} \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}-4x+1, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(4{x}^{3}-4x+1)^{′} \\\\  & =12{x}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =4(3{x}^{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{4{x}_{n}^{3}-4{x}_{n}+1}{4(3{x}_{n}^{2}-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{4{x}_{0}^{3}-4{x}_{0}+1}{4(3{x}_{0}^{2}-1)} \\\\  & =1.8-\\frac{4(1.8)^{3}-4(1.8)+1}{4(3(1.8)^{2}-1)} \\\\  & ≈1.309,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6f567585-3cf1-5a7c-a653-205c6d1fd118"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6f567585-3cf1-5a7c-a653-205c6d1fd118" :answer-field/id #uuid "6f567585-3cf1-5a7c-a653-205c6d1fd118" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e4172698-0aa4-50ed-b63e-36359a77fc3c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e4172698-0aa4-50ed-b63e-36359a77fc3c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4172698-0aa4-50ed-b63e-36359a77fc3c" :answer/id #uuid "e4172698-0aa4-50ed-b63e-36359a77fc3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.309" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b0787255-f508-561f-a5f7-88e7552739fb" :question/id #uuid "b0787255-f508-561f-a5f7-88e7552739fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-256804" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)+\\frac{1}{x+1}$  in the interval $[0,1].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=0.8.$ Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+2)+\\frac{1}{x+1})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{x+2}-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(x+2)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}_{n}+2}-\\frac{1}{({x}_{n}+1)^{2}})}{(-\\frac{1}{({x}_{n}+2)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{({x}_{n}+1)^{3}})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{{x}_{0}+2}-\\frac{1}{({x}_{0}+1)^{2}})}{(-\\frac{1}{({x}_{0}+2)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{({x}_{0}+1)^{3}})} \\\\  & =0.8-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{0.8+2}-\\frac{1}{(0.8+1)^{2}})}{(-\\frac{1}{(0.8+2)^{2}}+\\frac{2}{(0.8+1)^{3}})} \\\\  & ≈0.575,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-86bdc7aa-e58d-58a2-934c-5e0eb671151b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-86bdc7aa-e58d-58a2-934c-5e0eb671151b" :answer-field/id #uuid "86bdc7aa-e58d-58a2-934c-5e0eb671151b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-15534455-4458-5ae5-8251-1f88374ee9af" :answer-field/choices ["answer-15534455-4458-5ae5-8251-1f88374ee9af"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15534455-4458-5ae5-8251-1f88374ee9af" :answer/id #uuid "15534455-4458-5ae5-8251-1f88374ee9af" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.575" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-70144f61-d6d7-503d-8fc3-74d38b71f718" :question/id #uuid "70144f61-d6d7-503d-8fc3-74d38b71f718" :question/math-academy-id "q-256788" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-x}$ in the interval $[1,3].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=1.5.$ Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed.\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(({x}^{2}-1){e}^{-x})^{′} \\\\  & =(-{x}^{2}+2x+1){e}^{-x}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(({x}^{2}-2x-1){e}^{-x})^{′} \\\\  & =(-2x+2-(-{x}^{2}+2x+1)){e}^{-x} \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-4x+1){e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{(-{x}_{n}^{2}+2{x}_{n}+1){e}^{-{x}_{n}}}{({x}_{n}^{2}-4{x}_{n}+1){e}^{-{x}_{n}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{(-{x}_{0}^{2}+2{x}_{0}+1){e}^{-{x}_{0}}}{({x}_{0}^{2}-4{x}_{0}+1){e}^{-{x}_{0}}} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{(-(1.5)^{2}+2(1.5)+1){e}^{-1.5}}{((1.5)^{2}-4(1.5)+1){e}^{-1.5}} \\\\  & ≈2.136,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a6a5ed95-b9e5-5fc7-bee1-be473b9bf6cc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a6a5ed95-b9e5-5fc7-bee1-be473b9bf6cc" :answer-field/id #uuid "a6a5ed95-b9e5-5fc7-bee1-be473b9bf6cc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-3f358074-e50b-5e75-b704-d17dc4cfeb47" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3f358074-e50b-5e75-b704-d17dc4cfeb47"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f358074-e50b-5e75-b704-d17dc4cfeb47" :answer/id #uuid "3f358074-e50b-5e75-b704-d17dc4cfeb47" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2.136" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-12761a27-c492-58e4-90c1-fe69f195f607" :question/id #uuid "12761a27-c492-58e4-90c1-fe69f195f607" :question/math-academy-id "q-256797" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=(1-x){e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ in the interval $[1,2].$ Calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=1.5.$ Round your final answer to $3$ decimal places if needed.\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =((1-x){e}^{-{x}^{2}})^{′} \\\\  & =(-1-2x(1-x)){e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(2{x}^{2}-2x-1){e}^{-{x}^{2}}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =((2{x}^{2}-2x-1){e}^{-{x}^{2}})^{′} \\\\  & =(4x-2-2x(2{x}^{2}-2x-1)){e}^{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(-4{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}+6x-2){e}^{-{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{(2{x}_{n}^{2}-2{x}_{n}-1){e}^{-{x}_{n}^{2}}}{(-4{x}_{n}^{3}+4{x}_{n}^{2}+6{x}_{n}-2){e}^{-{x}_{n}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{(2{x}_{0}^{2}-2{x}_{0}-1){e}^{-{x}_{0}^{2}}}{(-4{x}_{0}^{3}+4{x}_{0}^{2}+6{x}_{0}-2){e}^{-{x}_{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{(2(1.5)^{2}-2(1.5)-1){e}^{-(1.5)^{2}}}{(-4(1.5)^{3}+4(1.5)^{2}+6(1.5)-2){e}^{-(1.5)^{2}}} \\\\  & =1.3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4ccc8ea8-3dd2-55a3-84bd-b9f4206be0d2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4ccc8ea8-3dd2-55a3-84bd-b9f4206be0d2" :answer-field/id #uuid "4ccc8ea8-3dd2-55a3-84bd-b9f4206be0d2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-9cc72c80-e1b4-5fd5-8e0a-495b85f07692" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9cc72c80-e1b4-5fd5-8e0a-495b85f07692"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9cc72c80-e1b4-5fd5-8e0a-495b85f07692" :answer/id #uuid "9cc72c80-e1b4-5fd5-8e0a-495b85f07692" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157713 :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-3x+4$ in the interval $[0,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from the point ${x}_{0}=0.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-3x+4)^{′}=3{x}^{2}+2x-3, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(3{x}^{2}+2x-3)^{′}=6x+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{3{x}_{n}^{2}+2{x}_{n}-3}{6{x}_{n}+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{3{x}_{0}^{2}+2{x}_{0}-3}{6{x}_{0}+2} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{3(0)^{2}+2(0)-3}{6(0)+2} \\\\  & =1.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{3{x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}-3}{6{x}_{1}+2} \\\\  & =1.5-\\frac{3(1.5)^{2}+2(1.5)-3}{6(1.5)+2} \\\\  & ≈0.886,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186154804 :answer-field/correct "answer-261a1d30-1f56-5658-9c23-85d3ff7e2027" :answer-field/choices ["answer-261a1d30-1f56-5658-9c23-85d3ff7e2027"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-261a1d30-1f56-5658-9c23-85d3ff7e2027" :answer/id #uuid "261a1d30-1f56-5658-9c23-85d3ff7e2027" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.886" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-69e00eda-cc65-5659-9307-298376017360" :question/id #uuid "69e00eda-cc65-5659-9307-298376017360" :question/math-academy-id "q-256818" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-2x+3$ in the interval $[1,3].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from the point ${x}_{0}=2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-2x+3)^{′}=2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x-2, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+x-2)^{′}=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{2{x}_{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{n})+{x}_{n}-2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{n})+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{2{x}_{0}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{0})+{x}_{0}-2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{0})+3} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2(2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+2-2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+3} \\\\  & =1.367\\,897…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{2{x}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{1})+{x}_{1}-2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}_{1})+3} \\\\  & =(1.367\\,897…)-\\frac{2(1.367\\,897…)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.367\\,897…)+(1.367\\,897…)-2}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1.367\\,897…)+3} \\\\  & ≈1.306,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-db36be24-0165-5b4e-82e0-fd0653f9f4e9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-db36be24-0165-5b4e-82e0-fd0653f9f4e9" :answer-field/id #uuid "db36be24-0165-5b4e-82e0-fd0653f9f4e9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a7ae75fa-25c7-5f37-8341-8b12042dd7e3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a7ae75fa-25c7-5f37-8341-8b12042dd7e3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7ae75fa-25c7-5f37-8341-8b12042dd7e3" :answer/id #uuid "a7ae75fa-25c7-5f37-8341-8b12042dd7e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.306" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-0cd7384c-d991-5262-b3c3-37190574fa1c" :question/id #uuid "0cd7384c-d991-5262-b3c3-37190574fa1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-256860" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})-4x$ in the interval $[0.2,1].$ Calculate the first two iterations of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point ${x}_{0}=0.2.$ Round your answers to three decimal places if needed.\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2})-4x)^{′}=\\frac{2}{x}-4, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={(\\frac{2}{x}-4)}^{′}=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\frac{2}{{x}_{n}}-4}{-\\frac{2}{{x}_{n}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={x}_{n}+{x}_{n}(1-2{x}_{n}) \\\\  & =2{x}_{n}(1-{x}_{n}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =2{x}_{0}(1-{x}_{0}) \\\\  & =2(0.2)(1-0.2) \\\\  & =0.32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =2{x}_{1}(1-{x}_{1}) \\\\  & =2(0.32)(1-0.32) \\\\  & =0.4352\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${x}_{1}=0.32$ and ${x}_{2}≈0.435,$ rounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-73b9b8c1-e860-5fe5-b064-419ade05631e" "answer-field-35bab8a8-2a72-5135-8859-4f7ad2ce0f46"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-73b9b8c1-e860-5fe5-b064-419ade05631e" :answer-field/id #uuid "73b9b8c1-e860-5fe5-b064-419ade05631e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-f43fc1f0-c354-54a5-958a-4ec67d9ec620" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f43fc1f0-c354-54a5-958a-4ec67d9ec620"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f43fc1f0-c354-54a5-958a-4ec67d9ec620" :answer/id #uuid "f43fc1f0-c354-54a5-958a-4ec67d9ec620" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-35bab8a8-2a72-5135-8859-4f7ad2ce0f46" :answer-field/id #uuid "35bab8a8-2a72-5135-8859-4f7ad2ce0f46" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ebde269c-8777-57f6-a886-b4fd8fd5c9a3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ebde269c-8777-57f6-a886-b4fd8fd5c9a3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ebde269c-8777-57f6-a886-b4fd8fd5c9a3" :answer/id #uuid "ebde269c-8777-57f6-a886-b4fd8fd5c9a3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.435" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-ce107c95-0185-5fbd-a5eb-aca5a16f428e" :question/id #uuid "ce107c95-0185-5fbd-a5eb-aca5a16f428e" :question/math-academy-id "q-256826" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)={e}^{x}-2x-1$ in the interval $[0,2].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from the point ${x}_{0}=0.5.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{x}-2x-1)^{′}={e}^{x}-2, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{x}-2)^{′}={e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-2}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-2}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}} \\\\  & =0.5-\\frac{{e}^{0.5}-2}{{e}^{0.5}} \\\\  & =0.713\\,061…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-2}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}} \\\\  & =(0.713\\,061…)-\\frac{{e}^{0.713\\,061…}-2}{{e}^{0.713\\,061…}} \\\\  & ≈0.693,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3af7dcdc-a31a-5223-ae74-a699e51c827c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3af7dcdc-a31a-5223-ae74-a699e51c827c" :answer-field/id #uuid "3af7dcdc-a31a-5223-ae74-a699e51c827c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d167953d-d9cf-5074-8f9a-a13130028616" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d167953d-d9cf-5074-8f9a-a13130028616"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d167953d-d9cf-5074-8f9a-a13130028616" :answer/id #uuid "d167953d-d9cf-5074-8f9a-a13130028616" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.693" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f6e563d1-d5b4-5654-a9e3-927ced1c53e0" :question/id #uuid "f6e563d1-d5b4-5654-a9e3-927ced1c53e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-256825" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x)={x}^{5}-4{x}^{3}+x-2$ in the interval $[1,3].$ Calculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from the point ${x}_{0}=2.$ Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n${x}_{2}≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{5}-4{x}^{3}+x-2)^{′}=5{x}^{4}-12{x}^{2}+1, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(5{x}^{4}-12{x}^{2}+1)^{′}=20{x}^{3}-24x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{5{x}_{n}^{4}-12{x}_{n}^{2}+1}{20{x}_{n}^{3}-24{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{5{x}_{0}^{4}-12{x}_{0}^{2}+1}{20{x}_{0}^{3}-24{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =2-\\frac{5(2)^{4}-12(2)^{2}+1}{20(2)^{3}-24(2)} \\\\  & =1.705\\,357…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{5{x}_{1}^{4}-12{x}_{1}^{2}+1}{20{x}_{1}^{3}-24{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =(1.705\\,357…)-\\frac{5(1.705\\,357…)^{4}-12(1.705\\,357…)^{2}+1}{20(1.705\\,357…)^{3}-24(1.705\\,357…)} \\\\  & ≈1.561,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-28c09538-67a9-5afd-bd61-be1b48c03142"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-28c09538-67a9-5afd-bd61-be1b48c03142" :answer-field/id #uuid "28c09538-67a9-5afd-bd61-be1b48c03142" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-ec3950a0-c6fb-5eed-b58c-76259612f3f2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ec3950a0-c6fb-5eed-b58c-76259612f3f2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec3950a0-c6fb-5eed-b58c-76259612f3f2" :answer/id #uuid "ec3950a0-c6fb-5eed-b58c-76259612f3f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.561" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-003098de-0500-522c-8022-2eac5e6b41e0" :question/id #uuid "003098de-0500-522c-8022-2eac5e6b41e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-367495" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{5}-6{x}^{2}+2$ has a critical point in the interval $[1,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=1.65$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{5}-6{x}^{2}+2)^{′}=5{x}^{4}-12x, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(5{x}^{4}-12x)^{′}=20{x}^{3}-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{5{x}_{n}^{4}-12{x}_{n}}{20{x}_{n}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(20{x}_{n}^{3}-12)-(5{x}_{n}^{4}-12{x}_{n})}{20{x}_{n}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{15{x}_{n}^{4}}{20{x}_{n}^{3}-12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.65,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{15{x}_{0}^{4}}{20{x}_{0}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(1.65)^{4}}{20(1.65)^{3}-12} \\\\  & =1.428\\,269…\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{15{x}_{1}^{4}}{20{x}_{1}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(1.428\\,269…)^{4}}{20(1.428\\,269…)^{3}-12} \\\\  & =1.349\\,003…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{15{x}_{2}^{4}}{20{x}_{2}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(1.349\\,003…)^{4}}{20(1.349\\,003…)^{3}-12} \\\\  & =1.339\\,016…. \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{15{x}_{3}^{4}}{20{x}_{3}^{3}-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(1.339\\,016…)^{4}}{20(1.339\\,016…)^{3}-12} \\\\  & =1.338\\,865….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈1.34$ and ${x}_{4}≈1.34$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈1.34,$ rounded to two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e2ee9a01-2e0f-5959-b627-c127878594e1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e2ee9a01-2e0f-5959-b627-c127878594e1" :answer-field/id #uuid "e2ee9a01-2e0f-5959-b627-c127878594e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2bc7369f-4914-557f-a6eb-63a4c6c07a86" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ff4a2150-a8b4-5a78-9311-6c6806e53953" "answer-2bc7369f-4914-557f-a6eb-63a4c6c07a86" "answer-7a331c84-ec7a-5cf3-8a41-1b4c4fc250b0" "answer-b59f4ce9-57f0-5b4c-ab7f-71027d3c81f6" "answer-11c3f308-2cf6-56a0-b5d5-965d575f5ffc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff4a2150-a8b4-5a78-9311-6c6806e53953" :answer/id #uuid "ff4a2150-a8b4-5a78-9311-6c6806e53953" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈1.31" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2bc7369f-4914-557f-a6eb-63a4c6c07a86" :answer/id #uuid "2bc7369f-4914-557f-a6eb-63a4c6c07a86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈1.34" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7a331c84-ec7a-5cf3-8a41-1b4c4fc250b0" :answer/id #uuid "7a331c84-ec7a-5cf3-8a41-1b4c4fc250b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈1.38" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b59f4ce9-57f0-5b4c-ab7f-71027d3c81f6" :answer/id #uuid "b59f4ce9-57f0-5b4c-ab7f-71027d3c81f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈1.41" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11c3f308-2cf6-56a0-b5d5-965d575f5ffc" :answer/id #uuid "11c3f308-2cf6-56a0-b5d5-965d575f5ffc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈1.42" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-afb3acd5-a2e1-5607-b3f9-fd70e360b05d" :question/id #uuid "afb3acd5-a2e1-5607-b3f9-fd70e360b05d" :question/math-academy-id "q-367472" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{5}+3{x}^{2}-x$ has a critical point in the interval $[0,1],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=0.5$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{5}+3{x}^{2}-x)^{′}=5{x}^{4}+6x-1, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(5{x}^{4}+6x-1)^{′}=20{x}^{3}+6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{5{x}_{n}^{4}+6{x}_{n}-1}{20{x}_{n}^{3}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(20{x}_{n}^{3}+6)-(5{x}_{n}^{4}+6{x}_{n}-1)}{20{x}_{n}^{3}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{15{x}_{n}^{4}+1}{20{x}_{n}^{3}+6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{15{x}_{0}^{4}+1}{20{x}_{0}^{3}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(0.5)^{4}+1}{20(0.5)^{3}+6} \\\\  & =0.227\\,941….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{15{x}_{1}^{4}+1}{20{x}_{1}^{3}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(0.227\\,941…)^{4}+1}{20(0.227\\,941…)^{3}+6} \\\\  & =0.166\\,829…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{15{x}_{2}^{4}+1}{20{x}_{2}^{3}+6} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(0.166\\,829…)^{4}+1}{20(0.166\\,829…)^{3}+6} \\\\  & =0.166\\,033….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈0.17$ and ${x}_{3}≈0.17$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈0.17,$ rounded to two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9fcfedb6-87ce-541d-becd-78995b9b5d57"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9fcfedb6-87ce-541d-becd-78995b9b5d57" :answer-field/id #uuid "9fcfedb6-87ce-541d-becd-78995b9b5d57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6ff05878-7976-51a8-8d87-dc4f029dbe8e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1fb7d3c3-f99e-5007-8ec4-1cbb177d4b03" "answer-6ff05878-7976-51a8-8d87-dc4f029dbe8e" "answer-f5d96544-e96b-56d9-bceb-3eaf6a5f5840" "answer-5c10bba8-b37f-5bb1-804a-6d8a5cbebc65" "answer-5d68c59f-4f44-585f-ada6-6e1af634b4b0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1fb7d3c3-f99e-5007-8ec4-1cbb177d4b03" :answer/id #uuid "1fb7d3c3-f99e-5007-8ec4-1cbb177d4b03" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈0.13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ff05878-7976-51a8-8d87-dc4f029dbe8e" :answer/id #uuid "6ff05878-7976-51a8-8d87-dc4f029dbe8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈0.17" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5d96544-e96b-56d9-bceb-3eaf6a5f5840" :answer/id #uuid "f5d96544-e96b-56d9-bceb-3eaf6a5f5840" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈0.20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c10bba8-b37f-5bb1-804a-6d8a5cbebc65" :answer/id #uuid "5c10bba8-b37f-5bb1-804a-6d8a5cbebc65" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈0.23" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5d68c59f-4f44-585f-ada6-6e1af634b4b0" :answer/id #uuid "5d68c59f-4f44-585f-ada6-6e1af634b4b0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈0.27" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c2a1716c-abcd-5c70-b5af-eb300c75e232" :question/id #uuid "c2a1716c-abcd-5c70-b5af-eb300c75e232" :question/math-academy-id "q-367494" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-3{x}^{2}$ has a critical point in the interval $[0,0.5],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=0.5$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-3{x}^{2})^{′}=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-6x=2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-3x), \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)-6x)^{′}=-16\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)-6=-2(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{2(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{n})-3{x}_{n})}{-2(8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{n})+3)} \\\\  & ={x}_{n}+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{n})-3{x}_{n}}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{n})+3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{0})-3{x}_{0}}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{0})+3} \\\\  & =0.5+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot 0.5)-3\\cdot 0.5}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0.5)+3} \\\\  & =0.272\\,999….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{1})-3{x}_{1}}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{1})+3} \\\\  & =0.272\\,999…+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot 0.272\\,999…)-3\\cdot 0.272\\,999…}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0.272\\,999…)+3} \\\\  & =0.283\\,140…., \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={x}_{2}+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4{x}_{2})-3{x}_{2}}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4{x}_{2})+3} \\\\  & =0.283\\,140…+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4\\cdot 0.283\\,140…)-3\\cdot 0.283\\,140…}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot 0.283\\,140…)+3} \\\\  & =0.283\\,068….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈0.28$ and ${x}_{3}≈0.28$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈0.28,$ rounded to two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c871c6f1-367b-5fb5-b189-b4789899d2db"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c871c6f1-367b-5fb5-b189-b4789899d2db" :answer-field/id #uuid "c871c6f1-367b-5fb5-b189-b4789899d2db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-17340bb5-a402-5d9c-958d-b98608061d86" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bbf6a465-36cf-5de1-9103-6e46c194df81" "answer-5195019a-0822-52d4-9694-c18192ce17ff" "answer-17340bb5-a402-5d9c-958d-b98608061d86" "answer-d0e7e852-c97b-521c-b40c-6c022ad8b9c0" "answer-c3f14a15-a50b-506e-9ef6-fbe0f2fd1732"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bbf6a465-36cf-5de1-9103-6e46c194df81" :answer/id #uuid "bbf6a465-36cf-5de1-9103-6e46c194df81" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈0.24" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5195019a-0822-52d4-9694-c18192ce17ff" :answer/id #uuid "5195019a-0822-52d4-9694-c18192ce17ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈0.27" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-17340bb5-a402-5d9c-958d-b98608061d86" :answer/id #uuid "17340bb5-a402-5d9c-958d-b98608061d86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈0.28" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d0e7e852-c97b-521c-b40c-6c022ad8b9c0" :answer/id #uuid "d0e7e852-c97b-521c-b40c-6c022ad8b9c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈0.30" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3f14a15-a50b-506e-9ef6-fbe0f2fd1732" :answer/id #uuid "c3f14a15-a50b-506e-9ef6-fbe0f2fd1732" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈0.32" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-34cbee90-4c9c-598a-b567-3653b89bdcd4" :question/id #uuid "34cbee90-4c9c-598a-b567-3653b89bdcd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-367477" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)={e}^{x}-2{x}^{3}+4x$ has a critical point in the interval $[1,3],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=1.2$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({e}^{x}-2{x}^{3}+4x)^{′}={e}^{x}-6{x}^{2}+4, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =({e}^{x}-6{x}^{2}+4)^{′}={e}^{x}-12x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-6{x}_{n}^{2}+4}{{e}^{{x}_{n}}-12{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-6{x}_{0}^{2}+4}{{e}^{{x}_{0}}-12{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =1.2-\\frac{{e}^{1.2}-6(1.2)^{2}+4}{{e}^{1.2}-12(1.2)} \\\\  & =1.080\\,875….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-6{x}_{1}^{2}+4}{{e}^{{x}_{1}}-12{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =1.080\\,875…-\\frac{{e}^{1.080\\,875…}-6(1.080\\,875…)^{2}+4}{{e}^{1.080\\,875…}-12(1.080\\,875…)} \\\\  & =1.074\\,640…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & ={x}_{2}-\\frac{{e}^{{x}_{2}}-6{x}_{2}^{2}+4}{{e}^{{x}_{2}}-12{x}_{2}} \\\\  & =1.074\\,640…-\\frac{{e}^{1.074\\,640…}-6(1.074\\,640…)^{2}+4}{{e}^{1.074\\,640…}-12(1.074\\,640…)} \\\\  & =1.074\\,623….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{2}≈1.07$ and ${x}_{3}≈1.07$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈1.07,$ rounded to two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8eb7f079-1f2a-5896-b4e4-a2128ff0ff05"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8eb7f079-1f2a-5896-b4e4-a2128ff0ff05" :answer-field/id #uuid "8eb7f079-1f2a-5896-b4e4-a2128ff0ff05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5ec02751-b46d-59f3-80bf-35b87b580d86" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8e309a29-9c1e-5920-a47f-96c7863b8574" "answer-4b6a7928-3480-546a-a1db-608d12f49372" "answer-5ec02751-b46d-59f3-80bf-35b87b580d86" "answer-f201bc27-4c58-5510-a349-d7278294decc" "answer-28160efb-5c1f-5bdd-ab7b-b9c2c3928a3e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8e309a29-9c1e-5920-a47f-96c7863b8574" :answer/id #uuid "8e309a29-9c1e-5920-a47f-96c7863b8574" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈0.90" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b6a7928-3480-546a-a1db-608d12f49372" :answer/id #uuid "4b6a7928-3480-546a-a1db-608d12f49372" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈0.97" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5ec02751-b46d-59f3-80bf-35b87b580d86" :answer/id #uuid "5ec02751-b46d-59f3-80bf-35b87b580d86" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈1.07" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f201bc27-4c58-5510-a349-d7278294decc" :answer/id #uuid "f201bc27-4c58-5510-a349-d7278294decc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈1.09" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-28160efb-5c1f-5bdd-ab7b-b9c2c3928a3e" :answer/id #uuid "28160efb-5c1f-5bdd-ab7b-b9c2c3928a3e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈1.13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-de551d86-8bf0-5d70-b4cf-bdf9b46a682a" :question/id #uuid "de551d86-8bf0-5d70-b4cf-bdf9b46a682a" :question/math-academy-id "q-389801" :question/problem "Given that the function $f(x)={x}^{6}-2{x}^{5}+{x}^{2}$ has a critical point in the interval $[1,2],$ use the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting from ${x}_{0}=1.8$ to estimate the critical point. Round your final answer to two decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})},\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{′}({x}_{n})$ and ${f}^{″}({x}_{n})$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at ${x}_{n},$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{6}-2{x}^{5}+{x}^{2})^{′}=6{x}^{5}-10{x}^{4}+2x, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(6{x}^{5}-10{x}^{4}+2x)^{′}=30{x}^{4}-40{x}^{3}+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{6{x}_{n}^{5}-10{x}_{n}^{4}+2{x}_{n}}{30{x}_{n}^{4}-40{x}_{n}^{3}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}_{n}(30{x}_{n}^{4}-40{x}_{n}^{3}+2)-(6{x}_{n}^{5}-10{x}_{n}^{4}+2{x}_{n})}{30{x}_{n}^{4}-40{x}_{n}^{3}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{24{x}_{n}^{5}-30{x}_{n}^{4}}{30{x}_{n}^{4}-40{x}_{n}^{3}+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{12{x}_{n}^{5}-15{x}_{n}^{4}}{15{x}_{n}^{4}-20{x}_{n}^{3}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=1.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{12{x}_{0}^{5}-15{x}_{0}^{4}}{15{x}_{0}^{4}-20{x}_{0}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12(1.8)^{5}-15(1.8)^{4}}{15(1.8)^{4}-20(1.8)^{3}+1} \\\\  & =1.656\\,564….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\frac{12{x}_{1}^{5}-15{x}_{1}^{4}}{15{x}_{1}^{4}-20{x}_{1}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12(1.656\\,564…)^{5}-15(1.656\\,564…)^{4}}{15(1.656\\,564…)^{4}-20(1.656\\,564…)^{3}+1} \\\\  & =1.594\\,573…, \\\\ {x}_{3} & =\\frac{12{x}_{2}^{5}-15{x}_{2}^{4}}{15{x}_{2}^{4}-20{x}_{2}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12(1.594\\,573…)^{5}-15(1.594\\,573…)^{4}}{15(1.594\\,573…)^{4}-20(1.594\\,573…)^{3}+1} \\\\  & =1.582\\,950…, \\\\ {x}_{4} & =\\frac{12{x}_{3}^{5}-15{x}_{3}^{4}}{15{x}_{3}^{4}-20{x}_{3}^{3}+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{12(1.582\\,950…)^{5}-15(1.582\\,950…)^{4}}{15(1.582\\,950…)^{4}-20(1.582\\,950…)^{3}+1} \\\\  & =1.582\\,567….\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the last two approximations ${x}_{3}≈1.58$ and ${x}_{4}≈1.58$ are the same when rounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the critical point of the function is $x≈1.58,$ rounded to two decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-2e9f9e09-53f9-57a2-9d1e-832e556456f0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-2e9f9e09-53f9-57a2-9d1e-832e556456f0" :answer-field/id #uuid "2e9f9e09-53f9-57a2-9d1e-832e556456f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-286b768a-7fa2-547f-90e9-9f0f5184d086" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f5d0f5c4-36ba-5792-b2b1-be19c6791c05" "answer-90f997ea-c7bb-5fd8-bca1-7ccbea8af2d4" "answer-286b768a-7fa2-547f-90e9-9f0f5184d086" "answer-7f3c7483-fb38-539f-8efc-25641745f0b6" "answer-549fb8a0-48d5-5f7f-b520-c39d00f8ecef"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5d0f5c4-36ba-5792-b2b1-be19c6791c05" :answer/id #uuid "f5d0f5c4-36ba-5792-b2b1-be19c6791c05" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈1.52" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-90f997ea-c7bb-5fd8-bca1-7ccbea8af2d4" :answer/id #uuid "90f997ea-c7bb-5fd8-bca1-7ccbea8af2d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈1.55" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-286b768a-7fa2-547f-90e9-9f0f5184d086" :answer/id #uuid "286b768a-7fa2-547f-90e9-9f0f5184d086" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈1.58" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f3c7483-fb38-539f-8efc-25641745f0b6" :answer/id #uuid "7f3c7483-fb38-539f-8efc-25641745f0b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈1.60" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-549fb8a0-48d5-5f7f-b520-c39d00f8ecef" :answer/id #uuid "549fb8a0-48d5-5f7f-b520-c39d00f8ecef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x≈1.65" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158438 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we learned how to *find the roots* of a function using *Newton’s method*. That is, we solved equations of the form\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0,\n$$\n\nby starting with an initial guess and refining it step by step using the update rule\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f(x_n)}{f'(x_n)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we turn to a closely related problem of *finding a local minimum or maximum* of a function $f(x)$. Recall that these may occur at points where the derivative $f'(x)$ is equal to zero. In other words, we are looking for the roots of the function's derivative.\n\nEarlier, we approximated the function by a straight line near our current guess. Here, instead of using a linear approximation, we assume that the function $f(x)$ behaves like a *parabola* near our current guess. That’s a reasonable assumption if $f$ is smooth. \n\nTo make this idea precise, we use a second-order Taylor approximation, $F(x){:}$\n\n$$\nF(x) = f(x_n) + f'(x_n)(x - x_n) + \\dfrac{1}{2} f''(x_n)(x - x_n)^2\n$$\n\nTo find the minimum or maximum, we first need to take the derivative of this approximation, which is\n\n$$\nF'(x) = f'(x_n) + f''(x_n)(x - x_n).\n$$\n\nWe then need to set $F'(x) =0$:\n\n$$\nf'(x_n) + f''(x_n)(x - x_n) = 0\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$  as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}({x}_{n})(x-{x}_{n}) & =-{f}^{′}({x}_{n}) \\\\ x-{x}_{n} & =-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})} \\\\ x & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{f}^{′}({x}_{n})}{{f}^{″}({x}_{n})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, just like in root-finding, we start with an initial guess and improve it step by step. But this time, we apply Newton’s method to the derivative $f'(x)$ instead of the function $f(x)$ itself:\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)}\n$$\n\n**Note**: The second derivative $f''(x_n)$ must not be zero. If it is zero, or even very close to zero, the update step can become extremely large, leading us far away from the solution.\n\nJust like in root-finding, Newton’s method works best when the function is smooth, and our initial guess is already close to the minimum or maximum." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158457 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function \n\n$$\nf(x)= \\ln(x+1)+\\dfrac{2}{x+1}\n$$\n\nin the interval $[0,2].$ Let's calculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization starting at the point $x_0=0.8.$ \n\nThe Newton-Raphson process for optimization is\n\n$$\nx_{n+1} = x_n - \\dfrac{f'(x_n)}{f''(x_n)},\n$$\n\nwhere $f'(x_n)$ and $f''(x_n)$ are the first and second derivatives of $f(x)$ evaluated at $x_n,$ respectively.\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+\\frac{2}{x+1})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+1}-\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-1}{(x+1)^{2}}, \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{x+1}-\\frac{2}{(x+1)^{2}})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{(x+1)^{2}}+\\frac{4}{(x+1)^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-x}{(x+1)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{n+1} & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{\\frac{{x}_{n}-1}{({x}_{n}+1)^{2}}}{\\frac{3-{x}_{n}}{({x}_{n}+1)^{3}}} \\\\  & ={x}_{n}-\\frac{{x}_{n}^{2}-1}{3-{x}_{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nStarting with $x_0=0.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-\\frac{{x}_{0}^{2}-1}{3-{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =0.8-\\frac{(0.8)^{2}-1}{3-0.8} \\\\  & ≈0.964,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186157519 :activity/expected-seconds 1500.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158355 :step/math-academy-id 688822 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158356 :step/math-academy-id 688823 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158357 :step/math-academy-id 688824 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158358 :step/math-academy-id 688827 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186158359 :step/math-academy-id 688830 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124651 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c44a3fc0-0b84-5773-98c9-aa672df4ee82"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c44a3fc0-0b84-5773-98c9-aa672df4ee82" :question/id #uuid "c44a3fc0-0b84-5773-98c9-aa672df4ee82" :question/math-academy-id "q-164407" :question/problem "Identify the following surface: \n\n$$\nz=1+(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{9}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the equation of an elliptic paraboloid centered at $O$ is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}},\n$$\n\nand an elliptic paraboloid centered at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ is given by\n\n$$\nz-{z}_{0}=\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}},\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is an elliptic paraboloid with the following parameters:\n\n$$\n({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})=(1,2,1),\\,a=1,\\,b=3.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f79d7671-2b5b-530a-ae16-c53529846a84"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f79d7671-2b5b-530a-ae16-c53529846a84" :answer-field/id #uuid "f79d7671-2b5b-530a-ae16-c53529846a84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-56ef26e6-92c5-5ea5-b133-22f7b64a33e2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-68d13e64-6b65-563f-999b-e9e60dd96c22" "answer-de32f43e-77ab-55e0-8416-a6838959b401" "answer-dcb21376-02e4-57eb-9eae-d7276dc0e580" "answer-95e635ba-07ae-51b7-9591-773690c09952" "answer-56ef26e6-92c5-5ea5-b133-22f7b64a33e2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-68d13e64-6b65-563f-999b-e9e60dd96c22" :answer/id #uuid "68d13e64-6b65-563f-999b-e9e60dd96c22" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A hyperbolic paraboloid" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-de32f43e-77ab-55e0-8416-a6838959b401" :answer/id #uuid "de32f43e-77ab-55e0-8416-a6838959b401" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A hyperboloid of one sheet" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dcb21376-02e4-57eb-9eae-d7276dc0e580" :answer/id #uuid "dcb21376-02e4-57eb-9eae-d7276dc0e580" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A hyperboloid of two sheets" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95e635ba-07ae-51b7-9591-773690c09952" :answer/id #uuid "95e635ba-07ae-51b7-9591-773690c09952" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "An elliptic cone" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-56ef26e6-92c5-5ea5-b133-22f7b64a33e2" :answer/id #uuid "56ef26e6-92c5-5ea5-b133-22f7b64a33e2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "An elliptic paraboloid" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-f9ebf790-6022-5a93-b998-ec0444d26510" :activity/id #uuid "f9ebf790-6022-5a93-b998-ec0444d26510" :activity/title "Identifying Quadric Surfaces" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050005 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-284d8b01-2f97-5ebe-9ab6-41f17bf5767e" "step-271e1473-cf3a-502d-8643-f9fbecb2a725" "step-9a570ba4-3fc5-59c3-8513-ce917bdc7167" "step-55aa02b0-039f-58cd-ae44-5767ea8c3cbb" "step-f6db678b-c89a-5ef1-8eab-c2e37f3bbe22"] :activity/first-step "step-284d8b01-2f97-5ebe-9ab6-41f17bf5767e" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-284d8b01-2f97-5ebe-9ab6-41f17bf5767e" :step/id #uuid "284d8b01-2f97-5ebe-9ab6-41f17bf5767e" :step/content 17592186323755 :step/next "step-271e1473-cf3a-502d-8643-f9fbecb2a725" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-271e1473-cf3a-502d-8643-f9fbecb2a725" :step/id #uuid "271e1473-cf3a-502d-8643-f9fbecb2a725" :step/content 17592186124831 :step/next "step-9a570ba4-3fc5-59c3-8513-ce917bdc7167" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9a570ba4-3fc5-59c3-8513-ce917bdc7167" :step/id #uuid "9a570ba4-3fc5-59c3-8513-ce917bdc7167" :step/content "question-c44a3fc0-0b84-5773-98c9-aa672df4ee82" :step/next "step-55aa02b0-039f-58cd-ae44-5767ea8c3cbb" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-55aa02b0-039f-58cd-ae44-5767ea8c3cbb" :step/id #uuid "55aa02b0-039f-58cd-ae44-5767ea8c3cbb" :step/content 17592186284500 :step/next "step-f6db678b-c89a-5ef1-8eab-c2e37f3bbe22" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-f6db678b-c89a-5ef1-8eab-c2e37f3bbe22" :step/id #uuid "f6db678b-c89a-5ef1-8eab-c2e37f3bbe22" :step/content 17592186381348 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166931 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e1726d67-859b-50b6-a736-a3033ae62d0e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e1726d67-859b-50b6-a736-a3033ae62d0e" :question/id #uuid "e1726d67-859b-50b6-a736-a3033ae62d0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-331948" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform $L$ to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a system with variables ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$ In terms of the parameter $s,$ the solution of the new system is\n\n${X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{2(2s-9)}{(s-1)(s-4)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=$ {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the Laplace transforms of both sides of each equation in our system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}L\\{{x}_{1}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{2{x}_{1}(t)-2{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ L\\{{x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\}=L\\{-{x}_{1}(t)+3{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nUsing the derivative property of the Laplace transform, we obtain the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}sL\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-{x}_{1}(0)=2L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}-2L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\} \\\\ sL\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}-{x}_{2}(0)=-L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}+3L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich can be simplified to the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}s{X}_{1}(s)-4=2{X}_{1}(s)-2{X}_{2}(s) \\\\ s{X}_{2}(s)-3=-{X}_{1}(s)+3{X}_{2}(s)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}$ and ${X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}.$\n\nRewriting the system in standard form, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(s-2){X}_{1}(s)+2{X}_{2}(s)=4 \\\\ {X}_{1}(s)+(s-3){X}_{2}(s)=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, from the second equation, we have \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=3-(s-3){X}_{2}(s).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(s-2)[3-(s-3){X}_{2}(s)]+2{X}_{2}(s) & =4 \\\\ 3(s-2)-[(s-2)(s-3)-2]{X}_{2}(s) & =4 \\\\ -({s}^{2}-5s+4){X}_{2}(s) & =4-3(s-2) \\\\ -(s-1)(s-4){X}_{2}(s) & =-3s+10 \\\\ {X}_{2}(s) & =\\frac{3s-10}{(s-1)(s-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${X}_{1}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{X}_{1}(s) & =3-(s-3){X}_{2}(s) \\\\  & =3-(s-3)\\cdot \\frac{3s-10}{(s-1)(s-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3(s-1)(s-4)-(s-3)(3s-10)}{(s-1)(s-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4s-18}{(s-1)(s-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(2s-9)}{(s-1)(s-4)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=\\frac{2(2s-9)}{(s-1)(s-4)},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=\\frac{3s-10}{(s-1)(s-4)}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-695e84fb-9997-5022-b11a-2cd4e22db019"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-695e84fb-9997-5022-b11a-2cd4e22db019" :answer-field/id #uuid "695e84fb-9997-5022-b11a-2cd4e22db019" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-df62ff71-4774-53f9-b7fc-ad0d27a68300" :answer-field/choices ["answer-df62ff71-4774-53f9-b7fc-ad0d27a68300"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df62ff71-4774-53f9-b7fc-ad0d27a68300" :answer/id #uuid "df62ff71-4774-53f9-b7fc-ad0d27a68300" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3s-10}{(s-1)(s-4)}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186166932 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-fafb126e-2901-5759-82dc-9622ce8bea50" "question-4d0a8858-fa20-5f8a-859e-5ff864dcdd44"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-fafb126e-2901-5759-82dc-9622ce8bea50" :question/id #uuid "fafb126e-2901-5759-82dc-9622ce8bea50" :question/math-academy-id "q-332299" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)={x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}=\\frac{3}{s-1},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{2}{s-4}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,{e}^{4t}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "To find ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to ${X}_{1}(s)$ and ${X}_{2}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{1}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{3}{s-1}\\} \\\\  & =3\\,{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-1}\\} \\\\  & =3{e}^{1\\cdot t} \\\\  & =3{e}^{t} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{2}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-1}-\\frac{2}{s-4}\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-1}\\}-2\\,{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-4}\\} \\\\  & ={e}^{1\\cdot t}-2{e}^{4\\cdot t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}-2{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3{e}^{t} \\\\ {e}^{t}-2{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0c8f6e0a-6fd1-5b8e-b6eb-9b1fb0b0fea0" "answer-field-cb445a54-016a-5ce1-8c7f-2dbdca7b13f9" "answer-field-54b51ff1-8e69-5b64-be8f-00264cd94dc6" "answer-field-3a030071-4ec4-5adf-89a3-4d7bacf1c66e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0c8f6e0a-6fd1-5b8e-b6eb-9b1fb0b0fea0" :answer-field/id #uuid "0c8f6e0a-6fd1-5b8e-b6eb-9b1fb0b0fea0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-4d847229-96cd-5ffd-a231-2ad72cf800f1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4d847229-96cd-5ffd-a231-2ad72cf800f1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d847229-96cd-5ffd-a231-2ad72cf800f1" :answer/id #uuid "4d847229-96cd-5ffd-a231-2ad72cf800f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-cb445a54-016a-5ce1-8c7f-2dbdca7b13f9" :answer-field/id #uuid "cb445a54-016a-5ce1-8c7f-2dbdca7b13f9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-87aed3d1-a1af-50a1-be6b-24983dbaafd0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-87aed3d1-a1af-50a1-be6b-24983dbaafd0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-87aed3d1-a1af-50a1-be6b-24983dbaafd0" :answer/id #uuid "87aed3d1-a1af-50a1-be6b-24983dbaafd0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-54b51ff1-8e69-5b64-be8f-00264cd94dc6" :answer-field/id #uuid "54b51ff1-8e69-5b64-be8f-00264cd94dc6" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-a141d64b-becb-5e33-93ee-ab35b9b1dd5b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a141d64b-becb-5e33-93ee-ab35b9b1dd5b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a141d64b-becb-5e33-93ee-ab35b9b1dd5b" :answer/id #uuid "a141d64b-becb-5e33-93ee-ab35b9b1dd5b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3a030071-4ec4-5adf-89a3-4d7bacf1c66e" :answer-field/id #uuid "3a030071-4ec4-5adf-89a3-4d7bacf1c66e" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-792912c3-c77e-5ff3-a071-8f72576c2ac8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-792912c3-c77e-5ff3-a071-8f72576c2ac8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-792912c3-c77e-5ff3-a071-8f72576c2ac8" :answer/id #uuid "792912c3-c77e-5ff3-a071-8f72576c2ac8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4d0a8858-fa20-5f8a-859e-5ff864dcdd44" :question/id #uuid "4d0a8858-fa20-5f8a-859e-5ff864dcdd44" :question/math-academy-id "q-332402" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)-4{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+2{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}=\\frac{6}{s}-\\frac{2}{s-4},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}=\\frac{3}{s}+\\frac{1}{s-4}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,{e}^{4t}$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "To find ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to ${X}_{1}(s)$ and ${X}_{2}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{1}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{6}{s}-\\frac{2}{s-4}\\} \\\\  & =6{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\}-2{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-4}\\} \\\\  & =6\\cdot 1-2{e}^{4\\cdot t} \\\\  & =6-2{e}^{4t} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{2}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{3}{s}+\\frac{1}{s-4}\\} \\\\  & =3{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\}+{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-4}\\} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1+{e}^{4\\cdot t} \\\\  & =3+{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6-2{e}^{4t} \\\\ 3+{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9a05b26b-fbd9-504f-b92b-f99d4bfac354" "answer-field-4dde5664-1551-5800-8729-df181a92b19e" "answer-field-65909c4d-3bfc-57f3-90ff-59677c9e4afb" "answer-field-a2187910-c471-5e05-bbb3-c1123deb8a70"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9a05b26b-fbd9-504f-b92b-f99d4bfac354" :answer-field/id #uuid "9a05b26b-fbd9-504f-b92b-f99d4bfac354" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-297fb137-5002-574b-b9ad-9272c0657eb2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-297fb137-5002-574b-b9ad-9272c0657eb2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-297fb137-5002-574b-b9ad-9272c0657eb2" :answer/id #uuid "297fb137-5002-574b-b9ad-9272c0657eb2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4dde5664-1551-5800-8729-df181a92b19e" :answer-field/id #uuid "4dde5664-1551-5800-8729-df181a92b19e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.